        <p>' [ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] afford an hotel; can you recommend Me any- where ?" A CHANGE OF RELATIONS "Well, miss," replied' the porter, slowly, • "there's a Mrs. Diver, a friend of my wife's, lets apartments, and I think she's got a spare room, but I'm afraid it ain't quite good enough By CHARLES D. LESLIE, for a young lydy like you. Author of "The Gold Trap," "The Hamilton "I haven't much money; as long as it's clean Honeymoon," "The Best Man," "Juliet of and respectable—" "Oh, it's that, miss, I'll guarantee 33, Straker's Farm," &amp;c. Rhodes-street, off the Grosvenor - road. Best leave your luggage in the cloak:room, and Mrs. I. Diver's Jim'll fetch it." Twilight was falling when, one late spring day, It was to the girl a dreadful ordeal to face the the London train slackened speed as it neared unknown Mrs. Diver; but the spur of necessity East Grinstead. In the compartment next the drove her, and the landlady, after all, was not guard's van the jar of the brake disturbed one of very formidable, and took the application as a the two passengers, a man, and he looked up matter of course, asking no questions beyond the from the novel he was reading to find the dark name of her lodger. For a shilling a night, or eyes of the girl who had got in at the last station Six ,_,shillndys was offered a small and poorly- spoke a week if paid in advance. Miss ra fixed anxiously upon him. Meeting his glance she spoke in a soft, tremulous voice: furnished, but perfectly clean, bedchamber, and "I beg your pardon, but I want to know if Promptly took it for the longer period. In you'll help me out of my difficulty ?" • another half-hour her portmanteau was delivered The man, surprised at this speech, mechanically to her, and she was settled in her new quarters. repeated the last word as he stared at her. She A feeling of elation at having so easily got was quite young, and exceedingly pretty, with over the initial difficulty made her contemplate dark, expressive eyes and clear-cut, regular the future with comparative cheerfulness. She features. Her face was pale, and her attitude, as examined her resources; they amounted to exactly she nervously played with her handkerchief, two guineas besides a few jewels. Surely it betokened much agitation. would be enough until she got a post as "I've run away," she went on, a little colour governess? Thus reflecting with the buoyancy of returning to her face—"run away from my step- youth and the courage of ignorance, she dismissed mother. I gave her the slip and just caught the all anxiety and went to bed. The lumpy mattress train. I left her on the platform; didn't you kept her awake awhile, and she found herself hear her shouting after me and ordering the thinking of the man in the train; he had a nice train to stop ? " face and no doubt he meant well, but a lone The man vaguely remembered there had been bachelor girl cannot be too particular, and she some commotion as the train left the last station, acted wisely in snubbing him. So deciding, she but had not connected it with his fellow- fell sleep. passenger, who with a basket trunk had rushed IL ' into the carriage as the train was starting; Hilda Sandys had received a varied and excellent absorbed in his book he had hardly noticed her education, but the art of economising money, of before she spoke. making a little go a long way, had not been "It's just struck me she'll telegraph to East included among her accomplishments. This is Grinstead and have me stopped. I suppose she the only explanation for the fact that when a can," the girl said, interrogatively—"she's my week had gone by her capital was reduced to guardian." seven and sixpence, though she had practically "Well, I don't know," replied the man. "I'm starved herself, living for the most part on tea not up in law, but I should imagine she has a and scones eaten at tea-shops, and walking a legal right to stop your running away." great deal to save omnibus fares. But the worst 1 "That's what I thought, and that's why I want of it was, she was as far as ever from getting the your help." kind of post she sought, and she began to fear • "But what can I do ? " she never would. "I want to be your sister—just for the three 1 Her plan of campaign had seemed very simple minutes the train stops at East Grinstead. Then when first thought out; the agencies she must if the guard comes and wants to know who I amf avoid, for they would immediately ask for you'll say, ' the lady is my sister,' and then he references and shake their heads individually and can't do anything." I collectively when she said she 'had none to give. The man hesitated, wondering what the legal But by answering advertisements and advertising penalty was for anyone convicted of adopting a herself, and calling personally on ladies replying, strange young lady met in a railway carriage as she would surply meet some sympathetic lady to a sister; he temporised. I whom she could confide her history, and who 1 "Is your stepmother quite unendurable—is it would be glad of her services. Unfortunately I quite imperative that you should escape from her , the desired lady had not materialised. Her own to-night?" ' advertisements broughtWo suitable replies, and at The train was rushing past the simml-box' and those houses where she called in answer to an the girl's eyes filled with tears. "fwill not go advertisement she was generally told the post was back to her !" she cried. "I can't bear it any ' filled, while the few ladies she did interview longer—l would rather die!" ! were one and all so unpleaffluit that the conversa- The sight of her tears utterly vanquished the tion never got so far as the subject of references. man. "Don't cry," he said, hastily, as though i It was the casual question of her landlady speaking to a child. " All right, I'll say you're . which brought matters to a crisis. "Are you my sister if any questions are asked." I going to stay on another week, miss?" Mrs. 1 The train stopped as he uttered the promise, 1 Diver had inquired, letting her in as she returned and the girl with a grateful whisper of "thank I about four o'clock after an unsuccessful journey ' you so much," dabbed her eyes with her handker- Ito Hampstead. Then Hilda, replying she would chief, and moving opposite to him picked up his I decide on the morrow, had made the discovery discarded paper and pretended to be engrossed in ! previously mentioned. it. She looked perfectly unconcerned now, and 1 "I'll turn servant, take a place as parlour- her newly-adopted brother, as he anxiously awaited , maid," was the desperate resolve she came to developments, saw she was quite equal to playing ' after an hour's gloomy reflection. her role. He held his novel before him, his But for parlourmaids, scarce though they be, I ears strained for the opening of the door, and references are quite as much a necessity as for I the guard or stationmaster appearing, but the ' governesses. Hilda was practical enough to unexpected happened, the door remained closed, 1 recognise that. She turned out her purse again in and,undisturbed and uninterrogated,three minutes 1 vague hopes of finding more money, but there later the train sped them Londonwards. I was only the card of her East Grinstead Station The man drew a deep breath of relief as they "brother." The girl read the name—Mr. Francis moved on; he was unfeignedly glad at their Thorpe, Junior Reform Club. escape, while the girl's joy was only tempered by I Hilda felt desperate, and clutched at the card silent regret that in her extremity she had given like a drowning person at a straw. She recalled , a stranger of the opposite sex so much of her his face, appearance' manner; a pleasant, honest- confidence. I looking man of about thirty. Such was the Mr. "I think you're safe," said the man; "we only 1 Thorpe she remembered, and he had said she stop at Croydon until they collect tickets at . might need him. Truly the time had come, and Battersea." _ ! she sat down forthwith and wrote as follows:</p>
        <p>"I needlessly alarmed myself. I'm sorry I troubled you," she murmured. The man laughed, and then grew grave. "You're doing a very foolish thing, he said, looking critically at her. "Are you going to friends? You're not so mad as to go to Leman by yourself if you've no one to go to who knows you?" The speech was tactless, and the girl stiffened; she had carefully scanned him, and decided he could be trusted ere she first spoke, and in her relief at her escape she would have trusted him altogether had he been more diplomatic. "Oh, yes, I have a friend in London to go to— an old governess, she keeps a boarding-house," she answered, distantly. He saw by her manner she did not care to be questioned and relapsed into silence, pulling his moustache and furtively regarding her. He began to grow very curious; she was an interesting enigma, and how very pretty! Her dress was quiet, a dark skirt and jacket and an unobtrusive hat, but she wore them with an air of distinc- tion, and he somehow correctly guessed they were well made and expensive; she was unmistak- ably a lady. Suddenly she looked up and caught his eyes. "You are not overburdened with luggage," he remarked, merely to cover his scrutiny. "I couldn't get more away without fear of detection. I've got another frock there and a few necessaries," she replied, nodding at the basket portmanteau—"all I shall want at present." "Well, I hope you and your stepmother will soon be reconciled." "I think not. I have no intention of returning. I mean to stay in London and earn my living." "How?" he could not resist asking. She raised her eyebrows. "Oh, there are many ways—marriage, thank Heaven is not the only career open to a girl nowadays! I see you are reading Scribbler's new novel. What do you think of his style?" The man accepted the hint the words conveyed, and they talked on impersonal topics till Battersea was reached and their tickets collected. At this stage,reliet that she was quite safe from re-capture drew from the girl a word of gratitude for his action at East Grinstead. "If I can be of any further service—" he began. "Thank you, I can manage for myself quite well now." But he persisted. "I don't wish to force my acquaintance upon you," he said, speaking with a certain embarrassment, "but isn't it possible you may have need of me presently? There are times when the advice of a disinterested friend— won't you accept my card?" "You are very good," she said, coldly, and taking the card as the train ran into the station, put it in her purse without looking at it. "Now I'll put you into a hansom and bid you good-night," he said. "Please don't trouble, I should prder a porter to do that." She was quite within her right in snubbing him, but the man was illogical enough to feel angry. He opened the door and called a porter. "Attend to the lady," ho said, and raising his hat to her stepped out and strode off without a word.</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir,—A week ago you said I might need your help again. It is perfectly true, though I didn't think so then. I have failed to get a post as governess' and as I want immediate work, have decided to turn domestic servant—parlour- maid—for a while. Even for this I must give references. I can't write home. Would you mind being my reference ?—Faithfully yours, "HILDA SAHDYS." To this somewhat naive missive, posted immediately, the following reply came the next • morning: "Dear Miss Sandys,—ln reply to your letter it is necessary for me to see you before doing what you ask. I will call at the address you give at half-past five this afternoon. Faithfully yours, "FRANCIS THORPE." Mr. Thorpe was punctual. A very diminutive girl let him into a very diminutive passage, and asking him to wait in the 'all a minute, went upstairs. Following her summons, Hilda came down the narrow stairs in her hat and jacket. "It's very kind of you to come," she said, simply. "I'm sorry there's nowhere to ask you 1 to sit down. If you don't mind walking down Grosvenor-road with me—" She was just the same, no, she was different, the man decided, as he muttered something in reply. She looked older, thinner, though not less beautiful, than the girlish runaway of the week before. There was the same proud air and innate refinement of manner that had previously attracted him. He felt tongue-tied, and it was she who spoke first as the shabby door closed behind them. "You see, I've come down in the world," she said, with . sad smile, indicating the house they , had left. "You bai • friends to go to? I half suspected it." "None, and-I've spent almost all my moneys advertising and hunting for a place as governess, so I must descend to the kitchen." "But that is absurd. You must go home." "I won't!" she cried, mutinously. "You seriously mean to turn parlourmaid ? " "Yes, and if you won't give me a reference I'll forge one, or look for a place where servants are so badly wanted they won't ask for one." "If I am to help you you must take me a little more into your confidence." "Oh, I'll tell you everything. My father was Professor Sandys." "The analytical expert? He died three years ago, did he not?" "Yes. I was only a iaby when my mother died. Papa married again when I was ten, and I got on pretty well with my stepmother even after papa's death, but six months ago her son by a former marriage Caine back from New Zealand. He's an odious man. I think he had a sunstroke there, and he's certainly more than half mad. He hadn't been with us a week before he asked me to marry him, and his mother actually approved and encouraged him. Between them they gradually made life unbearable, and presently Hubert got on may nerves and I began to he afraid of him. He got worse, I really couldn't stop. It wasn't safe."' "But had you no friends in the neighbourhood A doubt as to whether she had been wise to to shelter you? " repel his friendly advances crossed the girl's "By an unfortunate chance the only family I mind at his abrupt departure. But it was too could appeal to arc away on a sea voyage." late to confess the truth, to confess the j It was much the kind of story Mr. Thorpe had ex-governess was a myth, that she knew not anticipated,a.nd he straightway made up his mind. where to go, that she had not a single friend in "You put me rather in a difficulty, Miss Sandys. London, even had it been the wiser course. I do not quite see how I could be your reference, "Keb, miss?" said the porter, taking the for all I know of you rests on your own word_ portmanteau. but that I believe implicitly. Still, I can .get you "No, take it to the cloak-room, please." a iilace as a parlourmaid at a certain house—my Then, as they walked along the platform, she had own." an inspiration. "I want a lodging for the night "Oh!" said Hilda, blankly, and the colour near the station.." she said. nbruntiv. can't rushed to her face. "You don't like the idea? Well, if you will turn parlourmaid, you could hardly be better off. My sister keeps house for me, and we keep three servants, cook, housemaid, parlourmaid; there</p>
        <p>neneve you are dreadfully afraid of her. "I am, more or less," he confessed, "but I assure you she would not raise a finger to help you if you told her the truth. I have indicated the only way by which you can rease your ambitions."</p>
        <p>"You need not laugh at me," said' Hilda, indignantly; but he gravely declared he had no intention of doing so, and taking her consent for granted, went on to arrange the details of her assumption of her new role.</p>
        <p>The Thorpes lived in Kensington-square, in a hause left them by their father, who had made a moderate Cortune as an electrical engineer, chiefly through one or two patents he took out, and F.-ancis carried on the business. It languished throuzh competition and lack of capital. Thorpe had not a free hand, his sister, as partner under their father's will having a voice iri the manage- ment; borrowing money would probably save tha dwindling trade, but Miss Thorpe( with narrow- minded obstinacy, denied her consent to this.=l</p>
        <p>"Oh!. you're the new parlourmaid, are you?" said the servant who Welled the door to Hilda in har new character. "Come and 'Ave a cup of tea before you take your things off. The old cat's out for the afternoon. Cook—Miss Smith, the new parlourmaid. Been out before, Miss Smith? Ah! I thought not. Well, you'll find it pretty comfortable 'ere if you can stand the old cat's tongue." • Miss Thorpe was not a good mistress from the .ervants' point of view. She belonged to that class found in all stations of life, and called "naggers." She was a petty tyrant. But the modern servant will not stand much tyranny, and Miss Thorpe had often unwillingly to curb her tongue. She would relieve her feelings on her brother, who heartily wished he had the power of giving "notice" like a domestic. But he was hardened to Louisa's ways and Louisa's tongue, and used to console himself with the reflection "How much worse off I should be if she was my wife." "She seems quite a superior kind of young woman," said , Louisa, referring to the new pariourznaid. "But very uncommunicative. I hope you made sure her references were genuine, Francis?"</p>
        <p>"I'm perfectly satisfied they are, Louisa." It was a strange life for Hilda, gently nurtured, and naturally rtither proud of disposi- tion, and yet she shook into her position with comparative ease. Happily the humour of it all sweetened contemplation, and, much to her sur- prise, she found herself not absolutely unhappy. It was not as if she had to be a parlourmaid all her life. she explair.A to Mr. Thorne. who</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FAMOUS PHYSICIAN Prescribes a Simple Remedy for INCREASING STRENGTH and FLESH.</head>
        <p>A FAMOUS PHYSICIAN Prescribes a Simple Remedy for INCREASING STRENGTH and FLESH.</p>
        <p>are hardly any guests, so you won't be hard worked. We have no parlourmaid now, and my An eminent physician, who is well known as a sister asked me to go to a registry office about specialist and author, writes :—" No treatment for one. It will be understood I engage you from Phthisis, or weak longs, would he complete without Gillett's Agency, and your references are satis- Dr. Csssell's Tablets. I have subjected these Tablets fletory." , to the severest tests and the results were of the most "But—won't you tell your sister who lam ? " satisfactory kind. They increase strength and develop "No, she wouldn't approve of your—inde- flesh by their power to aid assimilation. I attribute pendence. She's much older than I am, and several important cures mainly to the timely use of rather old-fashioned. Besides, forgive my saying these Tablets—they have been added to my Thst of so, she would at once suspect you of designs successful medicines." " Surely the u on ire. authority opinion of inch a gteat -• " authority is sufficient to convince sufferers from nerve "Designs troubles, stomach and kidney disorders, thinness and "Matrimonial," he said, apologetically. "It's weakness of the body,wrecked constitution, decline,and it craze of hers; any passably_ good-looking girl similar ailments, that 101 d. laid out in a box of this who exchanges two sentences with me is at once successful remedy would be greatly to their advantage, suspected of yearning to marry me." Any chemist can supply Dr. Cassell's Tablets. Hilda laughed to cover her eonfnstion..</p>
