      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>always contrived tb exchange a rew Serftences with her daily, either at breakfast, which meal he took by himself, or in the evening when in his study. "How do you get on with the other servants ? " he asked.</p>
        <p>Hilda shrugged her shoulders. "I hate associating with them," she said, frankly. "But I'm careful not to give myself airs, and we are friendly. I've propitiated Mrs. Groves— this was the cook—by trimming her Sunday hat. I have a room to myself, which is much to be thankful for. And if the baker's man wouldn't make eyes at me and accuse me of being 'aughty because I reject his advances, I should be fairly resigned." These snatched conversations and whispered confidences became quickly the one bright spot in the lives of the nominal mister And the new parlourmaid. Thorpe told Hilda something of his business worries, and her frank sympathy brightened his hitherto lonely lot. So passed about five weeks, and then one morning when Hilda took up "master's breakfast," he shelved her the following advertisement in the personal column of the Standard. "Will Miss Hilda 8— communicate with Messrs. Beresford, Solicitors, Lincoln's Inn?"</p>
        <p>"They were papa's lawyers," said Hilda. "I wonder what it means ? Will you go and see them, Mr. Thorpe,and find out, but don't give them my address. I expect it's only. Mrs. Sandys trying to find me."</p>
        <p>_ That very morning there came a crisis in the career of the new parlourmaid, of which Kate was the immediate, and the baker's man the responsible, cause. Mention has b'een made of the gentleman. It must with pain le recorded he had jilted Kate in favour of Hilda, and the latter's frosty reception of his suit failed to quench the smouldering anger in Kate's breast. For Kate loved the baker's man. It broke forth, and in no ambiguous terms. Kate, who had been lately watching and spying, accused the parlour- maid "of setting her cap at the master. Hilda lost her temper, and delivered her private opinion of both Kate and the baker's man, which did not tend to amity._</p>
        <p>Hilda. silenced' Kate, but the latter went to Miss Thorpe and related "the carryings on" of the master with the parlourmaid she had observed. How • Hilda lingered in the break- fast-room in the morning; how Mr. Thorpe invariably invented some excuse to ring for her each evening when in his Study; and how they gossiped on such occasions. Quite long conversa- tions, the words of which Kate, with the best will in the world, and an ear to the keyhole,could not overhcp,. Mae ilaar.' CM ath inst. I aa.a swaar</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Examiner Satdrday, March 14, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Satdrday, March 14, 1908.</p>
        <p>to," eofidluded the taleteller, "he calls her Misi Sandys."</p>
        <p>"The profligate!" ejaculated the scandalised Miss Thorpe. 'Send the deceiving creature here, Kate." And when Hilda appeared, the storm broke forth.</p>
        <p>"You've got a secret understanding with my innocent'brother," began the spinster, and epithets such as "hussy," "wretched servant," "designing creature," hurtled about Hilda's ears, amid a hurricane of reproaches and insults combined, with an order to quit the house that very day. The girl grew red, and then pale, and having finally secured a hearing, proceeded to make a clean breast of it. She said who she was, how she had first met Francis Thorpe, and the nature of the mysterious understanding Kate had detected.</p>
        <p>But Miss Thorpe heard the tale with a sniff of incredulity. "You're nothing Lont an adventuress," she declared, "and your real object is to try and entrap my brother into a marriage." "Hey, what's that?" exclaimed Mr. Thorpe, entering at that moment. "Who's trying to entrap me? What is all this about ? " "Mr. Thorpe," cried Hilda, half laughing, half in tears, "Kate has been telling tales about our talks together, so I've had to explain to your sister who I am, and how you befriended me. But she doesn't quite seem to believe." "This young lady is really Miss Sandys, Laura, daughter of the late eminent Professor Sandys, and being obliged to leave her home and being determined to earn her own living—" "Bah! Determined to catch a husband ! " inter- rupted Miss Thorpe. "You fool, Francis, can't you see she's an adventurecs, and wants to inveigle you into a marriage?" "Financially it would be a splendid thing for me if she did, as she's worth twenty thousand pounds, and we are rapidly descending to bank- ruptcy. Now hold your tongue, and don't further insult the lady who has honoured me with her friendship.</p>
        <p>"Your ;tep-brother is dead," continued Thorpe, addressing Hilda directly. "He died in a fit, and your stepmother has gone into a semi- religious institution. But the Cattermoles are back from their travels and longing to have you to live with them."</p>
        <p>"Oh, I'm so glad !" cried Hilda. "They were not coming back till the autumn." "You can go to them at once. And next month you will be twenty-one, and come into twenty thousand pounds your father left you. You were not to be told of this fortune till you were twenty-One, but your stepmother, already desiring to marry you to her son, suppressed the information. Mr. lieresford understanding I was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO MR. S. W. JEFFERY.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. S. W. JEFFERY.</p>
        <p>. As we briefly intimated in our last issue, Mr. S. W. Jeffery was on Friday evening last the recipient of a handsome present from the Warrington Certificated Class Teachers' Association. The gift consisted of a mahogany writing bureau, tastily inlaid with satin-wood. The interior is fitted with drawers and pigeon holes. A brass plate,bearing the following inscription is affixed:— "Warrington Certificated Class Teachers' Association. Presented to S. W. Jeffery, in recognition of his services as founder and secretary, 6th March, 1908." It was supplied by Messrs.J.M.Greenhalgh and Son,Ltd., of Bridge-street. • The CHAIRMAN (Mr. J. Morgan) said it was with very great regret that at the last meeting of the associa- tion they heard that Mr. Jeffery had decided to give up the position of secretary. They all admired him for his geniality and straightforwardness, and respected him in many ways; but they thought most about him for the i way n which he had shown his courage in the cause of class teachers. Inside their own circle they had proved his worth ; but outside, in the Lancashire Federation of • Assistant Teachers his merits bad been recognized. ' Mr. T. EDDEN made the presentation and said that to honour one long in service, and loyal to the cause was .pleasing indeed. Working in the cause of the teachers, Mr. Jeffery had a record which was not to be despised. In his career he had held the position of secretary to the local branch of the N.U.T. ler some two years. He had been president twice, and he was the only local , teacher who had ever gained that honour. He had seen some twelve years' continuous service as a member .of the committee. Be was the founder of their Class j Teachers' Association, and had been secretary for nearly j five years. tie was the secretary of the local branch of , the Teachers' Provident Society, and he had been one ' of the delegates on their behalf to the conference on several occasions. (Applause.) In the list of subscrip- tions, taking the names of the various local schools, everyone was represented, a fact which was very gratifying and spoke volumes as to the esteem and kind regard in which Mr. Jeffery was held by the teachers. He must have made great personal sacrifices in looking after the interests of class teachers. He had great pleasure indeed in asking Mr. Jeffery to accept . the handsome writing bureau as a token of their esteem and regard, and they all hoped he might live long to put it to good use. (Applause.) Messrs. F. H. LONGSHAW, I. BOWEN, J. ARMSTRONG and F. J.EmsoN also spoke of the valuable work which Mr. Jeffery had acomplished. Mr. JEFFERY, in reply said, he hoped the members did not think he had looked for any reward, nor that he needed any inducement to continue his interest. To be debarred from taking an active part in questions affect- ing the interests of teachers would be to him a severe punishment. There was need for an association like this and there was likely to be still greater need. In the new Education Bill now before the country there were benefits foreshadowed, but at the aame time there were great disadvantages. Were teachers to be split up again into two different factions? Were teachers of provided schools to look down upon their less fortunate</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>'Beware of Substitutes; ask for " OWBRIDGE "when buying Lung Tonic and</p>
        <p>'FAKE NO °THERA</p>
