      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>lin your confidence. gave me this information." "Oh, how could she? I thought I was penniless. And I can go to the Cattermoles I to-day." • ' "You see your troubles are all over." 1 "Yes," said Hilda. She looked about her, they I were alone. Miss Thorpe had beaten a retreat, 1 the girl laughed hysterically. "I'm an inde- I endent young lady—l, the beloved of the baker's man!" "Go and pack," said Thorpe: "How glad you must be to get away." "You must let me help you," she said, eagerly. "You must let me lend you some money. Yes; I insist. What would have befallen me if you had not been my friend? I owe you more than I can ever repay." "Child, child," he said, roughly, "I can't take your money, and I won't. Come, say good-bye." "You seem anxir Ili to get rid of me ? " she cried, reproachfully. • He turned away without answering, and walked to the window. Yet their eyes met ere he did so, and that look was a revelation to Hilda, not only of his feelings towards her, but why Kate's coarse gibe had so infuriated her. She took her courage in both hands and followed him. She spoke lightly: "Then good-bye, Mr. Thorpe. I shall have such funny stories to tell my friends of my experiences RS your sister's parlourmaid. And how I was accused of setting my cap at you." "I think you might spare me that," he said. He would not turn, he could not trust himself. Hilda came close behind him, stood on tiptoe, and whispered distinctly into his ear. And this is what she said: , "Oh—you—goose!" A mere chronicler cannot explain why immediaely afterwards he turned and took her into his arms, and why the lady raised no ~rniest worthy of consideration. He merely records it Nuts so, ana mat a few minutes later Thorpe said as one stating a fact: "It's certainly time for another change of relationship. 'you've been my sister and my parlourmaid, and now you shall be my wife." And Hilda did not contradict him. I [THE END.]</p>
        <p>WIDNES GASWORICERS' TREATMENT. At Widnes Town Council on Tuesday, the Gas and Water Committee reported that they had fully investi- gated the charges brought by Councillor George Davies of inhuman treatment of the men at the gasworks. The Committee declined to believe that there was any ground whatever for the charges.—Councillor Clarke said that Councillor Davies was chairman of the Gas Committee for years, and, as conditions had not altered, he must have sanctioned that to which he now objected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>brethren whose lot would be to ioil in " Contracted- out " schools ? (Applause.) He hoped they would not go back to the" dark days." But that one and a half millions was not to be 'sneezed" at,and abolition of tests for teachers would probably mean an improve- ment. However, he appreciated their kindness,especially as the presentation had been made by their treasurer, with whom be had worked so long. He accepted the gifts with many thanks, and trusted he might long con- tinue to merit esteem full regard. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>CHRIST CHURCH.—Special sermons were preached on Sunday in aid of church expenses. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. J. W. Glendenning, of St. Barnabas, Warrington. In the evening there was a large congregation the preacher being the vicar-designate the Rev. G. W. Dickenson, of Southport, whose theme was " Character." DISTRICT NuuSE Fun.—The whist drive promoted by a committee of the ladies' sewing meeting for the purpose of raising funds in aid of the Padget° and District Nursing Association, which took place in the Church House produced £7 10s. The winners were : Ladies : 1, Miss Diamond; 2, Mrs. Maginn ; consola- tion prize, Miss Whitlow. Gentlemen : 1, Mr. E. Alien ; 2, Mr. E. Atkinson ; consolation prise, Mr. F. Blackwell.</p>
        <p>Theft by a Servant. At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, Dora Buckler, eighteen, of Wellfield-street, Warrington, was charged with stealing the sum of £3 Is. 6d. and a lady's collarette, value 2s. Gd., the property of Joseph Taylor, of Hatton. It Was stated by the wife of the prosecutor that the girl entered her employ as a domestic servant about a month ago. The money, which had been placed in a cupboard, was missed on February 24th. Prisoner was at first suspected, and she at first denied the theft, but later admitted it, and revealed the money, which was hidden under the washstand. The magistrates bound her over for twelve months under tits Probation of Offenders Act.</p>
      </div>
