      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>INFLUENZA SEQUEL.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA SEQUEL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CASE AT THE ASSIZES.I Remarkable Action Against Essence</p>
        <p>Manufacturers.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday, before Mr. Justice Pickford and a special jury, Thomas Henry Pretty, Oakdene, Padgate, traffic manager for Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, brewers, of Warrington, and Edith Mabel, his wife, sued Brand and Co., of London, proprietors of Brand's Essence, for damages for injuries sustained by the wife and an infant 'son through their taking a tin of the essence which, it was alleged, had been negligently prepared, and had set up ptomaine poisoning. They also set up a breach of warranty. The defendants denied neglect, and pleaded that there was no warranty or breach of warranty. Mr. Horridge, K.C., M.P., and Mr. Cuthbert Smith (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) were counsel for the plaintiffs ; and Mr. Sanderson, K.C., and Mr. Crawford (instructed by Messrs. Trotter and Paterson) for the defendants.</p>
        <p>PLAINTIFF'S CASE. Mr. Horridge explained that last September Mrs. Pretty was laid up with influenza of the rheumatic type. The doctor suggested Brand's essence as part of her diet, and three or four tins of this preparation were taken by the patient without ill effect. On September 26th a new tin was opened, which had been purchased the day before from a Warrington chem,st. A few hours after taking the essence on this date Mrs. Pretty became violently sick, and later on the little boy, to whom half a teaspoonful had been given, also became very unwell. Mrs. Pretty was confined to her bedtoom for three weeks, and was unable to leave her home for some considerable time after that. At present she was very much broken in health. Mr. Pretty had communi- cated with defendant company, complaining of what had happened, and in that letter he pointed out ' that the essence in this particular tin had been in liquid form instead of a jelly, as in the tins he had pur- chased previously. He also mentioned that Dr. Collins, his wife's adviser, had stated that the essence was unlit to eat, and had set up ptomaine. Defendants replied disclaiming responsibility,as every precaution was taken with all the tms of the essence sent out from their establishment. Counsel pointed out, as a matter of fact that if the tin had been proflkly sterilised and herme- tically sealed it would have kept tor almost any length of time.</p>
        <p>Thomas FL Pretty, traffic manager for Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons in his evidence stated that the little boy was practically well again, but his wife, in his opinion, was at present a physical wreck. Mrs. Pretty also gave evidence. Dr. Celina stated that before her illness Mrs. Pretty had been a strong woman. The influenza she contracted in September was not of the gastric type. After par- taking of the particular tin of essence mentioned Mrs. Prettv's condition was dangerous for at least a week, and she had up to the present been unable to leave home.</p>
        <p>When he examined the essence of which Mrs. Collins had partaken, he found it to be in the first stage of putrefaction._</p>
        <p>Dr. W. B. Warrington physician to the David Lewis Northern Hospital, said he did not consider that there had been any putrefaction in the contents of the tin. The bacteria which produced food poisoning were not putrefactive. In cross-examination witness said that even the most infinitesimal puncture in the tin might have admitted putrefactive bacteria.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PTOMAINE BACTERIA.</head>
        <p>THE PTOMAINE BACTERIA.</p>
        <p>The most remarkable medical evidence was that of Dr. Craven Moore, of Manchester, lecturer on food inspection, Ac.. in the University Of Manchester. Dr. Moore said that simple putrescence was not a necessary condition of poisoning. This condition was due to the presence of one set of bacteria, whilst the production of ptomaine was due to the work of another and rarer family. The latter could only have found their way into the essence at the time of the manufacture, either through the use of unsound meat or contamination during the manufacture or making up, but when once there, and sealed up, they might remain dormant for years and at once become active on the tin being opened. The microbes of putrescence on the other hand were a much more numerous and active family. The most minute puncture was enough to give them access to the contents of the tin. When once there they would become very active, and would speedily destroy the microbes of ptomaine ; and on the tin being opened the smell would at once proclaim the fact. Questioned on the point as to whether the microbes of putrescence were not equally injurious, he said they certainly Were not. A person eatingrputrescent meat, &amp;a. for the first time might be made sick and ill for a short time, but not to any extent and not nearly so seriously as in the case of ptomaine poisoning. High game was simply game in a putrid state, and they knew that the Chinese preferred putrid eggs. It was no doubt fortunate that the bacteria of putrescence ate up and destroyed the bacteria of ptomaine poisoning. THE DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>The defence was an absolute denial of negligence. and detailed testimony was produced of the precau- tions taken in the preparation of the essence. Emphasis was laid on the fact that every tin of essence was sub- jected to a hot brine bath at the temperature of 230 degrees, which it was said, was amply sufficient to sterlise it completely and kill any bacteria. The defen- dants also disclaimed any warranty, and urged that they could not be responsible for any tin five years old, such as this tin, which from its make had certainly left their factory at the latest in 1902. It was even possible it was one of a Plot of surplus stores which bad come back to this country after the South African War, and had passed through changeable climatic and other unfavourable conditions. Thomas Dance, managing director of Brand and Co., Ltd. gave evidence to show that there was not the slightest risk or danger of contamination in the process of the mansfacture of the essence.</p>
        <p>--- . Cross-examined, witness said that they would not guarantee the essence for more than two years, because it might 'become cloudy And be less agreeable, though not injurious. Alexander Henry Deuce gave a description of the sterilising bath of 230 degrees in which all the tins were placed, and of the other measures of precaution adopted. Although the essence had been frequently analysed it had never been known to contain bacteria. Other evidence of a similar character was given. Francis Henry Tate, senior partner of Messrs. Norman Tate and Co., said that as the result of an analysis of the essence and the tin he was satisfied that it was care- fully prepared meat essence, and a thoroughly sound food. The process described by the witnesses was an exceedingly good one. There were no preservatives in the essence.</p>
        <p>Herbert E. Davies. the public analyst, of Southport, testified to similar effect.</p>
        <p>His Lordship said tit, plaintiffs' case bad this odd feature, that whereas it originally rested 'on the idea that the essence was in a state of incipient putrefac- tion, that idea was now discarded and quite the opposite view was put forward, and it was seen that in order to succeed it was necessary to prove that there was no putrefaction at all. Dealing with the fact that it had not been possible to trace the history of the tin since the date of its issue by the defendants, his Lordship remarked that large quantities of the essence had been sent out to South Africa durinst the war, and a consider- able proportion was subsequently sold—like so much of the other surplus stores—at disastrous prices to the Government. Some of the tins bad found their way back to England, and Brands had spent £l,lOO in buying them up for the purpose of destroying them and so pro- tecting their good name.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>JURY'S DISAGREEMENT.</head>
        <p>JURY'S DISAGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>The jury, at the end of an hour and twenty minutes' deliberation, communicated to the judge that there was no possibility of their coming to an agreement. Council on both sides ag-eed that there would be no use in keening the jury any longer, and they were accordingly discharged. "One word of advice T will give you,' his Lordship said. "1 daresay you will he approached by people to know how you were divided in the jury room. I think it would be just as well not to tell them." Mr. Sanderson gaid that the defendants would like to take the judge's ruling on the point of law which he had raised at the opening of the defence—that was, supposing the plaintiffs' statement were nccepted in its entirety, there was no case against the defendants. His Lordship said he would hear the arguments on that question whenever it suited the convenience of counsel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WORTH KNOWING.</head>
        <p>WORTH KNOWING.</p>
        <p>With so many people starving, and food scarce and dear, it is well to know that, owing to the American financial crisis, a large quantity of Persian DATES have been consigned to England, which otherwise would have been consumed in the United States, and this fruit can, at the present moment, be bought at Grocers and Fruiterers at l?,d. to 2d. per lb. The analysis Is :-54 p.c. sugar. 26 p.c. albumen, etc. 20 p.c. moisture. . _ -</p>
        <p>• It is marvellously cheap food. That Dates have wonderful sustaining properties ia proved by the fact that many of the virile races of Arabia, Persia, and other Countries, whose only food is bread and Dates, are remarkable for their splendid physique and powers of endurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DEATH AT A COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>DEATH AT A COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon Mr. John Lambert, a member of the Bradford City Council, died suddenly at the Town Hall. He was takiog part in a discussion when he had a seisure, and was removed to an ante-room, where he expired in a few minutes. The meeting was at once adjourned. Mr. Lambert was a well-known wool-comber.</p>
        <p>ECZEMA. Nothing spoils a good disposition, Nothing taxes a man's patience, Like any irritation of the skin.</p>
        <p>Piles almost drive you mad. All day they make you miserable. All night they keep you awake.</p>
