      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS</p>
        <p>Carrie : "He said he would go to the end ol the earth for me." Maude: "What did you say?" Carrie: "I proposed that he compromise, and simply go to my home." Kate : it's a mercy you left Wurzel- shire and have come to spend a few days with us, Cousin Joe! I suppose down where you live the people knew nothing of concerts or even social hops?" Cousin Joe: "Aw ! Moi feyther ha' ten acres o' nowt else but hops, but I dunno about social 'uns. We call um Kent hops." The Count: "I haf been told, madame, your daughtaire haf ze bad tempaire." The Mamma:. "Ah, yes, count; but you know she loses her temper so easily." The Count: hose loafly !"</p>
        <p>"Are we free men . or . are we slaves?" thundered the orator. "I pause for a reply.° "Well, most of• us are married," came the answer from the very last row of seats. It was at the 'house of the bride's parents after the wedding. On a table were exposed the .costly 'presents. Two gentlemen were examining them. Said one of them, suddenly:l "These, you say, are the bride's presents; but what does the groom get?" "Oh." replied the other, "he gets the woman !" First Gentle- man: "Is that all? Poor fellow !"</p>
        <p>"It's perfect-ly d!sgraceful!" she exclaimed.. "What's the matter?" inquirsd her husband. "Here's a description of a woman who gets ur, in public and declares herself an anarchist. "Well," was the rejoinder, "maybe it's all for the best. I'd 'like to see women crowd the males out of the anarchist business. When they throw bombs they wouldn't be so likely " hit anybody." "How old would on take me to be, Mr. 4, Griffin?" she lisped, looking unutterable things. "Des't know, I'm sure," responded Griffin, tugging nervously at his moustache. "I an' awfullv..olcl. I assure you," she went on. "I've seen twenty-thres summers." "Then you ought to wear glasses," responded Griffin, .earnestly. "What! Glasses at twenty-three? Oh, Mr, Griffin !" "Yes, your eyesight must be bad.' "I'm sure I don't know why you should think so." she pouted. "Well," said Griffin, slowly. rnd looking for a safe exq meanwhile, "I'm afraid about twenty, sninnters. have gone by' with.-ut your noticing them." "No. Mr. Jones. I cannot be your wife." "But you'll he a ,sister to Me. ,Promise that." "It is unnecessary. Your brother pro- posed to me last week and I promised to be his sister. I have therefore been 'your sister for a week."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Crabshaw: "Do you know, my dear, why womiu is am-re careful of her weddinr dress than of any other?" Crahshaw : "I suf pose it is because sho hss to pay for it herself; "Young man." said MS. N. Peck, "you will never know what re^t hliqs is until you have a home of your own." "Eh?" said the young man, astonished at such a remark from such a source. "Fact. Nobody but a man situated al I am can properly appreciate the delights ol ooing up to town for a few hours of gloriouB</p>
        <p>"Now, isn't that a burning shame?" said Mrs. Seldom, as she pushed her spectacles up on her forelikirl and laid down the morning paper. "What's that, mother?" said her youngest son - "Why. Emperor ,William gave an audience to Prince yesterday. Think of that, my son—a whole audience given away like 3° many cattle. It's awful." Two editors quarrelled across the Atlantic. One editor referred to the other's early career. "As for our contemporary," he wrote, "wh9 can we expect from a man who five years ago was peddling around with a mule, an ill-con' ditioned beast at that?" His rival did not den) it. "Our nontemporary," he wrote in his nog number, "says that five years ago we wet@ peddling around with a mule, and an ill' conditioned beast at that. He is quite right We were so occupied; but we are surprise; to find that the mule has such a Rood, memory.</p>
        <p>Charlie (who has been blowing the cornet an hour): "Say, Ned, do you think there is music in me?' Ned: "I don't know. Thk ought to be; I didn't hear any come out." Master (to coachman) : "John, just go do to the well and draw some water for Bridget John: "I was engaged to drive the horses not to draw water, sir." Master: "Oh, well, ' get the horses and carriage out and take Bri( to the well, then." Mrs. Veri Rich (viewing his masterpiece. "Yes. I'll take that one. It will just match wall-paper in the spare room." "Biddy," Pat began, timidly„ "did ye think av marryin'?" "Shure, now, th' subj, has niver intered me thoughts," demurely reoe„' Biddy. "It's sorry Oi am," said Pat, turnio away. "Wan minute, Pat !" called Biddll softly ; "ye've set me a-thinkin'." "Can't I put a burglar alarm in your house ?'e asked the agent. "No, we don't. need it," plied the lady. "But " "No, I -mean the family across the street watches the Pl3O so closely that even a burglar couldn't get without being seen !" Butcher: "What can I do fo4 you, munli Young Wife (trying to give herself the air veteran): "One pound of beef sausage, but out bone, please!" Husband of popular actress: "My wife_haqo64. fr-</p>
        <p>cided to retire from the stage." friend: -01 deed. At once?" "N —no, not exactli once. We have not yet decided upon the e%9 t year, but he will begin her first farewell t° next season." "My son," said the irate parent, "I am s_ prised, mortified, and amazed to find that a bind, at the foot of the class. I can har.dlYd. jd lieve it possible." "Why, father," replie son. "it is the easiest thin ri in the wetki.."</p>
        <p>L. v. FP,A</p>
        <p>"I came to ask for your daughter in marriage,- sir," said the 'young man. "Have you any, money of your own?" asked the careful father. "Oh, you misunderstood me, sir! Ido not want to buy her." Gendarme: "Ah, what are you doing there? I suppose you're taking a sketch of -the forti- fications?" Artist: "Goodness gracious me! Just look; I am only drawing that'old willow- tree there." Gendarme (after a look at the picture: "You won't get out of it that way.; it may just as well stand for the fort. You come along." Her Ma: "You should get hi rn to sign the pledge before you marry him." Daughter "Why? He doesn't drink." Her Ma: "No, but he may be tempted to later." A boy of five years was sent to the family doctor for medicine for his mother the other day. "Who is your Mother?" asked the doctor's wife, who was in the shop. "D'ye no ken us?" says the boy. "We're customers o' yours. We'd a baby from here lest week."</p>
        <p>_ Dudley: 'Do you think iliss Elderkin is serious in her mtontions regarding me?" Chumleigh : "You bet I do." Dudley: "Well, now, honestly, do you think she means matrimony or adoption?" Mr. Strongmind : "What trash are you read-, ing now?" Mrs. Strongmind : "Belva Lock- wood's book, ` My Efforts to get to the Bar.'" Mr. Strongmind : "Why didn't she ring the bell and have her lemonade brought to her room?"</p>
