      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Our Imperial Visitor.—The visitors of Queen Alexandra's brothers and sisters to this country have been few and far between, and her Majesty is naturally 4-Delighted in again entertaining her sister, the Dowager „„nPress of Russia, who stayed a few weeks here just rsolit this time last year. On that occasion their.slajesties spent a good deal of time in paying surprise "bts to hospitals and charitable institutions, theatres. 'museums, galleries, &amp;.e., anti it is easy to understand ~.,,waat this informal life is much appreciated by the uesvager Empress. Of her personality we do -not know _very much. Her Ife has been one of constant anxiety and fears such as would tend to harden most natures, but the Empress showed by many touching episodes last year that she is not only courageous, hut also a.haltes to the full the kindly, generous, and vivacious °lsPotition of our own much-loved Queen. The King's Holiday Resort.—Biarritz, where the Ring is spending the first few weeks of his holiday, was formerly like most other fashionable watering places, of modern times, a small fishing hamlet It was the Ex-Empress Eugenie who "discovered " Biarritz, and the Hotel du Palais, where King Edward has his apart- ments, stands on the site of the Imperial villa. which Napoleon 111, had erected there in 1855. The view from the windows presents a magnificent panorama of Mountain, sea and sky, but whilst the coast is rugged there is a fine sandy beach for bathing. There are many attractions, such as hunting, golfing, masked balls, gymkhana sports, and in fact nothing is lacking to con- atitute to social gaiety and the daily round of enjoy- naent. One hears almost more English spoken than ?tench, and King Edward's successive visits have helped to make Biarritz more popular with the visitors from this country. Queen Victoria, in the latter years of her life, usually went to one or other resort on the French or Italian Riviera, but she was at Biarritz in 1889, together with Princess Henry of I3attenburg and the baby Princess Ens, now Queen of Spain. The Royal Visit to Canada.—The visit of the Prince 4.!f Wales to Canada is to take place the last week in July, just forty-eight years after King Edward paid his list and only visit to the Dominion. It was on July 1860, that the King then the youthful Prince of Wales, landed at St. Jo'hn's Newfoundland, and after- wards made a tour of Canada, which was extended to the United States and a visit to the tomb of Washing- ton, The King must therefore remember his visit to the heights of Quebec, the famous city founded by Champlain 300 years ago, whose tercentenary celebra- ,tions are to be on something like a national scale in ,-,,,..anada in the summer. The Prince and Princess of Wales are both acquainted with the Dominion. for,after their visit to Australia in 1901, their Royal Highnesses returned by way of the American continent, and visited the leading cities of Canada. Irish Industries.—There will be a great muster of stiea of Trish title in London, for the Exhibition and sshiece the Royal Trish Industries Association, for house •I'Vd anii Lady Londonderry have lent their LoildoTevark Lane, on March 17th and 18th. Lady ing will 17% who is to take an active part in the sell- in-lass ssaYe the assistance of her beautiful daughter- sys(i)elliebt; "'lay Castlereagh, and all the Association stalls Presided over by well-known leaders of Irish the R The King and Queen and various members of Sale (la! Family always take a kindly interest in the lace' at Which all goods offered are genuine Irish work, earal, embroideries, linens, tweeds, homespuns, knitting, the lag, etc. Since the early days of the Association tso gaacla offered for eale on these occasions show a nderful improvement, in quality and craftrnanship, —"Well deserve attention, no less on account of their aerits RS from any desire to bring grist to the mill of e. Irish Cottage workers. A Curious Idea.—Dance dresses in white tulle— over two or three under-dresses of white ,rs "°n, and embroidered in silver are much in evidence. ;'.`,Le silver employed is usnally that known as "old" • o'rer, a very appropriate name for it, as in the majority as,eases it looks as though it had just been unearthed— years of neglect. Few women who have to con- __ ar ways and means., venture upon expensive silver Tisbroideries, for the simple reason that they are so niscult to preserve. If the garment they adorn is pnt rY the least bit damp, or where damp can get to it, alu.e_beautiful trimming is spoiled, or at least we have }3il29's considered it so, until this recent craze for "old" Ber .Fame along. ettairal Embroidery. Lace, braid and ribbon, hedic°2dered with beads, are greatly employed to trim, the he; Old-world looking wraps, and to " weight are „seal of fashionable over-skirts. The best varieties or skiT.ensive, but this fact need not trouble the owners sac fingers, as such work can be very quickly and bskil accomplished at home, and at little cost. The etroo.:an be bought by the ounce, and these should be Plea rs.asPon very fine florists' wire. This " rope " is to upon the lace, braid, or the ribbon, and bent Stitch :5 the pattern as desired—the wire being lightly With th clown with silk, to keep it in place. Compared °lse by es, °Id-fashioned method of sewing the beads on Fast;"ae, this procedure is very easy and expeditious. les, 'ng and Mourning.—As a nation we have become arid Ptillsticular as regards the observance of fast days ,Coatioe wearing of mourning, than is the rule on the by th "t. One sees little of Lenten fasting, except as s Boman Catholics, and the very devout, and then sofo'segards mourning, this is much more vigorously is toreed abroad that in this country. The Italian Court Weat mourning for a year and a day for the late arc ss6 and Crown Prince or Portugal. The French too, li(:)l(v)ery particular abo,:t mourning, although it is not recalla or deep in France as it is in Italy. When we e the extremes to which mourning was carried in early and mid-Victorian times, such as for instance t`a hideous garb of the newly widowed, one cannot ;egret the chsnge in public taste, that has taken place' 'asPlis country. tluCkles and Slides.—At several recent weddings !;.,atalsome sets of buckles and slides have figured among Presents. Some are carried out in gold, set with 9i-precious stones, others in aluminium, and others PE' in oxidised silver, with the design picked out in te eate enamels—the high slide at the back and the Twh° buckles being more ornamented than the rest. 54 'sets " can be threaded with ribbon or velvet, sar'°e Wearers prefer, and are much more useful and in a, better taste than the whole belts of gold, silver or Th '0414141m e : usiness Girl.—The Prince and Princess of Wales tho`ed the Business Exhibition at Olympia, at which tlelotYPical " business girl " was much in evidence, 111,-tOB tYPing, showiac appliances, time savers, and tirqat. (late systems in the conduct of office work. To a gaeatte.xtent the American hustling style hag been intro- office departments in the world of commerce, th: rince and, Princess of Wales were astonished s_l,Pista wonderful speed attained by some of the girl 'Neil 4 and were much interested in the many devices n "aye been designed to save time and trouble in lefti(Otlet of business. At many of the stalls it wa,s, relatin the "business girl" to do the" talking points lOatifoif .to typewriters, office fixtures and appliances, s_ebd so ding machines, index and letter filing systems, "age r4a, and her ability to do this to the best advan- aantrtss°nished visitors' among whom were many of the sst and shrewdest business people. MADGE. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>I DEATH OF MR W. D. HOUGHTON.</head>
        <p>I DEATH OF MR W. D. HOUGHTON.</p>
        <p>The news was received with general regret in War- rington that Mr. William Dickson Houghton, ;LP., who had long been prominently identLied with the com- mercial life of Warrington, passed away at his residence Queen's Lodge, Colwyn Bay, at 8 o'clock last Friday evening. Mr. Houghton who had attained the advanced age of eighty-two, was taken seriously ill in January, a sharp attack of pleurisy being succeeded by a paralytic seizure. Although the end was not unexpected, many people in Warrington and district heard of it in the sense of a great personal loss, for Mr. Houghton was highly esteemed by a very large circle. He was the chairman and managing director of the firm of W. D. Houghton, Sankey Wire iPils, and up to quite recently took a great interest in the workings of the concern. He came to Warrington from Halewood at an early age, and received the greater part of his education at a school which is no longer in existence. When quite a youth he displayed business capacity of no mean order, and assisted his father in a wire works at Town's End. On his father's death Mr. W. D. Houghton became a member of the firm of Smith and Houghton, wire manufacturers, Dallam-lane, but ultimately severed his connection with it and a little over thirty years ago built the present extensive works at Sankey Bridges where from 400 to 500 men and boys are employed in the manu- facture of steel wire ropes, &amp;c. He was also chairman and managing director of the firm of Hobson, Houghton and Co., Ltd. the Don Steel W ffi orks,Sheeld, where a similar number of men are engaged, so that it will be seen the late Tr. Houghton was a very busy man. He succeeded the late Earl of Derby as the chairman of the Lancashire Watch Company at Prescot. He was wonderfully preserved, and up to the time of the seizure gave promise of many more years of active life. Prior to taking up his esidence at Colwyn 13ay twenty-six years ago, Mr. Houghton lived at The Beeches, Moore. It e was a man of many interests and had a great love for pictures, his home being enriched with many choice art treasures. Many years ago he presented to the Warrington Art Gallery a beautiful picture study by H. Clarence Whaite, P.R.C.A., representing a particularly fine Welsh landscape, namely, a mountain farm, Acc., near Trefrew, in which the artist is seen at his best. At the foot of the picture are the lines— A meadow gemlike chased, 411 the brown wild,and mowers mowing in it.</p>
        <p>While living in this district Mr. HOngliton devoted much of his time to municipal affairs and was a hard- working and valued member of the Town Council. So highly were his services appreciated that he was elevated to an alderrnanic seat and it is not improbable that had he been able to continue the work he would ultimately have been appointed to the civic chair. He was an active member of several committees, but it was in the Museum Committee that he was most interested, being associated at that time with such memorable men as the first mayor of Warrington (Mr. W. Bearnont), Mr. John Rylands, and others. In religion Mr. Houghton was a Unitarian and for a long time was identified with Cairoetreet Chapel. He was one of its most generous supporters and in addition to defraying the whole of the cost of re-decoration,pre- sented a fine stained glass window depicting the Good Samaritan. He was a Conservative but never took a prominent part in political warfare. Mr. Houghton was a J.P. for the County of Denbigh and at tbe Colwyn Bay Sessions, on Saturday, the Chairman (Ur. Kneeshaw), in referring to his death, said Mr. Houghton had attended on the bench very regularly, and was very much respected in the neighbourhood. He proposed that a vote of condolence be conveyed on behalf of the court to the widow and family. This was seconded by Mr Jal-nes Wood, who said he was a very old friend of Mr. Houghton. Anyone who had known him could not have failed to notice his great devotion to duty and his magnificent industry. The resolution was carried. Mr. Houghton leaves a widow and four sons and two daughters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FUNERAL,</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL,</p>
        <p>Get this into your mind</p>
        <p>Striking testimony-io- il;e--ie-s-rTect in which the deceased gentleman was held was afforded by the large attendance at the funeral which took place in the peace- ful churchyard at Daresbury on Tuesday. The coffin was brought to Daresbury by rail, arriving shortly after noon, and a procession was formed outside the station headed by a large number of employees from Sankey Wire Mills which were closed for the day. The funeral service was of the simplest description, and was conducted by the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones (vicar) and the Rev. F. J. Barnes (curate). Amongst the mourners were Miss Dora Houghton (daughter), Miss Maggie (sister-in-law), Mr. J. Houghton, Mr. F. Houghton, Mr. H. Houghton and Mr. E. Houghton (sons), Mr. and Mrs. White, Mr. H. White, Mr. R. Wilson (Sheffield), Mr. W. D. Jeans, jun. Mrs W. Houghton Mr. R. Houghton, Mr. G. Gil), Mr. Tatham (Manchester), Mr. Menzies (Liverpool), Mr. J. Hill, Mr. R. Dixon, Mr. J. Smith, Mr. W. Sherratt, Mr. NV. Saint, Mr. J. Lang, Mr. W. Houghton. the Mayor of lVarrington (Alderman Smethurst), Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Mr. Wm. Owen, Mr. F. W. Monks, Mr. W. Sloane, Mr. E. Ridgway, Mr. J. V. Houghton, Mr. Herbert, Houghton, Mr. Percy Houghton, Mr. James Houghton, Mr. James Houghton, jun.,Mr. Harold B.Houghton,Mr. Chas. Houghton, Mr. T. Priestman, and Mr A Priestman (Liverpool), Mr. E. De Prez Mr. W. Yarwood, Mr. W..J. Wallington, Mr. J. L. Jones. Mr. H. Rigby, Mr. George Leicester. Mr. John Walker, Mr. S. Barlow, Mr. G. Taylor, Mr. W. Bramhall, Mr. J. Elliott, Mr. Joseph Harrison, Mr. Thomas Harrison, Mr. W. Forster, Mr. W. Forster, jun., Mr. J. Forster, Mr. F. Stowell, Mr. S. Highley, jun., Mr. R. Lent, Mr. A. T. Lunt, Mr. T. Sankey, Mr. R. Soarishrick. Mr. J. Goulding, Mr. P. Wilding, Mr. W. Hill, Mr. T. Clarke, Mr. A. Cook, and the following old employees :—Messrs. W. Crosskill, E. Gram, C. Flelsby, J. Gleave, J. Watkins, R. Higbley, S. Welsby, J. Cooper and .P. Pownell. The coffin, inscribed with the words, William Dickson Houghton, died-March 6th, 1908, aged 82 years," was borne to the graveside by the following employees: —Messrs. John Forster, W. Backhouse, T. Waterhouse, George Watson, George Rigby W. Hewitt, W. Bingham, R. Siddall, R. Graham, C. Hartley, and R. Jones, Messrs. Hartley and Jones being from Colwyn. - Floral tributes were placed over the grave from "The grandchildren at Penketh," " Beatriz," Mr. an Mrs. NVhite (Streatham), Mr. and Mrs. J. S. Russell (Chiches- ter), Mr. and Mrs. Sherratt, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Dixon and family, the Misses Hunt, 111 r. and Mrs. Priestman and family (Birkenhead), Mr. and Mrs. William Owen, Mr. and Mrs. Hill, the employees of Sankey Wire Mills, and there were two others from employees. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. E. Connor, Sankev-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>"Bovril repels Influenza"</head>
        <p>"Bovril repels Influenza"</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>the neck turns back a plain roll collar of the material edged with a line of machine-stitching, machining also outlining the long overlaid breast-seams. The coat fastens with two buttons, the fronts being sharply cut away from the lower button. Of novel shape, the sleeves aro almost straight from shoulder to hem, and aro finished three or four inches above the wrist by a line of machining and three small buttons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>How TO SHEW OUR FEET TO ADVANTAGE.</head>
        <p>How TO SHEW OUR FEET TO ADVANTAGE.</p>
        <p>We all have to wear short skirts sometimes, and we should pay much greater attention than we do to our shoes. Stand at the corner of a street in Paris and note the 'dainty feet of the Parisian; her dress frillings and her petticoats all as fresh as can be. It is a lesson, declares the Queen, worth -the learning. English women fail much in shoe leather. It is absurd to wear your shoes too small. It brings lines on the face and prevents a graceful carriage. Some feet look well in the ordinary shop shoes, stock sizes, but the majority require personal atten- tion. A good cream should be used daily on boots and shoes. Women look in the glass for their faces, but they seldom consult their mir- rors for their feet, or they would be more care- ful of their appearance. A pad on the foot will often keep the instep from flattening, and feet that ache after exercise should be put into hot water and well massaged with cream. Wear- ing a variety of shoes causes the pressure to be relieved. Darned stockings are not good for the feet. Fresh stockings improve the appearance of boots and shoes, and often take away the tired feeling. ' We should be far better if we could occasionally let our feet go bare. NEW BLOUSE TSTyLI.</p>
        <p>Although bodices are still a subject of deep Interest. says our Paris correspondent, the sleeve is the pivot upon which new fashions revolve. That is considered by those in authority the thing to depend upon for the coming spring, and a bit of prophecy that no one fails to be pleased with. Among the new blouses many shew the front laid in inch pleats, entirely acros,s the figure, so that the arm sleeve seams lie hidden. . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SIMPLE C.A.MISOLE,</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE C.A.MISOLE,</p>
        <p>Pictured in our sketch is a simple and most practical camisole, which is designed in the very latest taste, and is just the thin,g for hard every- day wear. The top of the camisole is decorated by scallops padded and worked in embroidery cotton. Below these scallops come buttonholed</p>
        <p>"I'' -:',--Rata\tisec • : '\, i t (1, ' \ Irough which ' . . . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>slots, ti</head>
        <p>slots, ti</p>
        <p>is threaded pink washing</p>
        <p>ribbon, which is drawn up to fit the shoulder. and tied in front. The camisole fastens down the front, and has all the fulness drawn into the waist and firmly machined into place. There arc no sleeves, their place being taken by armholes finished with a row of embroidered scallops to match the neck. India longcloth, linen, French lawn, and cambric are all. suitable materials for the realisation of this design.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>WOMEN AS COMPANY DIRECTOTIS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN AS COMPANY DIRECTOTIS.</p>
        <p>Suffragettes are beginning to make their in- fluence felt at company meetings. At the annual meeting of a London limited company the other dat, at the Hotel Cecil, a lady shareholder criticised with knowledge of finance several points in the balance-sheet of that company. She said she was glad to see the name of a woman on the directorate, and hoped to see woman take her place in Parliament.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE. Ee SWISS OMELETTES. Swiss omelettes is a dish that will be new to most cooks. Take the shreds of fowl or turkey and the dressing; mince both fine. Allow one egg for each member of the family; separate the egg, and beat the white light and dry, then fold into the beaten yolk; add to every six eggs one-half a level teaspoonful salt, one-fourth teaspoonful pepper; add one tea- spoonful cold water to each egg used. Divide the mixture in two parts; have frying-pan hot, and place in one level tablespoonful of butter. When hot turn in half of the egg mixture, and spread evenly over the pan. Cook gently until the to begins to set: then spread the meat. &amp;0.. over this, fold over the omelette, and slip oa to a hot dish until the other portion is cooked the same way. Cover the omelettes with stewed tomatoes which have been pressed through a. sieve and reheated. By dividing the ruature better-shaped omelettes are secured,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON HOME RULE BILL.</p>
        <p>The debate on Horne Rule which opened in our local Parliament on Tuesday evening bad been anticipated with much interest and there was a large attendance of members and "strangers " when the Speaker took the chair. The Clerk of the House (Mr. W. Mason) was again unable to attend and his place was taken by the Deputy Clerk (Mr. N. Cook). The Ministry was present in particularly strong force.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>QUESTION TDIE.</head>
        <p>QUESTION TDIE.</p>
        <p>In reply to the Member for Northwich (Mr. G. H. NV. Steell, the PREMIER stated that it was a fact that municipal and Government officials discharged the duties of presiding officers and poll clerks at municipal and general elections and the Government regretted it was so. They did not quite understand what the questioner meant by his statement that professional men were deprived of a perquisite in that way,but they thought the appointments should be open to heavily rated tradesmen who, having the ability to discharge the duties, were at present shut out by the appointment of officials who were drawing their salaries for the same day's work in their respective departments. (Cheers.) The MEMBER FOR IPSWICH (Mr. Wilkinson) asked the Attorney-General what steps he intended to take with reference to the unfortunate scene which occurred in the High Court when two K.C.'s came to blows. The PREMIER said the matter was being dealt with by a committee of Inns of Court and no doubt the offenders would be drastically dealt with. Mr. JENKINS (Walton division of Liverpool) asked , the President of the Board of Trade if the Government I 'proposed to do anything to prevent the unfair cora- -1 retition with the Southport shrimp industry caused by the importation of Dutch shrimps which were subse- quently sold as" Southport shrimps." The PRESIDENT OF THE BOARD OF TRADE (Mr.l Jarman) said it did not seem to be possible to take pro- ceedings under the Merchandise Marks Act. The question should be taken up with the dishonest traders who took advantage of Free Trade to allow shrimps to be imported and falsely labelled them as " Southport shrimps." Mr. Jarman concluded with the satirical remark that the Southport shrimp industry seemed, like many other industries,to be much in need of Protection. (Laughter). In reply to the member for Northwich, the P. REMIER said that with the limited time at their disposal it would be impossible to devote an evening to the dis- cussion of the unemployed question. The MEMBER FOR PETERSFIELD (Mr. F. G. Taylor) asked the Colonial Secretary whether members of the present Government did not make and support state- ments in Warrington at the general election that white men could perform the work for which Chinese were imported into South Africa and how he explained the fact that although the Chinese had been reduced by 6,000 in two years,fewer white men were employed than before.</p>
        <p>Mr. ENGT,AND (Chancellor of the Exchequer), answer- ing in the absence of the Colonial Secretary, admitted that the statcments mentioned were made and said the Government were prepared to stand by them because they believed the white men could do the work equally well if not better than the yellow men. Up to date 30,000 Chinese had been sent home and they hoped that by the end of the year there would not be a Chinaman in South Africa. There were gow more of the King's subjects employed in South *riea than formerly, and they hoped that more white' men would be employed than at any previous time in the history of South Africa, when things regained their normal condition. Mr. TAYLOR : Will the right hon. gentleman explain what he means by King's subjects? Does he mean there are 32,000 more natives employed, although there are fewer white men? _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. ENGLAND said it was true that 32,000 natives were employed, but they were British subjects and it was the intention of the Government to see that every British subject was treated well end that work should be found for him in his native clime. As time went on more white men would be employed. Mr. TAYLOR remarked that the right hon. gentleman had begged the question and played with the House. The MEmucn FOR FALKIRK asked the Postmaster General whether he would take advice as to the legality or otherwise of delivering circulars in addressed envelopes without having first passed through the Post Office. . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. WYE Joss (Foreign Secretary), in the absence of the Postmaster General, replied that whilst the Government did not intend to interfere with personal liberty in the delivering of letters they would exercise their power to suppress any organized system designed to infringe the nation's monopoly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>HOME RULE DEBATE.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE DEBATE.</p>
        <p>Mr.J. F. AwnEßy(Secretary for Ireland) then intro- duced the Home Rule Bill,the main provisions of which appeared in last week's " Examiner.' Ile made a well- reasoned and effective speech which was punctuated with Nationalist cbeers. He said Ireland had been the political sepulchre of a great many men of talent and ability on both sides of the House, and the members would therefore recognize the difficult position in which he was placed. He did not apologise for intro- ducing the measure. "We on this side of the House," continued Mr. Awbery, " conscientiously, believe that a measure of this description should have adorned the Statute Book of this country a great many years ago. (Cheers.) When the hon. gentlemen opposite monopolised political power for the greater part of a quarter of a century, they were frank enough to tell us that Home Rule was dead. (Opposition "Bear, hears.") Home Rule can never die. (Nationalist cheers.) The death of Home Rule can only take place when you have exterminated the Irish race from the face of the earth." (Cheers and Councillor Toole : " That can never be.") Continuing, Mr. Awbery said he did not yield that the Irish were asking for a concession. He maintained they were asking for a right. There had been a remark- able growth of English opinion in favour of Home Rule, and that was e.idenced even in the highest quarters of the land. He contended that the extension of self- government would have a tendency to cement the loyalty of the Irish and instanced the happy effects which the granting of this measure to Canada had pro- duced. We had to-day 27 Home Rule institutions in touch with the Empire and it was an astounding thing that one of the oldest parts of the Empire still remained outside the pale of Oelf-government. The last 100 years of British Government in Ireland had been a failure unparalleled in the history of any civilized community. Mr. Awbery alluded to the decline of industrial pros- perity in Ireland, to the low standard of education, the exorbitant burden of taxation, and the curtailing of personal liberty. Civil Government in Ireland cost more than in any other kingdom in the world in propor- I tion to its size. He asked the other side to erradicate from their minds the prejudices with which they bad become steeped on this question. "I observe " said Mr. Awbery that my hop. friend the Member for Newton Division is in his place to-night. A prophet has no honour in his own country,' lam told, but I believe be is here for a given purpose and no one welcomes his presence More than I do." It was neces- sary that the stumbling block of Dublin Castle administration should be removed, because it had come between the nation and many an earnest and well- meaning Chief Secretary for Ireland. Mr. Awbery con. eluded with an appeal to the House not to plunge Ireland still further into the abyss of degradation but to place her once and for all amongst the self-govern- ing communities which represented this great and mighty Empire. (Cheers.) The MEMBER FOR KNCTSFORD DIVISION (Mr. Miles) seconded. We bad, he said, tried to rule Ireland according to English ideas and the result was a miserable failure, because we failed to grasp the fact that the Irish were different from ourselves in char- acter and temperament. The union was one on piper only. If the people of England knew one half of what had been done in their name in Ireland they would not delay Home Rule any longer. The MEMBER FOR NEWTON DIVISION (Mr. J. H. Bottomley) moved the rejection of the Bill. "The right hon. gentleman, who brought forward the Bill," he said,</p>
      </div>
