        <p>The MEMBER FOR DUBLIN HARBOUR (Mr. W. Kilduff) said the English rule in Ireland was the one dark spot in our history, the one exception to England's glorious traditions at home and abroad. Under the present system Irish labour was dumped on to the English market and helped to increase the unemployed and he maintained that if Ireland were governed under happier c.nditions that would not be the case. The MEMBER FOR LANCASTER (Mr. J. Howes, jun.) moved the adjonrnrnent of the debate and the House went into committee. Next week's meeting will be held on Thnrsday evening.</p>
        <p>ueu mat its being matter how small tie earnings,will assist in the mainten- ance of a home the income of which is barely sufficient to sustain life especially if that home is already bereft of tbe chief parent and breadwinner. I know the view is held that the needy circumstances of the home are not a matter for the consideration of the Education Com- mittee but rather of the Board of Guardians—an opinion I venture to think which would not find favour in this meeting to-day—meaning as it does the pauperising of the family in order that the child may be kept at school. I believe in such a question as this that the Committee should have full discretionary power to exercise their judgment.</p>
        <p>"Josh Billings" wrote, :—The best way to tell a good oyster from a bad one iz. to eat it. The man who kan't tell the difference in this way isn't worth convincing. Luv iz a difficult sensashun to define. About all we ken say about it iz its viktims feel phoolish and akt phoolish, too. I hay se-en men whom I thought waz striktly honest, but honesty iz so rare a quality that I should want large odds, even if I WA/ a-going to bet on miself. Thane isn't a more thanklese task in this world than trieing to help the improvident. Yung man, don't never put yure hand in.the lion's mouth. If yu happen to askape it, it only proves that the lion just at that tizne waz look- ing for a bigger job. I have seen men whom the only safety in deal- ing with was to implicitly trust. 1 would az soon think -ov pulling the tale feathers out ov a peakok az to interfere with the innosent vanity ov a man. Advice iz very cheap in market just now; the supply haz killed the demand. Flattery iz nothing more than lieing, and it iz the most abject kind, too. A man's bed iz his citadel, and hiz heart iz the worst enemy he haz to fear. A prude seems to me to be a coquette gone to seed.</p>
        <p>Men ov strong pashuns are less dangerous than men of weak ones; it iz the milksops that need the most watching. When a wize man undertakes to do a cunning thing he allmost allwuss makes a miserable failure or it.</p>
        <p>The only difference in the vanity ov men iz this—sum hal+ got more than others. Truth iz sed to be stran.ger than fickshun ; it iz to most pholks. Married, life iz a game in which the woman, if she iz called, iz allmost sure to have a strate flush.</p>
        <p>The best thing I know ov iz a rust rate wife; the next best thing is a second rate one.</p>
