      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Literary and Philosophical Society.</head>
        <p>Literary and Philosophical Society.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Literary and Philo- sophical Society was held at the Masonic Hall, on Monday evening. The President (Mr. A. J. Tolley) was in the chair, and there was a good attendance.— In the annual report the Hon. Secretary (Mr. R. Dobson) stated that the preseat session was the thirty- eighth of the society. There were 145 members, as compared with 147 last session.—The following officers were elected :—President, Mr. A. J. Jolley vice-president, Alderman Bennett ; hon. treasurer, Mr. W. Silcock ; hon. secretary, Mr. 0. Lofthouse ; mem- bers of the council, Mr.R. Dobson and Mr. Stuart Page. Mr. H. Stuart Page gave a paper on the famous War- rington poetess, Mrs. Barbauld, and submitted a most interesting sketch of her career. Summing up, Mr. Page said : She is acknowledged to have been one of the most attractive figures of her age, and one of the most elegant writers of whom England can boast. As Warringtonians we are proud to claim relationship with this distinguished woman. Though she left Warrington comparatively early in life we know that it always occupied a prominent place in her affections. It was here she spent her youth and the early years of her womanhood. It was here by our river side, in , our lanes mid fields, and amidst the classic surround- ings of our Academy that she first courted the Muses and her unfledged genius tried its first flight into the realms of song. Bound to our town by ties so numerous and so strong, we claim her as one of our own, and among the many famous names that have graced the literary annals of Warrington we must place her's first and crown her memory with the laurels of our admiration and affection. (Applause.) —ln the discussion which followed, the President, Alderman Bennett and Mr. C. Madeley took part, and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Page for his interesting paper.— The President said he would like to express the genuine regret of all the members of the society at the retirement of Mr. Dobson from the position of hon. secretary which he had held for several years. Mr. Dobson had not spared himself and he had been a model secretary. They were all sorry to lose him. He moved a special vote of thanks to him for his past services. Alderman Bennett seconded. and the resolution was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
