      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <p>The first day's discussion on the Scottish Small Holdings</p>
        <p>THE LORDS'</p>
        <p>FIRST MOVE, Bill could leave no doubt in</p>
        <p>any fairly acute mind as to what the House of Lords meant to do with that measure. There was an evident determination that it should not even get so far as a second reading. What happened on Wednesday, therefore, was merely the realization of expectations. The Bill has been thrown out with something approaching I contumely. The Lords are obviously determined to fight this question to the bitter end. Had a second reading been given to the Bill, some means of accommodation might, have been found. In. Committee, especially if it had been a committee of both Houses, such amendments might have been introduced as would have been satisfactory to Lords and Commons, taking it for granted, of course, that the Lords were prepared to be satisfied</p>
        <p>However, all opportunity of compromise has. been ruthlessly cut away. In their own House, the Lords have the strength of a giant, and they have used it as might a giant who is at once blind and deaf, and defiant of all sorts of persuasion Their lordships, it should be carefully noted, pre- tend to be in favour of the main object which this unlucky Bill was designed to promote. The amend- ment, which was moved by the Duke of Montrose, declares that the House of Lords is desirous of promoting on sound economic principles the eaten-</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>sion of small agricultural holdings in Scotland, and of considering such amendments of the Crofters Acts as are shown to be required. Those who choose to believe in the sincerity of this declara- tion are at liberty to do so. We regret that we are not able to include ourselves among the number. " Sound economic principles" is a phrase that may mean anything. As employed in the amendment of the Duke of Montrose, it means in fact nothing whatever, except that the vastly pre- ponderating majority in the House of Lirds is determined to regard as sound or economic no legislative proposals which are not in harmony with its own views, and clearly innocuous to its own private and personal interests.</p>
        <p>The Peers profess to be acting in the real interests of Scotland, and according to the genuine desires of the Sottish people. This is a pretension which is easily put to the test. The Bill which ' was thrown out on a second occasion on Wednes- day is generally supported by all but a handful of the members for Scotland. Since the rejection of the Bill by the House of Lords last year, there has been ample time and opportunity for reconsideration. If the Scotch Liberal mem- bers had been proceeding in opposition to the opinion of their constituents, they would have found it out during ;the Recess. As a matter of fact, they came back to Westminster more than , ever determined on the Bill. On the other hand, the son of the Duke of Montrose, the leader of the House of Lords on this occasion, was defeated at the General Election by between 3,000 and 4,000 votes. Lord DALMENT, the son of the Earl of Rosebery, another opponent of the Bill, has received the sharpest possible reminders from his constituents that if he opposed the Bill he mast not expect to retain his seat. "Are these the garments of thy son ?" a great warrior demanded of the Pope, holding up the bloodstained armour of a bishop, the sparing of whose life was required on the ground that he was a priest. Is the overwhelming defeat of the son of the Duke of Montrose, is the exhortation addressed to Lord DALMENY by those who sent him to Parliament, proof that the Lords under- stood the mind of Scotland better —than the elected representatives of that country FLEThe question is one that certainly need not be pressed. The Lord; have :acted recklessly, irresponsibly, fatuously in defence of a system which is depopu- lating the rural districts of Scotland, whilst professing to be in favour of the same objects as the Government, thus storing up wrath against the day of wrath.</p>
        <p>When Mr. A. H. orosfi3ld THE BOROUGH M.P. first addressed the</p>
        <p>• MEMBER. -House of Commons last ~TMEfj elfin June the effort was quite properly described as his " maiden " speech. Oa Thursday, Mr. Crosfield delivered what may, perhaps not unfittingly, be described as his " married "speech. The one seems only a natural sequel to the other, and moreover the speaker himself has been married since his " maiden " effort in the House. Warrington people will be specially grateful to the Member for being in his place on Thursday evening, as the Bill under dis- cussion was of considerable importance to the old, town. The Liverpool Corporation, as por- motets of the Bill, desire to have Warrington's rights to a supply of Vyrwny water annulled,—a proposal strenuously resisted by the Warrington Corporation and ably opposed in the House of Commons by Mr. Crosfield. It is not often a Member of Parliament has the chance of espousing directly the specific interests of the constituency he represents, and the opportunity on Thursday would, we are certain,affcrd Mr. Crosfield particalar pleasure. ,</p>
        <p>Temperance work in War- THE CITIZENS' riogton has too long TEMPERANCE remained undone for the LEAGUE. want of an organisation _ _ .</p>
        <p>which could direct the undoubtedly strong temperance sentiment into the proper channel. At one time the Temperance Federation did good work in the town, but for I some years it has been but a memory and a regret. The re-habilitation of the Federation was not desired, but the organisation of some united temperance society on a broad boa s was an urgent requirement, and we are glad to state that it has at last been consummated in the form of the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League which sprang into being at a very enthusiastic meeting held on Monday evening. The .League is founded on a basis so broad that .we have the highest hopes ot its permanency. Any man or woman who has temperance reform at besrt may join. He or she need not be Church,Dissent, or even Christian ; need not be even teetotal. It was said by one speaker that a publican would be welcomed were his objects bona-fide. The work of temperance should not necessarily be left to the total abstainers ; there are otkers with an equally earnest desire to see the p:)wer of the Trade lessened, and the sobriety of the people increased. True citizenship embodies temperance, and we hope there will be a strong rally to the Citizen's League, particulars of which will he found in another column.</p>
        <p>Both in point of attendance THE LIBERAL and enthusiasm the annual AssociaTioN. meeting of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Liberal Association on Mon- day evening afforded much encouragement to workers in the progressive cause. The room was well filled and the tone of the meeting was such as to leave no doubt of the determination of Warring- ton Liberals to back up the Government in the great work of reform to which they have set their hands. The President in his address wisely laid stress on the fact that a testing time for Liberalism is at hand, for when the furthering of the general interest clashes with the power of monopolies, selfish opposition is certain to be aroused. It behoves Liberals to stand firm when such vital issues are at sake and to take their part in dispelling the misr 3presentations which are being so widely circulated. We are glad that the meeting unanimously passed a resolution in support of the Licensing Bill and that there is a prospect of a big demonstration being held to counteract the effects of the" trade" propaganda. The Government measure is one that must com- mend itself to all who are concerned for temperance reform and the uplifting of the people and we are sure the great body of opinion- in favour of the Bill in Warrington will manifest itself in no uncertain way. The re-election of Mr. C. E. Parker as Presieent of the Association will give general satisfaction for he has •spared no efforts to advance Liberalism in the district, while his able advocacy of Free Trade on the platform and through the Press has been of the greatest value to the cause.</p>
        <p>It is no mean achievement WORIUNGMEN on the part of the Working- AND TEE men's Committee to have INriamARY. raised £l,OOB for the</p>
        <p>ing to the deduction of a small sum weekly from their wages had increased their annual contribution from 14s. to £2O. The system adopted is for employees to subscribe id. per week if they earn over £1 and 2d. if they earn over £2 and the amount is deducted in the office. Several delegates pointed out that a similar course is already followed in several works but there can be no doubt that if it were in operation everywhere the result would be a very appreciable increase of the subscriptions. The attendance at S4turday's meeting was one of the largest we have seen and, I given industrial prosperity, another successful year may be safely anticipated.</p>
        <p>The tune has come to decide PUBLIC OR whether the public or the BREWER ? brewer shall control the Liquor LAWS. Mr, Balfour handed over control to the brewers. Mr. Asquith seeks to restore it to the people, and his Licensing Bill has consequently stirred the liquor monopoly to its deepest and foulest depths. Every kind of argument—scriptural, blasphemous, and unscrupu- lous—is being used by rile Trade to mislead the people and bolster up the ystein whica has given the brewer a vested interest in the licence, ad put shackles on all agencies for reform whether social, municipal; or relieions. It has been said that " The Church's one foundation—is the beer barrel." This was a cruel jibe ever; at the Estab- lished Church, which has probably more privilege than the liquor trade, but it was not without truth. To-day the Church is being threatened by the Trade with the withdrawal of support if it dares to take its stand for the people, for justice, and for morality. And it cannot be doubted that thcse fat subscriptions from the brewery have their weight at a time like this. The public is being threatened with dearer beer in order to inflame the unthinking and thirsty section of the electorate against the Government Bill, while in . one instance a deliberate boycott of honest trades- men is openly advocated by a spokesman for the Trade. At the Liquor Demonstration in Queen's Elall, London, on Monday, Mr. Harold Smith (known to fame solely as the brother of Mr. F. E. Smith, M.P.) asked every man arid woman in the hall "to pay not a farthing to any tradesman who Was in favour of the measure" (the Licensing Bill). These are the tactics to be pursued by the Trade in order to maintain its dominion over the people. And by poster after poster the people are actually asked to help. The colossal efirontery of it all I Luckily there is a sub-stratum of commousense in the minds of the bulk of the people which should enable them to see that the Government Bill is founded on principles of justice, while the appeal of the Trade is purely selfish and mercenary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Infirmary in the past twelve months and congratulation was naturally the dominant note at the annual meeting on Satur- day. The result is all the more creditable when it is realized that the extension fund has made additional dernads upon the workers, who have most loyally supported the effort. Since its inauguration the committee has been the means of obtaining £16,334 Os. id. for the institution —a handsome record indeed. While the past year's work was generally regarded as satis- factory, there was a feeling that much more might be done by the adoption of a uniform scheme at the works. Mr. Harry Fair- dough instanced how his own workmen by consent-</p>
        <p>At a local meeting this week the hymn "Stand up, stand up, for Jesus' was given out with the request that the congregation would remain seated whilst it was sung. „ * *</p>
        <p>" Your beer will cost you- more," says the brews', with his tongue in his cheek. Our's won't at any rate, and we should not mind if some other people's cost them 6d. a glass. They would then drink a more moderate quantity.</p>
        <p>* * * There are only three millionaires announced to speak on "confiscation, robbery, Mid ruin " at the " monstre "(should be "monstrous ") Trade defence meeting in Parr Hall. The way out of their diffi- culties is forecasted in " dearer beer." Dividends must be kept up somehow ! * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Horatio Bottomley- is publishing .the list of clerical shareholders in Brewery Companies in his weekly paper " John Bull." He has found consider- ably over a thousand clerics with money invested in the liquor trade, and his first list of about 150 names occupies a full page headed " The Parson and the Pub." No wonder the Trade can usually count upon the Church as an ally. * * * All was enthusiasm and harmony at the annual meeting of the Liberal Association on Monday. Most of the officers were re-elected, with the exception of a change in the treasureship. Mr. W. Furness resigned the office after ten years valued service, and Mr. T. Pemberton was appointed in his stead. No better choice could have been made. Mr. Pemberton has the reputation of being an ideal treasurer in other directions. * * *</p>
        <p>"Your beer is sure to co;t, you more." This is what the brewers are telling the people of Warrington by placard. They ought to know, certainly, if they have already decided to raise the price of beer, and from apparent indications they are ready to jump at the opportunity of extracting another copper per pint from the workingman. But why, NI ith all their candour, they should break forth into doggerel we do not know.. The "dear beer" placard concludes as follows : "How great your dread of clearer bread! Have you no fear of dearer beer ? " * * * Mr. H. I. Wynne Jones—a director of Wilderspooi brewery —has a grievance. He has discovered that he is not the only pebble on the beach. Another Wynne Jones —Mr. " E -has dared to write to the press in support of the Licensing Bill. In consequence Mr. " H. I's" soul is filled with fear lost Ihrough a sad case of mistaken identity Sir Gilbert should "kick him straight out of the Brewery." Mr. "R.I. " does not know Mr." E " and does not want to. Possibly the latter feels equally unsociable. We should regret exceedingly to see anything in the nature of a family disagreement arise from so trivial a matter.</p>
        <p>* * Picturesque language was employed by Mr. Rowland Hunt, M.P., in a debate with Mr. Francis Neilson Colonel Kenyon Slaney's opponent in Newport, who is well known in Warrington, on Tariff Reform versus Free Trade. Mr. Hunt claimed that Protection was the only solution for lack of employment, and asked what" the blind, blue blazes " was the use of our continuing the present unfair fiscal system, which taxed our own people for the English market and for the upkeep of foreign markets. Mr. Neilson said in the statement of the Tariff Reformers that by taxing foreigners they would contribute to our taxes. There was never a more colossal lie told our people. * • * Numerous public-houses on the outskirts of towns will be, we believe, badly hit by the extension of the bona fide traveller limit from three to six miles. Under Mr. Balfour's convenient Act they were kept busy on Sunday by " travellers" who had walked or cycled or driven three miles in order to work up a, thirst worth quenching. It was quite a Sunday recreation for hundreds of men to cover the three mile limit and then drink more than was good for them. The Act thus encouraged the worst class of drinking—i.e. drinking for dnnking's sake. With six miles to traverse the "traveller " will be rarer and the public-house quieter on Sunday. * * • The newly-formed Citizens' Temperance League has plenty of work before it. To begin with, the mendacious statements which the Trade is plastering all over the hoardings require answering, or the man in the street may be misled. He ought to reflect that it is not his interests and welfare the Trade is con- cerned about but its own. Some of the statements , will then appear in a different light. The work of the League was really commenced before its birth for the , opposition to the licence transfer applied for last • week is one of the things which such an organisation • should hold itself responsible for. Then there are municipal matters requiring attention. Future Trade nominees for public office will have a new and effective force to reckon with in the Citizens' League. , *s* The local poster headed " The Public House s' Public Need " is a production remarkable for the profound way in which it brings religion and morality to the aid of the drink-seller. For instance " The Sabbath is made for man and not man for the Sabbath " (it is not made for him to desecrate with drunkenness) ; "personal liberty is the principle article 'of faith in an Englishman's political creed. To dispossess him of it is to deprive him of his manhood' (how much manhood has he left when he has drunk all the publican dare supply him with ?) ; " whenever freedom of action is reduced moral power is reduced at the same time" (fancy the Trade concerned With its customers' moral power !) All this is about the restriction of hours for Sunday drink-selling. There ' is not a word about the poor publican and his helpers who have to slave seven days a week through the rapacity of the brewers. NVhat about the publican's " personal liberty," his " manhood," and his " moral power ?' "</p>
        <p>0111110</p>
