      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <p>LOCAL NEWS. Coming Events. In aid of the Railway Benevolent Institution a grand concert will be held in the Parr Hall this (Saturday) evening. The artistes will be Madame Agnes Croaton (soprano), Miss Winifred Ditchburn (contralto), Mr. John Draper (tenor), Mr. G. H. Ditchburn (bass), Mr.J. H.Ditchburn (bass), Mr. John Phillips (humorist), and Mr. P. M. Darby, .‘tus. Bac. (accompanist). At the meeting of the Junior Liberal Association to be held on Monday evening Mr. Darbyshire will introduce a discussion on " Current Events."</p>
        <p>After the proceedings in connection with the open- ing of the Infirmary extension next Thursday Lord Cross will visit Stockton Heath and plant a tree in Victoria Square. Lady Greenall will also plant a tree.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of Infirmary governors to be held at the Town Hall on Thursday, the 26th inst., the business will include the appointment of an honorary dental surgeon. The annual show of bulbs in connection with the School Children's Branch of the Beautiful Warrington Society will be held in the Co-operative Hall on Saturday next. Infirmary Ball. The Hon. Treasurer of the Infirmary begs to acknowledge with thanks the receipt of £B7 from Captain George Crosfield, hon. sec. to the Ball Com- tnittee, being proceeds of the ball held on the 22nd January. boted Quaker's Death. Mr. Edward Worsdell, one of the ablest and foremost members of the Society of Friends, died on Wednesday at Darlington, where he was seized with tis,_sies. while addressing a gathering a few days ago. rlrei Woradell was born at Warrington, 36 years ago, fourZ the only son of Mr. George Worsdell, the Zesidi,er of the Dallam Forge, Warrington, now Ira 4t Lancaster, and grandson of the first rail- Y carriage builder in Great Britain. PREMIER GUINEAS will purchase a fully guaranteed —RMIER CYCLE. Inspection invited.—J. S. GAR- ., &amp; 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. me at a Sub-Post Office. In the early bolus of Wednesday morning a fire poke out at the shop and sub-post office of Mr. clovard Rudge, Lovely-lane. Fortunately the occu • 15atda were aroused and quickly made their escape without injury. The Fire Brigade, under Superin- leadent Turner, was quickly on the scene, but the liames had gained such a firm hold that the shop was completely gutted and great damage was done to thel /rout upstairs room, which was used as a printing' °Moe. Exciting beetles were witnessed, neighboursl assisting in removing the furniture. The damage atnounting to several hundred pounds, is partly ' covered by insurance. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST - BOOTS IN LAN'CASIIIRE.—Advt. Welcome to the Rev. R. E. and Mrs. Letheren. v. A social to extend a welcome to the Rev. R. E. and Letheren was held at Golborne-street Baptist _ Ichool on Thursday evening, when there was a large Rttendance. Speeches of welcome were delivered by 111,r. Herbert Buckby, secretary of the church, Mr. S. kettl, leY, superintendent of the Sunday school, Mr. ti'r, secretary of the Band of Hope, Mr. Pepper, eecretary of the Bible class, Mr. E. Wynne-Joues, tlrbsasurer of the Church, Mr. T. Davies, secretary of Yboung Men's Mutual Improvement Society, and thai,V. Few who presided. Mr. Letheren returned woZde and remarked that if they went forward they all. „Prosper. They must throw the Church open to pe, 'le would fill the pulpit ir they would fill the ..8. During the evening Miss Mulkey arid a lifinartette party gave several solos for which they were :a1t1137 encored. ‘4%d his sister and aunts as he kissed 'em, what's kept the 'fin' germs from your system ?" „.Isuth to tell."</p>
        <p>• - repL,u lie, sole reason I'm free, that takin 'Life Tonic,' I've missed 'em ! " ror, -</p>
        <p>__•°alU, M. ARTON, NVarrington.—Advt. ueath of Colonel Pilkington. Many of the inhabitants of this district will deeply gret to hear of the death of Colonel Richard C. 13., Rainford Hall, St. Helens, ex- Member for Newton Division, and one of the principal ltembers of the glass-making firm of Messrs. l'ilkington Bros., St. Helens. The deceased gentle- ban, who was 67 year of age, had had a very active 'and useful career, having been Mayor of St. Helens on loUr occasions,and was one of the strongest supporters (4 the Volunteer movement in the town. Up to 1885 'Colonel Pilkington was•a strong supporter of Mr. Glad- but he ultimately threw in his lot with the Liberal 11* unionists. He represented .the Newton Division from to 1900 when he was defeated by Mr. J. A. '3eddon (Labour) by a majority of 541. He had been lzfr 6 rm • 'll.llg to contest the seat again at the next election t44d.- bis death has come as a great blow to his 4PPortera. 11r0l'INSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Ilthial troubles. Old. &amp; Of Chemists.—Advt ratlesmen and Electricity Charges. At .A.BB ,a meeting of the Warrington Tradesmen's V,' • I.,„aetation on Tuesday evening, the principal subject • to"rr consideration was the refusal of the Corporation -ti aeede to the AssociatiAt's application for a reduc ft?:trofthe charges for electric light. Mr. J. E toorson (president) said the position in Warring • ,e would seem to be that those ratepayers who used iei nos, . ty as an illuminant were paying through the ror the current, whereas those using it for motive tialj,rBca would appear to have had the same at a price tak:-7 covering the cost of production. If the under- iCould not he worked on more profitable lines a --f,„ r Present conditions, the only way to bring about •cot:re equitable charge would be to raise the price to ~ ex,araers for motive power.—Several other speakers Tir %Bed indignation at the charges for lightinff. Vith "atter was referred w the Executive Committee instructions tq continue to press for a reduction.</p>
        <p>Let others sing of Prince and King, For mine's a theme that's greater; that great, that wondrous thing, Cough's Exterminator ! No victor in triumphal oar Shows conquests half as sure, ror greater far the triumphs are Of Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
