Crown Vaults, Mr. J. E. Hannan was beard to advantage in , Heath-road ; and the Millstone Inn, Mill-street.—Five " Roses." Mr. W. Clay was warmly applauded for an ' of these houses belong to Messrs. Greenall, Whitley. effective interpretation of " Thy sentinel am I." The and Co., Wilderspool Brewery, and Mr. Lake, who duet " The spider and the fly " by Miss M. Houghton appeared for the firm, did not object to the Ferry and Mr. W. Clay proved highly popular, and the Hotel, the Cornwall and Devonshire, Rose and Shamrock,te t at: ieCrown d Millstoner n Lake objected err T ed. n quartette "In this hour of softened splendour" by regard Misses M. Houghton, E. Parr, and Messrs. J. Corrin and, after hearing the evidence, the Chairman said and W. Clay was another pleasing feature. Mr. L. ; Messrs. Greenall had met the Bench so reasonably in Ford gave two concertina solos in very creditable the other cases, that they had unanimously decided to style. Mr. Lyndon Wyatt was the humcrist and his renew this licence.—ln regard to " The Albion" in sketches created great merriment. Peel-street, belonging to Messrs. Peter Walker and Co., i Mr. J. W. Knowles of Widnes, objected to the refer- ence.—Superintendent Ennion said within a radius of . 400 yards there were eighteen fully-licensed houses, and . fifteen beer on-licences,--In answer to Mr. Knowles, ; witness said the house was well conducted.—Mr. , Knowles said this could not be considered a redundant : house, as it was doing an average of 112 barrels a year. ,It had been in one family for twenty years.—The licence was referred.</p>
        <p>CRAY,</p>
        <p>The scenery and mounting of the play were bo t artistic and pleasing. Of the incidental pl3.ntatio glee-singing in the third act, we cannot say much. It was not that the voices were not good enough, but rather that the vocal parts were so badly arranged i . The piece which has been presented during the and had so little of harmony in them. ' week at the Royal Court Theatre is a drama of the NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. domestic order by Mr. J. A. Campbell which appeals On Monday and during the week, Messrs. W. H more to the pit and gallery than to patrons of the Savery and A. T. Pigott's powerful Company will pre- stalls. Although much of the incident is of the con- -1 sent the great Adelphi drama " The English Rose" by George R. Sims and Robert Buchanan, and specially ventional order which forms the basis of the popul.tr ' novelette of fiction there is plenty of motive which revised and re-written by George R. Sims. ` The i touches the sympathies of a popular audience. The English Rose" is no ordinary drama, but a thoroughly story hangs together in but a loose way, vet it 'high-class one and the fact that it is sent out from Mr. H. Benson's Shakesperean Company's offices is situations that the desire to see the play through I ,is kept moving with such a succession of strong 'F. sufficient guarantee that it will be done well. The I to its logical and too self evident conclusion play abounds in exciting incident, with plenty of broad is undeniable. But much of the acting is humour united, and yet so natural and beautiful is the needlessly overdone, and the whole play would go development of the story,that the audiences are said to be quite carried away and to almost forget it a play , better and more naturally if greater restraint were ' shewn by many of those taking part in it. Curiously they are watching. The Company, whiq.i is of enough the parts of the villain of the piece, Lord unusual excellence, includes Miss Violet Farebrother, Howard Kenilworth, and his colleague, Patricia an accJmplished actress, one of her greatest successes ' b I Bathurst, in the hands of Mr. Frank Liston and Miss being the title role of " Candida," while Mr. Oswald Ruby Loncraine, were least open to this objection. Cray, who is in the leading rale, has played principal , p 1 The characters of the heroine and the hero, Dorothy parts in some of the most successful productions on Robin and Andrew Ross, were in many ways well Mr. Guy the road, his grnatest success prior to his present portrayed by Miss Maud Warburton and I . engagement being the' Silver King" in "The Silver . King." The remainder of the cast is in capable ' Hastinbs, while some minor impersonations such as those of the two footmen were at times so true hands and altogether the piece provides an excellent night's amusement. ,to life as to be irresistibly laughter-provoking.</p>
