      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Old W)11-i:at Home."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Old W)11-i:at Home."</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN ENGINEER'S HEROISM.</p>
        <p>We learn that the Mr. Oubridge who formed a member of the first rescue party to descend the colliery at Hamstead is the third son of Mr. J. A. Oubridge, of IVargrave-road, Earlestown. He and a colleague were brought out of the burning pit in a fainting con- dition. John Oubridge served his apprenticeship at the engineering works of Messrs. T. and T. Vicars, Earlestown, and afterwards was employed as surface engineer at the Wood Pit, Haydock, of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Limited. His Earlestown acquaintanc e speak of him as being always a man of great courage EAREESTOWN EISTEDDFOD.</p>
        <p>The annual Earlestown Eisteddfod was held in Earles- towo on Saturday afternoon and evening, and was a great success. The afternoon proceedings were pre- sided over by Mr. W. Speakman of Newton-le-Willows. and the chair at the evening meeting by Mr. J. Jones,of Liverpool. Tne conductor throughout was the ttev. Wynn Davies. The musical adjudicator was Mr. T. Price and the other adjudicators were :—Elocution, the Revs. Wynn Davies and S. C. hall ; translations, ttev. Wynn Davies ; ar,cl art, Mrs. Chandley, Mrs. Valentine, Mr. J. P. Walker:, and Mr. Ben Davies, Warrington. Miss Profit, of Earlestown, acted as accompanist. The hall was fairly well filled. At the afternoon meeting. . . . .</p>
        <p>! In th; ladies' challenge solo competition (own choice) Miss Lily Fairhurst, of Warrington, was awarded the !prize (a gold watch). She sang " A summer night,"and the judge, in commenting upon her singing, said she had a capital voice, and she sang beautifully. All was artistically done, end he thought she ought to go in for something butter. She richly deserved the prize. In the choral competition for mixed voices (23 to 35 in number), the piece was " Ar don o then gwyntoedd ' " I was tossed by the winds " (Parry). There was only one entry, the Earlestown Glee Society (conductor M.r. H. .1. Hughes). The prize was £5 ss. and a silver rose bowl to the conductor.</p>
        <p>The competitions for children's choirs attracted three entries, viz., the Sutton United Juvenile Choir (con- ductor, Mr. W. Enapper), Prescot Co-operative Juvenile Choir (conductor, Mr. D. J. Williams) and Warrington Secondary School Choir (conductor, Mr. It. H. Albery). The piece was Mis 7 Blorlan," " The month of May." (Parry.) In announcing his decision, the adjudicator said all three choirs sang excellently. rhe Sntton choir had a beautiful tone, and the enunciation was almost perfect. In the Secondary School choir, the singing of the boy soprano was very rich. The voices in the Prescot choir were not so rich, and the enunciation not so perfect as in the other two choirs. He awarded the prize, which consisted of £4 45., and a gold centre medal to the conductor, to the Sutton choir. Pianofore solo, " Playfulness " (Lang) for juniors under 12 years of age : I, Dorothy Bonne, Sutton ; 2, Emily Whalley, Runcorn. Best dressed doll : I, Hilda Owen. Earlestown. Solo " Springtime is returning " for boys under 15 years : 1, Nlalcohn Bennett, Liverpool ; 2, lloweW INilliams, Earlestown. . _ _</p>
        <p>Violin 'solo, for juniors under 16 years, " Chant de Soir " (Ambrose) : 1, Harry Cope, Widnes ;2, William Swain, Warrington. Translation, English to Welsh : 1, Mr. T. Morris, of Bi rkenhead.</p>
        <p>Competition for best pillow cases (open to children attending the elementary schools in the area of Newton- n-Maker(ield) : 1, Alice Nlartindale ; 2, Ida Rawlins. Both attend the Manor School, Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The evening proceedings we're presided over by Mr. J. Jones, Liverpool. The first item was a pianoforte solo, " Celia's lullaby," for which there were two com- petitors. The playing of the first-named was rather fast and too loud. The winner, Mr. A. R. Tracey, Aintree, Liverpool, kept good time, has a delicate and artistic touch, and well deserved the prize, a silver</p>
        <p>_ cup. The second item was a translation from Welsh to English—a difficult task. The winners, Mr. Aristides, was awarded the prize of 55. The third item was a solo for girls under 15, and entitled " Megan's daughter," and the prize was won by Miss Ethel Redhead, Liverpool, who has a well- trained voice. The second prize fell to Miss Margery Williams, Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>The prize for the open solo, "Harps of gold." was I won by Miss Margery Williams, Newton-le-Willows. The gold centre medal prize for the English recita- tion "Brutus's speech," was won by Miss Lizzie Fielding I Failsworth, who spoke eloquently, and thoroughly understood the passion and poetry of the piece. In the double quartette," Rock of Ages," the victors ' were Mr. Woodcock's Party from Warrington, who sang exceedingly well and kept splendid. In the gentlemen's challenge solo competition, the winner was Mr. Watkins Hughes, Rhos, who sang " Deeper and deeper still " ; while for the best paint- ing spray of roses, the first prize was awarded to Miss Annie Duckworth and the second to Miss Ada Downham. For the chief choral competition three choirs entered, namely, Sefton Park Welsh Glee Singers, Mr. D. H. Williams, conductor ; Oldham Harmonic Society, Mr. H. Hannan, conductor ; and the Stockton Heath Wesleyan Choir, Mr. T. E. Penketh conductor. The judge's remarks were as follows The Seften Park Choir opened rather fast. Very brilliant sopranos, altos weak, tenors good, bass very good, but rather held their words. Tempos of sopranos excellent. Oldham hartnoic " opened rather fast, bass and altos good, quartette good, chorus not very good. Trio rather fast, but voices blended beautifully and [ harmoniously." Stockton Heath "opened splendidly, quartette and chorus very good, trio very good. Grave movement vary effective and brilliant, awarded the , prize, .£9 Ps. and silver cup to the conductor ; also gold I centre medal for the best soloist, Mr. Herbert Plumpton, Warrington. The best drawing of the Earlestown Town Hall was I an excellent production in black and white, the shading havin-, been done very well. The winner was Miss Ethel Duckworth, I A vote of thanks was passed by the Rev. Wynn Davies to Mr. Jones for presiding, and the singing of 1" Land of my fathers " and " God save the King," brought the proceedings to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>TOWN CLERK OF WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE TOWN CLERK OF WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. S. Oppenlaciim, solicitor, Liverpool and St. Helens, and Town Clerk of Widnes, was on Wednesday last called to the Livery of the Worshipful Company of Cordwainers, London. Mr. Oppenheim was admitted a member of the company and to the freedom of the city of London on the 14th of March, 1903.</p>
        <p>. • - . . .</p>
        <p>The hoardings are being extensively utilised just now to further the interests of the brewing monopoly. The Licensing Bill which has been introduced into Parliament is not a "sop " to the Brewers and they, having become so accustomed to free gifts, very naturally object. There is a growing feeling through- out the country that monopolies should not be owned by private individuals but that the nation —the people—should own them. The battle has begun ; and the millionaire brewers and their parasites are putting on their armour. They are not going to fight themselves ; in fact they are not fighting for them- selves (?) ; they are waging war on behalf of the workingman and his beer (?); and they threaten him that, unless he fights for the brewer the brewer will increase the price of his beer. How nice ! And how one-sided the battle !! It is a case of "Heads I win, tails you lose." ! ! !</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>The Spring Poet is at work in Warrington. " Have you no fear of dearer Beer? " You must have,seen that rythmic question on the hoardings. It is not for me to ask who paid for the printing and posting of those particular ; bills doubt- less they were not issued at the expense of the workingman. Probably they were paid for out of the funds of the " TM.de." It does not matter, however. They are there and need answer- ing. What are you going to say? Is beer not dear enough already? When one con- siders the price the people have to, pay for it in loss of life and happiness, the price is abnormally high. If the Brewers, and those others who are directly or indirectly associated with them in the sale of Beer, will give us some undertaking that " dearer beer " will mean less poverty, less misery, less unhappy homes, less broken marriage vows, less lunacy, less political jobbery, then we shall have " no fear of dearer beer."</p>
        <p>* * * *</p>
        <p>We have a belief that it is the rich, and not the poor, who have most t.) gain out of the liquor traffic. The rich are supposed to be able, by education and public opinion, to restrain themselves from those excesses into which the poor are tempted by drink. And the rich are spared the bitter penalties which drink inflicts upon the poor. It is not necessary to enlarge upon the revolting cases which are presented to our notice, altnast daily, of the appalling effects of the almost unrestricted sale of alcoholic drinks. And yet those highly educated persons, posing as philan- thropists, and well-wishers of the poor widows and orphans, have the audacity to ask that the list of unhappy victims should continue, and that no restric- tions should be placed upon the sale of their pernicious and soul-destroying "beverage." If you don't allow yourselves and your children to be con- tinually tempted to excess by the glamour .of their aleshops' then you must expect to be boycotted. One public speaker on behalf of this great and glorious soul-uplifting trade has gone so far as to advise as follows : " Now I advise you not to pay one farthing to any tradesman who is in favour of this (Licensing) Mind that remark was made, not by a poor ignorant individual, but by a gentleman attached to the great ' political party, the party that has the monopoly of chivalry and pleads for the Bible t-) be retained in the day schools, —probably because it can all the easier play with the devil outside.</p>
        <p>And we are reminded by one local speaker that the present Licensing Bill is "Socialism to the very bottom." Of course, it is. All good bills are. That is why some folks object. They want bad hills so that they can continue to make themselves richer at the expense of others And then we are told that it is Robbe, y and Con- fiscation. Rubbish! How can it be robbery to take something which already belong; to you? How much did the License Holder pay for the privilege of selling intoxicating liquors? And again, the Brewers are concerned because it would mean turning many thousands out of employ- ment. And again I say "Rubbish" Is it not a fact that the trade employs a far smaller number of persons in proportion to capital than any other trade?</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>Now there are 97,554 licensed premises in England and Wales. Oct of that number there are 27,097 ante- -18f59 beer-houses, the vast majority of which do not employ anyone. The houses being kept by the licensee and his wife the husband often follows another employment by day. Mr. C. Pinhorn of the National Temperance Council, in his recent investigations in Bedford found that out of 120 licenses in Bedford 35 of the licensees followed other employments. " T known cases where the men were 'bus drivers, smiths, shoe makers, porters—and in one case an engine driver. These men kept a little beer-house to which their wives attended by day. I have been making observations in London for nearly forty years ; and I know there are numbers of these beer-houses in back streets at which no one is employed beyond the licensee. Now, if 32,000 licences are suppressed in fourteen years, and these mainly consist of the 27,000 ante-1869 beer-houses, the number to be closed at which persons are employed is reduced to about 5,000 or 6,000—spread over fourteen years !” * *</p>
        <p>There are more errors of fact,and more inaccuracies of judgment, in this attack by the Brewers on the rights of the people than we expected. The trade in strong drink as at present conducted, is the great social disturber. Crime, pauperism, insanity, disease, death, follow its development It creates the necessity for taxation and public burdens, while those profiting by it bear no adequate proportion of those burdens. Corruption, political and social, gather round the public-house as a centre ; and whilst there are some properly conducted, yet it is necessary that instead of being in the minority they thould 133 in the majority ; and we welcome any measure which shall make it hard to do wrong and easy to do right. The la w of self-preservation is equally applicable to society as to the individual. • Society has more control over any- thing that society manages. * *</p>
        <p>Viewing all these facts, and taking into account the advantages which would accrue from a more carefully conducted and better devised scheme of recreation for the people, we have no hesitation in saying that those employees could find by the help of the State, a more congenial employment than that in which they are at present engaged. Socialists are condemned by the monopolists. The reason is not far to seek. " John Bull ' tells us that there are at present about 6'0,000 married persons whose husbands or wives are in lunatic asylums; about 7,003 married persons are annually separated by judicial orders ; and about 40,000 illegitimate children are annually born in Eng- land and Wales. A pretty picture, in a Christian country, isn't it ? That happens under the present competitive system and Socialists are denounced for wishing to change it. The Brewers have n'it fared badly, judging by the millionaires who are next week to address the work- ingmen of Warrington. Perhaps they will tell us whether they themselves would like to spend their evenings in some of the public-houses and beer-shops and take their wives and children with them. Work- ingmen must insist upon the best possible form of recreation ; and my contention is that they • have not got it, and that people who have made millions put of their establishments are not t be considered altogether as philantropists in the matter. The sooner the drink traffic is under public control the better. And hence we believe that the nation should own the Trusts.</p>
        <p>JOHN WORKVAN.</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, 1 for Coughs and Colds never fails. 1/li, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD, M.P. SPEAKS IN THE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD, M.P. SPEAKS IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>REPRESENTING WARRINGTON'S INTERESTS.</p>
