when an agreement had STOLEN HANDKERCHIEFS. been arrived at between important public William Taylor, aged thirteen, of Thatto Heath was authorities, and sanctioned by Parliament, it sent magistrates reformatory ofornma_toryfor flveyesatrs by the handkerchiefs theliHrceieenfss should not be open to one of those authorities to ask frogal.s a pawnshop.trat It was stated that in January last for its rescission. As to what the hon. Member for the boy had been put under the new Probation Offenders Everton had said as to this being a matter for Act for Vireo years for stealing tram-tickets. committee, he felt that it was of such very deep and , vital importance to Warrington, that he ought to enter a protest on the second reading. But an appeal had ! been made by the Chairman of Ways and Means that ! he should not press the matter further at present, but i Death of a Local Priest. should agree to its being considered upstairs in Roman Catholics in Warrington and district have committee. He was very happy to agree to the heard with deep regret of the death of Father Byrne suggestion of the bon. member for Evertou without in who had charge of Si. Lewis's Catholic Church, Croft. any way withdrawing the protest he was bound ta The deceased, who had attained his 67th year, had make as a matter of principle on the second rsa.ding been suffering from heart trouble for about twelve of the Bill, months, but up to three notiths ago, was able to .. . . _. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Seddon (Newton), while protesting against a clause affecting the township of Prescot, took a similar course. The bill was then read the second time and referred_ to a Committee.</p>
        <p>attend to his duties. Father Byrne who came to this district from Sheffield had been ordained priest about 50 years, a portion of that time being a chaplain to the Volunteers. The Bev. John Donohoe (Haydock), has been appointed to succeed Father Byrne.</p>
