      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>NEW fHEOLOGY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW fHEOLOGY.</p>
        <p>Rev. E. Lloyd Jones Lectures at Bold-street.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. Lloyd Jones, the famous lecturer, of Manchester, lectured at Bold-street Wesloyan,uCnutch. on Thursday evening to a large gathering on. e New e Theology." The meeting had ueesi arranged oy toe Wesleyan Methodist Biota Class League Vd.rno,,too Circuit), and the Presideat of tan orgaulSillAUa kMr. J. C. Holland) occupied the chair. suchpt i privilege laeog thrrio'huegeholluAtlßGmrAeaNtsaßidriAtalirn. i'lauu3d'd Jones ‘eg:tseewmeellilk.ni.utwaa eo old do friend. b eable to i rl.i biro? bloou of the Bible Class League which has ueen est: 0 or dceretafoorrreedtotot ateoevootlcjeoeilootest for about 15 months and said there were uow Uolow SOU direct members. The chief anu was to stimulate Biblical study and they had held periodical itILIUOt and outdoor meetings. The Rev. . greatest LLOYD disturbance oNiIS 1110 f they i religious t hiunugthuet, midst of the within his memory and he could not go 'Jack to any period in Christian history when things were more serious than they were at the present hoar. The new movement was a crest and seriops oue, as wide as the Christian world itself. He hoped his hearers would make a strong and determined effort to dissociate the movement as much as possible from tne name of one man. It was not the creation of one man or of one book, but of a thousand, and if Mr. Campbell had never been born the new theology was born before him, and if he should unfortunately disappear we would have a new theology on our hands possibly more vigorous than it was through his instrumentality. lie (the speaker) regretted that anything had been said by those with whom he agreed that had been either personal or bitter. The gentlemen tbey had to oppose were not their enemies but their brethren, not a number of mad iconoclasts wishing to destroy the Christian faith, but enlightened Christian men trying to reconstruct it from within. Concerning the movement as a whole, he was not sur- prised at it. He thought it was inevitable. The penalty or privilege of living in the twentieth century was that we lived in an age of criticism. We had done with the rule of thumb or, still better, we had done I with believing simply because our grandfathers believed. I Some of the items of the new theology had entered into every Christian church in this country. He did not resent a new theology—indeed he would like a new one, provided only that it was true. (Hear, hear). His reason for disagreeing with many vital points in the movement had nothing at all to do with the fact that it was called new, but because he was profoundly convinced that they were not true. There was some- thing wrong in the presentation of the gospel to-day' Fewer people were coming to the churches than ever. and he would be a bold man who said that the way in which the gospel was preached had nothing to do with it. He believed they very often put the glorious gospel into everlasting old clothes, and the nap and the buttons had gone off. (Laughter.) It would be a God-send if they could find a man who would give the Gospel of Jesus Christ without altering its fundamentals, a new suit that would commend it to the twentieth century. (Applause.) To understand the new theology movement was a long, tedious and arduous task. Without any venom or cynicism he would say that if they wanted to understand the new theology, the one book they need not read, and suffer nothing at all by not reading it, was "The New Theology" by Mr. Campbell. They were told by the leaders of the movement that the point of view from which they looked at everything was belief in the immanence of God. Mr. Jones sub- mitted that there was no real and necessary connection , between belief in the immanence of God and belief in the other counts of the new theology. The doctrine of I the divine immanence had had its believers from the I days of the ancient Greek philosophers. The new I - theology was the assertion of the right of the human 'mind to criticise, examine and reject or accept that. :which a man by his own mind had proved to be true or otherwise. The movement at the bottom was a protest against authority and a plea for the supremacy of know- ledge in matters religious. It was really a metaphysical effort to describe God and not an attempt, or at least not supremely, to interpret the Bible. The movement would inevitably fail because they could not make any vigorous religion out of a metaphysical conception. It was too thin, cold, and weak, lie parted company with the movement in its teaching concerning God. \Vhile the idea of the immanence of God would sometimes relieve one's mind, he rejected it because it was only a half-trial). As truly as he could think of God being in everything, he was bound to think of Him being above everything and outside everything, by the laws of the same tkinking. We did not know sufficiently about God ta dogmatise as to what His nature was. If he believed in the immanence of God, he would not have the slightest idea of what was meant by God being a Person. For all religious purposes, a God that was not a Persoa was no good to him at all. He parted com- pany more completely with the movement in its failure to account for and explain Jesus Christ. Tne tendency of the movement was to place Christ in a class with certain other persons and to say there was no difference of nature but only of degree. His difficulty all his life had been to find a class in which to put Jesus. He could find no partner for Him. The only explanation was to devoutly commit themselves to "My Lord and my God." lie greatly regretted the teaching of the new theology concerning sin. Whatever sin was, it was an act of the will, and the greatest proof that sin was positive and not negative was that there bad been thousands of members of the human race sorry for and ashamed of their sins. In conclusion Mr. Lloyd Jones said he believed the great Spirit of God was as much the teacher of the twentieth century as of any century and the value of the Bible to him was that it demonstrated to him as a matter of history and fact, that God could speak to a human being, that lie knew the mystic way of getting at the mind and heart of man. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER in proposing a vote of thanks to the lecturer and the Chairman, said he thought the lecture would help them to discriminate in these matters between the false and the true. Mr. Jones had dealt with his subject in a very masterly style and the weaknesses of the new theology had been pointed out in a ivonderfully clear way. 'lo his (Mr. Parker's) mind there was much in Mr. Campbell's book that was very true and right but it was not it whole system. It did not hang together, but was just a delineation of the tendencies of thought in certain forward directions which were not correlative and did not necessarily depend upon each other. He was glad to recognise that in the Bible Class League there were great possibilities for usefulness, not only in the study of the Bible but in dealing with the practical problems of sin and sorrow that were in the world. (Applause). Dr. BENNETT seconded.</p>
        <p>The ltev. SIDNEY PITT in supporting advised his hearers to read Bishop Gore's honk" The new theology and the old religion." lie recognized in Bishop Gore a thoroughly honest man. lie was sorry to say the Bishop was strongly opposed to them at the present time 013 the Education Bill but he was with them on the Licensing Bill, and the Bishop who dare say be approved of that, measure was a strong and able man. (Applause). The vote VCRS heartily accorded.</p>
