      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <p>Runcorn have never beaten Warrington in a knock- out competition, and I hope and expect that the record will hold good to-day. Curiously enough Warrington have already this season appeared at Runcorn in a Cup-tie and won with ease. That was on October 12th when Warrington appeared at Irwell- lane in the first round of the Lancashire Cup Competi- tion and beat the Linnets by 15 points to 2. This should be a hopeful augury for the Wirepullers to-day, and not at all discounted by the fact that Runcorn won the League match there by 5 to 3. They were Ibeaten 14 to nil at Warrington, so that on form and ' everything else Warrington are looked upon as pros- pective winners of the second round.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that in the Lancashire Cup- tie Runcorn's team was pretty much the same, except that Jolley had to play full back in place of Harrison. Warrington were,on the contrary, weakly represented, and such members of to-day's team was Fish, Taylor, and Brooks were absent. The forwards played a fine game and settled the issue by smashing up the Run- corn pack. They can do the same to-day, and every- thing depends on their earnestness in scrummage work. The Warrington six have a big advantage in weight and everyman must get down —no winging ! Run9orn are sure to play the kicking game, which the Warnng- ton men know they must avoid like the plague. Their combination should be equal to putting up a credit- able number of points.</p>
        <p>There has been special training in both camps this week and each team has had a course of the brine baths at Northwich. The Warrington men played a trial game on Thursday with the idea of seeing how Jenkins would shape, but the half back's keee did not seem very strong.</p>
        <p>The team was finally selected as follows :—Tilley, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Watkin, threequarters ; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs ; Thomas, Shugars, Jenkins, A. Boardman, P. Boardman and Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>-- The only changes from the selection of the last few weeks are the two Boardmans for Heath and Naylor, and I think it is good policy to include such a fine pair of scrummagers as the first- named. There has been a great diversity of opinion about retaining Watkin, owing to his being somewhat weak on defence, but I am very glad the threequarter line has not been changed. It would have been rank folly to have moved either Dickenson or Taylor from the centre, even to introduce Isherwood, and Watkin has at any rate proved that he possesses scoring ability. The team is, in my opinion, the best which is available at present.</p>
        <p>Runcorn's team is also a representative one, the selected thirteen being : Harrison, full back ; Flannagan, Butterworth, Moran and Unsworth, three- quarters ; Jolley and Watton, half backs ; Walker, Padbury, Williams, Richardson, McDonald and Wood forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The Works Competition is to have some opposition in the shape of a similar tournament among the Association clubs. It is worth while remembering, however, that the Rugby competition is held in aid of charities, and it is thus entitled to wide and gener- ous support.</p>
        <p>The matches commence on Monday, March 23rd, and the draw for the first round is as follows : A.rmitaise and Rigby v. Richmond's Stove Co. ; L. &amp; N.-W. v. Rylands' Apprentices ; Longford Wire v.Crosflelds ; P. and K. (13ewsey) v.Walker's Brewery; Richmond's (Grappenhall) v. Mersey Lead Co. ; Robertson's v. Greenings ; Rylands (wet shop) v. Rylands No. 1 ; Greenhalgh and Co. v. Bifurcated Rivet Co. ; Whitecross v. Monks Hall •, Thewlis's v. Fletcher Russell's; Greenall's v. R.A. Naylor and Co.; Orford Tannery v. Tirth Co. ; Peri in's Tube Co. v. Castle Rubber Co.; P. and K. (Main) v. Co-operative Society ; .Byes St. Helens Cable and Post Office.</p>
        <p>Handbills giving the times of the ;;;;c1;;;; ill be issued this week-end.</p>
        <p>THE "A' TEAM.</p>
        <p>After a keen game, Warrington "A " won the match with Leigh Shamrocks on Saturday by 6 points to 5. They have another heavy engagement to-day at Pemberton, and the following team has been chosen to do duty :—Bramhall, full back ; Tinkler, Cart- wright, Jordan and Landor, three-quarters ; Daintith and Jenkins, half.backs ; Naylor, Harmer, Forster, Kettle, Fairhurst and Holbrook, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Pacidington Station, W.</p>
        <p>Hull were made to feel the weight' of Warrington's Cup-Tie vigour on Saturday, when the homesters ran up their record score for the season of 5 goals 9 tries (37 points) to 1 goal 2 tries (8 points). The visitors were fully represented with the exception ef their clever full back, Harry Taylor, but Warrington were similarly handicapped through the absence of Tilley. There was not such a disparity in the teams or in the play as the score would suggest. Hull played clever football, but finished badly, whereas Warrington were sound at all points and missed no chances of scoring. The Hull halves,Anderson andWallace,played a brilliant game, and if the three-quarters behind them had been anything like the calibre of the Warrington quartette, the struggle for supremacy would have been close. Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Watkin quite outclassed the opposition and did most of the damage. It was a very fast and interesting game, with the ball continually travelling from one end of the field to the other, and the spectators had plenty of fan for their money.</p>
        <p>The recording of two victories in one week has improved Warrington's League position somewhat, though they are still only nit,th. Their points for and against are still more than creditable. Only Oldham can show better figures. Wigan had scored the largest number of points with Halifar second, and Warrington third. It is only in defence where Oldham stand ahead of all others, and here Warring- ton make a good second obviously. On their capabili- ties, they should have been nearer the top of the League than they are.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows: For. Agst. Per- P. IV. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c't'ge. Oldham flonslet Broughton Sal ford Wigan Hull K. R. Batley 2B 18 10 0 53 66 304 39 54 240 64'28 Halifax Warrington Keighley 26 15 11 051 57 273 49 56 266 57.69 Wakefield T 27 15 11 1 55 79 347 51 61 285 5740 Barrow ... Bradford 27 14 13 0 43 54 248 39 75 303 51'85 Hull 2B 14 14 050 68 304 41 53 241 5000 Runcorn 26 13 13 0 22 56 212 24 44 180 50•C3 Dewsbury 26 11 14 1 41 49 229 38 63 265 4423 Rochdale Huddersfield Leeds York 24 8 16 0 40 48 224 59 65 313 3333 Swinton , Merthyr , Leigh St. Helens 25 6 17 2 28 36 164 46101 395 28'00 Ebbw Vale 24 5 18 1 18 27 117 48 73 315 22-91 Widnes 24 3 17 4 17 35 139 42 67 285 2083 Brarnley 95 4 21 0 24 31 141 76 123 521 1600 The match between Leeds and Hunslet, which was abandoned during the first half, is not included in the above table.</p>
