      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <p>April, 1901, he pasted notices on the site of all contracts he had in hand, asking his workmen if they were willing to subscribe a penny per week to the Infirmary if they earned over £l, and 2d. if they earned over £2, and have it deducted in the office before the money was sent to the jobs. Assent was willingly given everywhere and he had never heard a murmur since. If that system were generally adopted they ought to raise £12,500 instead of £1,250. It was, however, necessary for employers to interest themselves in the matter. (Hear, hear.) As to how they should dispose of the money, he remembered that Councillor Evans spoke very strongly in that room some months ago on the infantile mortality question. He (Mr. Fairclough) suggested that the extra money raised by the working- During the past year -21,008 9s. 81. has been raised men should go to the provision of lying-in wards which for the Warrington Infirmary and Dismenalry through he was sure would do a great deal towards giving the agency of the Workingmen's Committee. Naturally children a better start in life than was possible in some much gratification was (repressed at this achievement of the hovels in the town. ; at the annual meeting which was held at the Town Hat Several delegates pointed out that the system on Saturday afternoon. but there was a strong deter- advocated by Mr. Fairclongh was already in force at mination to snruttantially increase the amonnt in the some works in the town. near futnre. The meeting was very largely attended Mr. HAMPSON pointed out that the emp'oyees could by representatives of the various works in the town and not send all the money they raised to the Infirmary as neig.hbourbood, the Mayor (Alderman Sraethnrst) pre- at some works the recreation and sick clubs made ' siding. Amongst those present were :—the Revs. C. demands upon their contributions. Harvey Cook and Father Whittle, Alderman Burton : The accounts were adopted. (chairman of the Working Men's Committee).Messrs..T. ALDERMAN BURTON'S REVIEW. Charlton Parr, John Faircloueh, C. Jackson Holmes ; (hon. auditor), Geor,te Walker (vice-chairman of the Alderman BURTON gave a review of the work of the Working Men's Committee and representative of the yea-, and also submitted figures showing the gradual Education Committee),J. H. J. fiamPson (hon.treasurer increase in contributions since 1901. Since the corn- and general secretary to the inatitntel. Dr. Joseph, Mr. rnittee was formed the handsome sum of £16,334 es. Harry Fairclongh. Mr. .T. H. Smethnrst, and Mr. T. , had been obtained. (Applause). The annual concerts Wadsworth (one of the trrettees). had been the means of re dizing £1,2)9 7s. 2d. He spoke A letter was read from !fr. W. Peter Rvlands express- in high terms of the valuable assistance which had been ina his ret,ret, at not being able to attend and inviting rendered to the committee by the General Secretary of representatives of the committee to tails part in the the Infirmary Hampson). opening of the infirmary extension on Thursday next. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>- THE MAYOR'S ADDRESS The 7‘IA.YOt2. paid a high tribute to the work done by the committee and impressed upon them the fact that all the held they could give was needed in order that the institution might he of the greatest value to the poor. Oh visiting the Dispensary a few days ago, he was literally astonished to see the number of patients, the room being crowded. He was glad to say there were some subscribers who contributed willingly to every good object in the town but there were other People who had money and who took care to stick to it. If they looked through the annual lists of subscriptions they would see practically one class of people repre- sented. He was glad he had been able to do a little to nut the Nursing Association in a sound position—(hear, hear)—and he would like to try to do the same for the Infirmary, hut he was afraid they would call him a "cadging Mayor." (Laughter.) He would be delighted if some public-spirited townsman would lead off a sub- scription list so that the extension might be opened free of debt. He MOllld he pleased to do his share. (Applause.) The Workingmen's Committee had done nobly in raising £1,005 in the past year and he hoped next. year their contribution would be still further increased to £1,230. (Applause.) THE YEAR'S WORK. Mr. HAMTSON submitted the statement of accounts, which showed that the amount realized by the working men was £l,OOB 9s. ficl.. being an increase on the sum received last year of £9 Ss. 9d. (Applause.) He sub- mitted that that was very satisfactory considering the great demands which have been made upon working men in connection with the Infirmary extension. He was pleased to state that the subscriptions direct from the works amounted to £916 3s, 41., being an increase of nearly £5O on last year's figures. it was interesting to find from " Burdett's Hospitals and Charities 1907," that the contributions from the Working Men's Com- mittee of Warrington ranked high as compared with other towns. Leicester was first with .£3.4 per thousand of the population, and Warrington was sixth 7ith £l6 per _</p>
        <p>1, It was resolved that the delegates present form the general committee with power to add to their number, , after which Alderman Burton was re-elected chairman , and Mr. George Walker, deputy .chairman. It was intimated that owing to the demands of busi- ness, Mr. Ruben Higharn, who had acted as secretary , for some years, could Dot see his way to continue in office. On the recommendation of the Executive Com- mittee. Mr. J. H.. 1 Hampson was unanimously appointed to the position and a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Higham for his e`ficient-work. Mr. C. JACKSON Hor.atEs was re-elected hon. auditor. VOTES OF TEIANKS. Votes of thanks were heartily passed to the Chairman Deputy-Chairman, the Treasurer, the Hon. Auditor, the representative to the Education Committee, the Chief Constable and police, the Warrington Rugby Foothill Club, and Mr. Down's bind ; to the hon. medical officers ; the senior and junior house surgeons, the matron, and nurses, and to the :%fayor for presiding, and for the use of the Council Chamber. I Mr. J. CHAPtLTON PARR paid a tribute to Alderman IBurton's services as Chairman and expressed gratifica- tion that the committee had made such progress. He hoped that shortly their magnificent institution would be endowed with a £1..500 snbsrription from that com- mittee. They all knew that the extension would involve a considerably increased annual outlay, as they would require a larger staff. Mr. J. H. SMF:THURST said that as a member of the Finance Committee he could say that every penn y sub- scribed to the institution was well spent. The Rev, C. HARVEY COOK said this was a Socialistic age, and if they attached any value to Socialism they had an opportunity of showing their faith by supporting the institution so largely that it might become their own.</p>
        <p>Mr. JaCKSON moved and Mr. ROBERTS seconded the adoption of the accounts. Mr. HARRY FAIRCLOCGH said he did not approve of the sun) of £1,004. He did not think it was as largess it ought to be by about one tenth. He did not imply that anyone present had not done all he could for the benefit of the Infirmary, but when he told them that</p>
        <p>Collins Green colliery, where they employed 2,500 workmen' had subscribed more to St. Helena Infirmary than the total amount raised from all the Warrington works during the past year, they would see that they in Warrington were not doing all they could. in his own works during the past year tbe subscriptions came to £2O 12s. In 1902 they only amounted to 14s. 4d. In</p>
