      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>A YEAR'S CONTRIBUTIONS FROM THE WORKINGMEN.</p>
        <p>Further Increase Aimed At.</p>
        <p>IMPORT DUTIES RAISE PRICES.</p>
        <p>GERMAN GOVERNMENT PROOFS.</p>
        <p>By L. G. Chlozza Money, M.P.</p>
        <p>ARTICI E M.</p>
        <p>Let us "tax the foreigner " is ever the cry of the Protectionists in this country. In Germany Protec- tionists are honester. The German agrarians (Agricultural Protectionists) never allege that duties do not make food dear. They argue straightforwardly, that food is made dear by the duties, but that it is worth while to make it dear in order to preserve German agriculture. I talked with several German Agrarians when recently in Germany and always they talked as I have said. Why cannot our Tariff Reformers be equally plain ?</p>
        <p>The Object is to Raise Price.</p>
        <p>If import duties do not raise prices, why, it may be asked, did Mr Chamberlain exclude maize and bacon from his list of duties specifically that they might not be affected in price? Bacon he left out in order not to tax the food of the poor. That is to say, he recog- nized that a tax on bacon would not be paid by the foreigner but by the consumer here. What is true of bacon must be true of wheat, rice, tapioca, oats,barley, hops, cheese, butter, eggs, margarine, and the hundred and one other varieties of food which would be taxed under the Chamberlain scheme. Few people realise how long was the list of foods which would have been raised in price if the General Election of 1005 had not seen the Protectionists routed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain and Raw Materials.</p>
        <p>Again, "Nil- Chamberlain was careful to promise not to tax raw materials. Why ? if the foreigner can be taxed, let us tax his materials and make him pay the tax on them. Germany alone supplies 119 with £5,000,000 worth of raw materials every year, and America with many times as much, while from the world at large we import raw materials worth £.200,000,000. If the foreigner pays import duties, let us tax the £200,000,000 by ten per cent., and we get £20,000,000 and can have Old-Age Pensions to-morrow. The fact that Mr. Chamberlain was so careful not to suggest taxes on raw material shows that he fully realized that the foreigner does NOT pay impor t duties. I have some- times heard it argued that the foreigner only pays the import duty when the taxed article is pro- duced in the importing country. But even this will not hold water. We produce copper ore. Why not, then, tax imports of copper ore if the foreigner pays the import duty on articles which are produced in the importing country ? Why not ? Because the Pro- tectionists know very well thve the statement that the foreigner pays import duties is false. If they did not know it, they would boldly ask for a tax on raw materials.</p>
        <p>German Government Testimony.</p>
        <p>As I have said, the German Protectionists are straightforward. When they tax impotts they do it openly with the object of making higher prices. For instance, the present German tariff was passed in 1902. It is the custom in Germany (and it is a very good custom) for a Bill to be accompanied by a printed explanation of its nature and principles in order to show why it is introduced. Now let us see what the German Government stated in cold print as to the reasons they had for raising the German food taxes. Did they say it was to tax the foreigner? Did they imitate our Tariff Reformers ? Not at all. They boldly wrote thus :</p>
        <p>A means whereby the agricultural interests are enabled to cover their cost of production is to he found, under the given circumstances, by creating a factor which will determine the inland selling price through relative Protective duties. Although this means, as is shown by the fluctua- tion of prices of our wheat during the last twenty years, does not always guarantee remunerative prices, it still brings about generally improved inland rates. NLAND PRICES ARE RAISED, SO FAR AS \ A CONSIDERATION OF THE CIRCUM- STANCES OF THE LAST TEN YEARS WIIT. ALLOW US TO JUDGE, IN PROPORTION TO THE DUTIES." They then went on to give a table of prices from which I will quote two lines:</p>
        <p>Price of Wheat In Germany (1896.1899). Price in Dantzig without duty 128.80 marks per ton Average Inland Price Inland Price nigher by 35.70 „ „ Amount of Duty</p>
        <p>35.00 ~ Priee of Rye in Germany (1890.1899)</p>
        <p>Price in Dantzig -witout duty 98.70 marks per ton Average Inland Price 134.00 11 11 11 Inland Price higher by 35.30 „ Amount of Duty on Rye</p>
        <p>_ Be it understood these are not figures stated or collected by me, but figures advanced by the German Government itself in proof of the fact that 'mport duties raise prices.</p>
        <p>Benlin and London Compared.</p>
        <p>The German Government, in the same Memorandum, proceeded to compare the price of wheat in Germany and England respectively, proNing, just as is proved by the British Board of Trade, that wheat is always higher in price in Berlin than in London. Again they quoted figures which are unanswerable and thus com- mented upon them: "These tables show that the difference between the inland price and the price of foreign wheat (exclusive of duty) VARIES ACCORDING TO THE AMOUNT OF DUTY. It is therefore to be expected that a raising of duties will favourably affect our internal agricultural interests."</p>
        <p>In short, the Gerrnan Government raised the duty in order to give higher prices to German farmers, in red that is, to make Getman consumers pay towards the profits of the agricultural part of the population.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Row Duties Rise.</head>
        <p>Row Duties Rise.</p>
        <p>It may be well, in this connection, to give an idea of how food duties rise. To Germany they began with Is. 2d. per quarter in 1857. After that the duties rose thus :</p>
        <p>Rise of the German Wheat Duty. S. d.</p>
        <p>1857 ............ 1 2 per quarter 1879 ............ 2 2 „ „ 1885 ............ 1888 ............ 10 10 „ „ 1892 ............ 7 7 „ „ 1903 ............ 11 0 „ „</p>
        <p>When the Tariff Reformers tell us that the duties they suggest are small we shall do well to remember this significant table.</p>
        <p>6 Spiritualist Funeral at Hill Chile. On Monday afternoon the first Spiritualist funeral in the ancient burial ground at Hill Cliffe took place. By arrangement with the chaplain pro. tern., the Rev. J. F. Matthews, and with the consent of the officials part of the service was conducted in the chapel. The remains were those of the 17 months old son of Mr. and Mrs. W. Hammon, of 4, River-road, whose death had followed an attack of pneumonia. Mr. Hammon is the corresponding secretary of the Spiritualist Society, and their son had been " christened ' at one of the meetings. The President of the Society (Mr.W,E. I Stout) who was assisted by Mrs. Hazlehurst, of Man- chester, the medium conducting the weekly meetings at the usual meeting place on Sunday and Monday, performed the last rites. After the singing of a hymn and prayer at the house, the cortege proceeded to the chapel, where the usual preliminary service was held, and where a considerable number of members of the society and other friends had assembled to manifest their sympathy with the bereaved parents. The service was completed at the graveside amid tbe greatest reverence. Instead of throwing soil upon the little coffin, those present scattered flowers in the grave as emblems of the pure life which had changed its operations.</p>
      </div>
