      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <p>MY DEAR</p>
        <p>Many of my little nieces and nephews have warm, kind hearts for the poor little ones who have no home and kind friends. You well remember our competition for providing clothing at Christmas time for those in want. I was most pleased with the response to my appeal, and now my members are asked to help me to give some of the poor little ones a few toys. A fortnight ago I offered prizes to the senders of the beet penny toys. Now, please don't forget me, and give your help a second time to please the little children who are unable to have even a penny given them to buy their toys. I must thank Flossie Neale for her contribution of toys, and her kind little letter. I hope many more members will follow her example. Now to another subject—myself. Did you all see the new competition announced last week? The best sketch sent in of Uncle Ned. This subject is rather personal, but I had so many letters suggesting it that I felt bound to agree.</p>
        <p>-I am most anxious to see your ideas of myself. Welcome to the following new members. I hope to receive many stories, etc., from them in the future:—</p>
        <p>Jane Burgess, Nora Mooring, Leonard Har- rison, Emma Inman, and Betsy Thacker. Mirrors have been sent to the following:— H. A. Kirby, Bessie Lewis, Leonard Harrison, Flossie Neale, and Nora Mooring. I am sorry that I cannot insert all the letters received, but I am most pleased to hear from my members. I have received with pleasure notes from Nora Mooring, Flossie Neale, Dorothy Carmell, Veronica Moran, H. A. Kirby, and Lilian Coates. Well, I must finish my chat for another week. With love, UNCLE RED.</p>
        <p>_+___</p>
        <p>THE BEST WAY. This is a difficult world indeed, And the people are hard to suit; And the man who plays on a violin</p>
        <p>Is a bore to the. i-nari with a flute. And I myself have often -thought How very much better 'twould be If every one of the folks I know Would only agree with me. But since they won't, then the very best way To make this world look bright Is never to mind what people say, But do what you think is right. - CIS SY FLETCHISR.</p>
        <p>;TORY OF MIISSENTOTICHIT.</p>
        <p>, By the time baby was twelve months old she had learned many things. She called her mother "Ma-ma," and she could say "Kitty" to the cat. She knew " fire " and "burn," for she had once put her hand against the hot grate, and she never forgot the pain of the big blisters that came On her poor fingers. She also knew the lamp, and the, window, and the mirror. About this time she heard a long strange word many, many times a day. The word was " Mus- sentouchit. Baby wondered what " Mussen- touchit " could be. The strange thing lived in the chest of drawers. Baby knew that, for the moment she got her busy little hands into any drawer, somebody would say, " Mussentouchit.- It lived in the sewing-machine, too, for the moment baby set the wheel going mother took her hand gently away, and said, " Mussen- touchit." It sometimes lived in the tall jar that stood on the little round table. Everybody in the room cried " Mussentouchit " when baby stretched up her hand to toll&amp; that jar. In the corner of the parlour there was a glass globe with water, and in it lived three little gold- fishes. Baby was xery fond of climbing on a chair to see the tiny fishee darting across their pretty lake. But whenever she dipped her fingers into the globe to touch the pretty creatures somebody said, " Mussentouchit. Now, this went on till baby was two years old. There was no word she heard so often as the long queer word. "Mussentouchit." One day she found herself near the glass globe all alone. Her mother was very busy, •and for a few minutes did not notice what baby was doing. This was her chance. She climbed up on the chair and reached over to touch the goldfishes. But she reached a little too far, and fell to the floor, dragging the globe with her and drench- ing herself with the water. Baby was picked up, kissed, and scolded. She was frightened, but not hurt. This taught her a lesson which she did not forget. She learned not to put her hands into places where " Mussentouchit " lived. Ina Bow.</p>
        <p>ONE WAY TO A WEDDING. John (sheepishly): ' I—l s'spose you'll b. gittin' married some time?" Betty (with a far away air): " Oh, I dare say I shall some time. I dare say I'll git married, to—." " Oh." " Traps, we might both get married at the same time. Wouldn't it be awful, John, if the parson should make a mistake and marry us to each other?" " I—l shouldn't mind."</p>
        <p>.. ... "No; neither should I, to tell you the truth, John." CYRIL CARMELL.</p>
        <p>A LITTLE BROTHER! I have a little brother, _</p>
        <p>He's only three years old; To us at home who love him</p>
        <p>He's worth his weight in gold. We often play together, And I begin to find. To make my brother happy I must be very kind And always very gentle When we run about and play, And never take his playthings Or little toys away. I must not vex or tease him, And never angry be With the darling little brother Whom God has given me. JA 73URGESS.</p>
        <p>A HALF-CROWN WORD.</p>
        <p>A father recently overheard his young son use a word he did not approve of, and, calling the child to him, said "My boy, if you will promise me never to use that word again I'll give you sixpence." The little fellow promised, and, true to his word, refrained. About a week later he went to his father and said: "Papa, I've learned a new word, and I think it's worth TlM- half-&amp;-crown." DORIS KIVELL.</p>
