      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>HONESTY.</head>
        <p>HONESTY.</p>
        <p>At a street corner in Edinburgh a little ragged boy used to stand. One day, when he was stand- ing at his usual place, he asked a gentleman fora copper. Now, this gentleman had no less change than a shilling, so he asked the boy to go and get it changed. The boy went, and when he returned he saw the gentleman had gone, for he thought the boy would never return. Day after day the boy wandered up and down the streets. At last, after seven dap' searching, he found him, and handed him the change. This so pleased the gentleman that he took him into his home. H. A. KIRBY.</p>
        <p>THE POULTICE.</p>
        <p>A boy, having hurt his foot, was put to bed. When the doctor came he ordered poultices to be applied. The boy's mother attempted to put the poultices on; he murmured, and would not have them on, because he wae spoilt. His father promised to punish him if he made the least objection until the operation was over. Whilst the father was putting the poultice on, the boy made several attempts to speak. When the operation WAS over the youth was heard to say, "Oh, father; you have put it on the wrong foot 1" VERONICA MORAN.</p>
        <p>THE REASON WHY. "When I was at the party," Said Betty (aged just four) "A little girl fell off her chair Right down upon the floor. " And all the other little girls Began to laugh but me— I didn't laugh a single bit," Said Betty, seriously. "Why not?" her mother asked her, Full of delight to find That Betty—bless her little heart— Had been so sweetly kind. "Why didn't you laugh, darling? Or don't you like to tell?" "I didn't laugh," said, Betty, " 'Cause it was me that fell." DOROTHY DARMRLL.</p>
        <p>A WITTY LAD.</p>
        <p>A boy went to school one morning in the pouring rain without any special protection. On arrival he:was wet to the skin, and when the master asked him why he had not brought an umbrella he gave five reasons. First, he did not expect it to rain; second, the distance was not great if it did; third, he thought he could share with a school-fellow; fourth, he was sure he could take shelter; and, lastly, he had no umbrella. Theie's an old saying: "A lame excuse is better than none." But a lame excuse is often another name for a lie.</p>
        <p>BETSY THACKER.</p>
        <p>Visitor: "Why do you have 'Keep off the grase ' signs all over this park " Park-keeper: "We do it so that people 'will more thoroughly enjoy being on the grass."</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. Hall paid every blade of grass was a sermon. The iaext day he was clipping his lawn, when a parishioner said—" That's right, doctor. Cut your sermons short."</p>
        <p>, A collection which it might be worth while to preserve is that which Madame Betsey Patter- son Bonaparte made of her bonnets. She kept every bonnet she ever had in her life, and as these were remarkable in.thei way the charm of the collection is easily imagined.</p>
