      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday March 14, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday March 14, 1908.</p>
        <p>P.S A. Meetings. Mr. B. Farrar, the secretary, presided on Sunday at the usual meeting of the Wycliffe Society. Mr. William Light, of Warrington, gave the address upon " Giving thanks to God always. He said there were times when the gloom and difficulties of life for a moment made it difficult to promptly thank God, or to see God's dealings in a right spirit. God lived, and the troubles of life were often permeated by God, or were their own making ; but when they looked around,with careful thought, and sound reasoning, they were hound to acknowledge God's loving and mighty hand. Mr. W. Parker sang Nearer my God to Thee " and " There is a green,' which were greatly appreciated. Mr. W. 11. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. Weave also took part in the meeting. The chairman read a letter from Buckley-street P.S.A., congratulating the Wycliffe Society on the celebration of their Dith anniver- sary. The Buckley-street Society had reached their 10th anniversary.—There was an open meeting of the Warrington Society. Mr. David Owen cresided and spoke upon the relationship to be discerned between the Creator and Created beings, and shevved in very j lucid manner how the impress of the Divine was stamped on every human soul. Mr. E. Wynne Jones spoke on the Licensing Bill and urged all present to j do their utmost to help the Bill through both houses of Parliamcnt. Two clarionet solos, with concertina I accompaniments, were tendered by Mr. F. Addis and ' friend. The following resolution was unanimously passed —" We, the members of the Warr ington P.S.A. Society heartily welcome the Licensing Bill, J believing it to be a much needed measure of temper- ance reform, pledging ourselves to do all in our power j to secure its passing, and appeal to the present mem- , her of this borough to be in his place and support it.</p>
        <p>M`i</p>
      </div>
