      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>--- FORTHCOMING PARR HALL MEETING.'</head>
        <p>--- FORTHCOMING PARR HALL MEETING.'</p>
        <p>The Dear Beer Cry. [To THE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER."] Dear Sir.—" Have you no fear of dearer beer ?" is likely to replace the saying "My word if you're not off" May I be permitted to say as a working man to whom this question appeals that I "have no fear," because I know that this is only a war cry to bluff us working men to work and petition against the promised reform that will help us to rear our children in cleaner and brighter surroundings, and remove from their midst many of the places of temptation. I am also encouraged to have no fear by the statement made by the chairman of Hoare and Co., Ltd., Brewers, at the meeting of Shareholders held on Monday laat in London After condemning the cry" Your beer will cost you more "he said" increasing the price of beer was not possible." And Sir, I believe him because the brewer knows perfectly well that the British workingman knows better than pay anything approach- ing the 50 per cent, increase that Lord Burton tells us will be placed on beer if this bill is passed into law. We may be only working men, but we are not so soft as to believe all the Lords tell us. Now what about the other poster and a few of the statements thereon? We are told that the" Sabbath was made for man and not man for the Sabbath." In this I quite agree and as a working man who enjoys his Sabbath rest will support Sunday closing. Is it fair that the majority of works, shops and every department of trade except the drink trade enjoy what God intended they should enjoy while the poor publicans, barmen and barmaids are com- pelled to work from six to eight hours on Sunday ? Surely the working men of Warrington have a greater sense of justice than allow this sort of thing to con- tinue. Publicans and those they employ have souls as well as us and even if they do not care to attend a place of worship they would like to spend the Sabbath day in the home with their families or take a saunter into the country lanes. Then we are told " that Sunday closing has been a failure wherever it has been tried and will not increase the attendance at places of worship." This I can sincerely deny. For five years I lived in one of the most drunken towns in North Wales and during the whole time I was there I never saw more than three drunken persons in the town on one Sunday and these had been for a drive in the country. Occasionally you could meet some stray one who had walked the allotted three miles and was returning home the worse for wear, but if the six mile limit is passed these cases will become more rare. Regarding the attendance at places of worship, one has only to refer to the recent Royal Commission on the Church in Wales to prove that the attendance far exceeds that of any part of England. Or just take a trip into Denbighshire and contrast it with Lancashire and without very much trouble one can see at a glance the effect of Sunday Closing of public-houses on the Churches. Then we are told that " personal liberty is the principle article of faith in an Englishman's political creed, to dispossess him of it is to deprive him of his manhood." 'Tis true : and I take it that the main object of the Bill is to restore to the English working man that personal liberty he holds so dear, and which he does not enjoy, and is debarred from enjoying under the preSent Licensing laws. He has the public-house thrust upon him whether he wants it or not. The brewer applies for a licence and it is granted. The working man may protest but the magistrates think (mind you I say think ") that it is necessary, and say it must be so. Not much personal liberty for the working man there. Nor is this personal liberty given by the trade to the M.P.'s to vote as they like on this question, judging by some of the petitions received in the House during the last week, many of which contain threats to the M.P.'s that if they vote in favour of this Bill they vote at the peril of their seats. By all means let us retain our manhood and persoual liberty, and give us the power to say we will or we will not have a public- house at the corner of our street. Now just a word about this Robbery that we are bearing so much about. Who is being robbed? I have before me a few copies of the "Licensing World," the official organ of the Trade,and in the lists of dividends some of them are as follows :—Arthur Gniness and Son 20 per cent.; Bristol Brewery, George's and Co., 16 per cent. ; Bury Brewery Co., 20 per cent. ' • Sroud Brewery Co., 11 per cent. The Hull Brewery Co. pay 12 per cent and Si. per share bonus and transfer £15,000 and carry forward £lO,OOO. All these firms carry forward their thousands of pounds, while the number of com- panies who pay from 5 per cent. to 12 per cent, are too unmerous to take up your space with Mr. Editor. Will any of your readers tell me what tradesman in Warrington can pay such dividends or reap such profits The Public House a Public Need " an lay aside such amounts, outside the brewing • industries ? I am rather inclined to think it is we [To THE EDITOR OF THE " EXAMINER:] workingmen who pay 20. a pint for beer who are being robbed or there would not be such large proSts. When Sir,—The above is the heading of a poster which at the present time decorates the walls of our good old Lord Burton told us that there was a probability of the to xi" and, I must say, that for a mixture of sentimental price of beer being raised he did not tell us that his humbug, pious cant and downright sephistry the con_ firm bad a reserve fund of 21,400,000, and that the tents of this placard will be hard to beat. It is quite Aross profits of his firm for last year were £935,430. correct, to say that at one period of our history theph no ! he does not want the working man to look at d above heading was quite true. At the Reformation it in that light. when the Monasteries and Abbeys were abolished as an I hope the working men of Warrington will not be led away by the "dear beer " cry hut will examine these excresence and superfluity the hostels and pilgrim inns matters for themselves, and I think they will agree that erected by them remained as a necessity, and beame the old inns and taverns of England, and as travelling the time has come for us to have our "personal liberty" developed and stage-coaches began to travel from point restored and we shbuld have a say in the matter where t to point, the necessity of places of public safety and the public-houses shall be placed. I think it would he a change if we took a few away from Sankey-street and resort developed. In the reign of Edward I. there were put them in Palmyra-square and Wilson-Patten-street only three inns in London—one in Cherie, one in Walbroolc, and one in Lombard-street. Even in 1552 and a few more refined neighbourhoods. they numbered 40 in London, eight in York, and four While we clamour for shorter hours and more pay let each in Chester, Hull, and Newcastle-on-Tyne. The us think of our friends behind the bar whose hours are ancient inns of England were always under the long enough without working on Sunday. power of Government and Parliament, and were Apologising for trespassing on your space, I remain repeatedly interfered with by statute law. Mayors, yours &amp;c., bailiffs, and justices learned in the law were to " inquire in all places of all and singular of the deeds and outrages of hostelries and their kind." So Resolutions Supporting the Bill. publicans had to be watched in the past. Sufficient has WORKING:VIEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY. been said of the public-house of the past and now we At the close of the evening service at the above come to its present aspect with its great breweries, its chapel last Sunday, the following resolution was tied-houses and its huge drinking saloons called vaults, unanimously passed:—" That this congregation heartil). Those of us who can look back some forty or fifty welcomes the Licensing Bill, believes it to be a much- years can remember the days when the inn was a needed measure of temperance reform, pledges itself to place of resort for man and beast, where good and do all in its power to secure its passing, and appeals to wholesome food and drink could be obtained—the drink A. H. Crosfleld, Esq., MX , to be in his place and to brewed on the premises. Since then the capitalist has support it." come along and transformed the scene. Has it been WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION, improved ? No. Does it in its present character The Warrington Women's Liberal Association has nerve a useful purpose? No. Is the public-house in also passed a resolution approving of the Bill, and its present character a public need ? No : Two-thirds urging the advisability of a public meeting being held at of them have no accommodation for man and beast and which they would give their support.</p>
        <p>We understand that a big demonstration in support of the Government Licensing Bill is to be held in Parr Hall on Friday, April 10th. The Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Cbavasse) has promised to speak, and other speakers are being arranged for.</p>
        <p>NILREM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>TEMPERANCE RALLY IN WARRINGTON. Citizens' Temperance League Formbd.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE RALLY IN WARRINGTON. Citizens' Temperance League Formbd.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>i</p>
        <p>There was a crow 3ed and enthusiastic meeting of temperance workers in the Winmarleigh Cafe on Mon- day evening for the purpose of forming a central body to organise and direct temperance work in Warring- ton. The Rev. John Yonge occupied the chair and gave an inspiring address, which was followed by I, speeches from Mr. W. Roberts, the Revs. J. F. Matthews, C. Harvey Cook, J. B. Buglass, Mr. W. Nightingale and others. Eventually it was unani- mously decided to form a Citizens' Temperance League on the widest possible basis undenominati mat and non-teetotal. The Rev. John Yonge was unanimously elected president —an office which he willingly adopted pro tem. Over CO members were enrolled as a General Council, and the following were appointed to the Execntive Committee : Rev. T. Miller, Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Messrs. W. Roberts,C.E. Parker, E. J. Guest, H. Wardman, W. H. Sherburn, W. Wright, E. V. Walker, W. Nightingale, A. Mounfield, T. Dale, F Gandy, J. Griffiths, Mrs. Osborne,and Mrs. Simpson. Mr. J. Dodds was invited to act as treasurer and con- sented. Two secretaries were appointed in the persons of Messrs. G. A.Leach and H. Woolley. \ At the close of the meeting, the following resolution was unanimously passed, and copies forwarded to the Premier, the Chancellor of the Exchequer (Mr. Asquith) and the Borough Member (Mr. A. H. Crosfield) : " That this meeting heartily welcomes the Govern- ment Licensing Bill as a courageous and statesman-' like measure of temperance and social reform. It rejoices that provisions are in the Bill for fixing a time limit to the compensation clauses of the 1004 Act, for the restoration and extension of the discre- tionary powers of the local licensing authority, for the abolition of the appeal to quarter sessions, for shorter hours cf sale of drink on Sundays, for immediate application of the principle of looal option to new licences, and th 3 extension of the same principle to all licences at the expiration of the time limit, for the exclusion of children from the drinking bars of public- houses' for the better regulation of clubs, and the gradual and compulsory reduction in the number of licences, as recommended by the Minority Report of the Peel Commission. And the meeting hereby pledges itself to do all in its power to strengthen the hands of the Government in their efforts to insure the passage of the Bill into law without any unnecessary delay."</p>
        <p>bt ag pro Bin bye- the o I havc from adjourn Get the be prospt majority someone t Yours etc., The Pi [l'o Tli Sir,—The abc the present time toocri and, I must humbug, pious ca tents of this place correct, to say 1., above heading was when the Monasterit excresence and superi erected by them rema old inns and taverns developed and stage-cot to point, the necessity resort developed. In ti€ only three inns in Lou Walbroolc, and one in L they numbered 40 in Lond each in Chester' Hull, an nn ancient is of England power of Government an repeatedly interfered with bailiffs, and justices learnt. to " inquire in all places oi deeds and outrages of hostelrie pnbliesns had to be watched in been said of the public-house oi come to its present aspect with tied-houses and its huge drinking Those of us who can look back years can remember the days v place of resort for man and bens wholesome food and drink could be o brewed on the premises. Since then come along and transformed the sem improved ? No. Does it in its pi servo a useful purpose? No. Is the its present character a public need? No of them have no accommodation for man !`. s'l V'44.1.71. tri.l‘ 41 , , o- • .11' 14 re,(A T-,4"q5 *</p>
