      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Female Drinking. [To THE EDITOR OF TIIE " EXA..UINER:]</head>
        <p>Female Drinking. [To THE EDITOR OF TIIE " EXA..UINER:]</p>
        <p>Sir,—Having seen both "Phil Squod " and W. Murby's letters, I quite agree with some of their sug- gestions, but the most important thing (to my mind) which they have left out is the secret drinking amongst women. I know of a case personally, of a woman having beer on credit, from an off-licence. I should certainly agree to doing away with off-licences altogether, where provisions are sold, the same as they are doing in Birmingham. Then again, in the Midlands, there is a bye-law stating that no publican shall supply drink at the out door department to be consumed on the premises. I have also seen in Warrington women come straight from " the Uncle's " on a Nlonday morning and then adjourn to the public house, to spend some of the money. Get the women to be total abstainers, so that there will be prospects of brighter and happier homes, and the majority of the working men will follow. Trusting someone more able than me will take the matter up.— Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>SWING BRIDGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>t art .</head>
        <p>t art .</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 14, 1908.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT EVANGELICAL</p>
        <p>NONCONFORMIST MINISTERS' ASSOCIITION.</p>
        <p>At a."—m-ee—ting of the above held yesterday afternoon a resolution wa.s unanimously passed, he.irtily support- ing the Licensing Bill as it stands and urging the Member of the Borough, Mr. A. If. Crosfiell, M.P., and Mr. A. J. King, M.P. for Enutsford Division, and Nlr. Crossley M.P. for Altrinchars. Division to vote for the second reading of same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>SUPPORT- FROII WARRINGTON WESI,F,7INS,</head>
        <p>SUPPORT- FROII WARRINGTON WESI,F,7INS,</p>
        <p>At the close of a lecture delivered in Bold-street Church on rhursday evening by the Rev. E. Lloyd- Jones under the auspices of the Wesleyan Methodist I Bible Class League (Warrington Circuit), Mr. J. Upson pointed out that a meeting was being held that night in Bewsey Ward at which the Licensing Bill would be condemned and he thought it would be well if they passed a resolution promising their hearty support to the measure. He moved : "That this meet- ing representing the Bible classes of this Circuit heartily thanks the Government for the introduction of the Licensing Bill and promises its utrarst support to enable them to pass the measure into law." The Rev. W. Mellor seconded and the Rev. Sidney Pitt supported the resolution which was declared to be I carried with one dissentient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>LEGH-STREET PRIMITIVE METHODISTS. Successful Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>LEGH-STREET PRIMITIVE METHODISTS. Successful Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>A suceessful sale of work in connection with Legh street Primitive Methodist Church was held in the 1 schoolroom on Wednesday and Thursday. The ladies of 1 the congregation had taken up the project, with I enthusiasm and as a result of their efforts there was a large and tempting array of useful !and fancy wares. The following presided at the stalls : CONGREGATIONAL STALL :—Mesdames Dutton, Atkinson, Griffiths, Cook, Swales, Allinan, Himsworth, and other ladies. FLOWER AND FRUIT STALL.—Mesdames Mills, Elwell and Miss Crampton. . , REFRESHMENT STALL :—Mesdames Spruce, Bridge, Crampton, Phillips and Bennett. CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR STALL :—Mrs. Bailey, Misses Atkinson, Shaw, J. Shaw,Elwell, Parkes, Cotton, Buglass, and L. Buglass. SUNDAY SCHOOL STALL :—Mrs. Cowapyllisses Hanks, J. Woodland, and F. Snelling. The sale was opened on Wednesday afternoon in the presence of a large gathering by Mr. James Dodds. The chair was occupied by Alderman Pierpoint. After the singing of a hymn and prayer, the pastor of the church, the Rev. J. B. Buglass, briefly introduced the Chairman and explained that the primary object of the Sale of Work was to clear off a debt upon the Circuit Fund, but they needed and desired to raise a larger amount. They aimed at £lOO, so as to leave the church free in the next two years to provide the special £3OO assessed upon the Warrington Circuit by the Con- nexion as its contribution towards the ,2.50,000 the Connexion had decided to raise in commemoration of its centenary in 1910. Alderman PIERPOENT who had a hearty reception expressed his pleasure at being present,and in the course of an interesting speech confessed he had not, when asked to preside on that occasion, much idea as to the strength and importance in the religious field of the , Primitive Methodists, He remembered them preaching in the streets of Warrington 50 years or more ago when he was very young. He had been makin,, inquires as to the present standing of the body, and he found it embraced 1160 regular members, 16,259 lay preachers, 211,673 church members, 4856 chapels, 470,095 Sunday school scholars and that in addition to main- taining their ministers and general church work they had last year raised for new chapels £41,932. (Applause). The statement they had just heard from their pastor that a quarter of a million of money was to be forthwith raised by the body as a centenary thanksgiving fund was some indication of the success which had attended the Primitive Methodist Church during its hundred years existence. He was there for two reasons, to congratulate them on their past and wish them success in the future. His second reason for being present Was that he could not refuse to come when he found that his friend Mr. James Dodds was to open the Sale. He eulogised the public work of Mr. Dodds and said though he might have made enemies among a small section in the tow u, he was satisfied that before many year; had passed all War- rington would recognise in him one of their best and worthiest pnblic men. (Applause). Mr. Dodds said he hardly knew how, when, or where, he bad promised to undertake the duty attributed to him on that occasion. ' Nevertheless, it was a great pleasure to meet once more his Legh-street friends, and the pleasure was enhiinced when he found Alderman Pierpoint had consented to preside. There was no one he would have preferred to find in that postion, no one for whom he had a greater regard. The frank sincerity of his public life was known to all ; might he be long spared to serve on many similar oecasions and to sustain the public dignity of his native town. (Applause.) He had more than once refused the unanimous request of the Town Council to become Mayor, which was much to he regretted, as he would certainly have lifted the office, and gratified the whole town. (Hear, hear.) Continuing Mt. Dodds said he cherished many happy associations with Primitive Methodism. His fathers were members of the body, and some of his relations now gone had honoured recognition in its published history. Some of the most devout and saintly men and women he had ever known were Primitive Methodists. He need hardly have said therefore, that he was glad to find himself amongst its Warrington mem- bers. They were doing good work as a Free Church ; they received no enforced or State support and they would not have it if they were offered it. (Applause.) The contents of the stalls around and before him repre- sented the love of many hearts and the willing work of many hands, and he was delighted to have the oppor- tunity of wishing them success in their general work and the utmost success in the special effort which had brought them together. He had great pleasure in dechwing the sale open. (Applause.) I The Rev. J. B. BUGLASS proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Dodds and to Alderman Pierpoint for their presence and support. I This was seconded by Mr. J. GuIFFITHs, and sup- ported by Mr.J.WHITLF.v and carried by acclarnmation. The proceeds for the day amounted to £4l. THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>_ The sale was re-opened on Thursday by Mr. William Hickman, the chair being occupied by Mr. J. Griffiths. The Rev. J. B. Buglass was also present. The CHAIRMAN said he was glad to be associated with the work they were all engaged in. He thought they were doing a good work in connection with their movement but they were not so strong locally. The work was left to a few to do, but the results were encouraging and hopeful. (Hear, hear.) Mr. HICKMAN said he thought he had a bit of Primitive Methodist blood in him, because his father was a Primitive Methodist, and always worshipped in a Primitive Methodist Church. Speaking of the work of the Church, Mr. Hickman said it was the ladies who made the church prosper. Where would the sale of work have been if it were not for the devotional hearts of the ladies ? (Laughter and applause.) The Rev. J. B. BUGLASS moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman and Mr. Hickman.</p>
        <p>Mr. WHITLEY seconded, and the motion was heartily carried. _ _</p>
        <p>The total proceeds of the sale amounted to £76, including donations of £lO from Mr. Dodds and £2 from Mr. Griffiths. _ _</p>
        <p>The secretarial duties were ably discharged by Mr. Parkes and Mr. Billingharn. Concerts, arranged by Mr. A. Spruce, were given each evening and proved highly successful. The programmes included songs by Miss A. Woodland. Mrs. Warburton, Mr. Spruce, Jessie Woodland, Mr. Warburton ; duets by Messrs. Phillips and Spruce. Mr. and Mrs. Warburton ; quartettes by Miss A. Woodland, Miss E. Spruce, Mr. Phillips, Mr. Spruce; violin solos by Mr. E. Blunt; and organ solos by Mr. A. W. Clatworthy who also ably accompanied the other items.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCH CONCERT.—A successful concert and ruagio lantern entertainment was given at the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Stockton Heath, on Monday. Miss Emily Taylor's rich voice was heard to advantage in" Come Sweet Morning," and" The Fairy Sea." Mr. Herbert Plumpton was vociferously applauded for his fine rendering of " When the wild wind blows " and "Lighterman Tom." The duet by Miss Taylor and Mr. Plumpton " When the wind blows in from the sea" was heartily encored. Miss Elsie Taylor gave the violin solos Abendlied "and "Serenade " very beautifully; and Mr. John Handley's concertina was heard to great advantage in selections trom " Maritana "and ' Adeste Fideles." Mrs. Pendlebury ably presided at the piano. , Mr. George Whitfield's Lancashire dialect recitation was enthusiastically received, and he gave a sympathetic rendering of Trouble in the Amen Corner," illustrated by lantern slides. Mr. Kenneth F. Bishop delighted the large audience with some 250 views shown through the Warrington Photographic Society's excellent lantern. lany of the pictures were of unique interest, showing life in the slums. The proceeds of the concert were in aid of the building fund. STOCKTON HEATH PARISH COUNCIL. The half-yearly meeting of the parishioners of Stockton Heath was held at the Hall Cad on Thursday evening, Mr. Bouchier in the chair. RECREATION GROUND.</p>
        <p>The following letter was received from the Latchford Without Parish Council :" At a meeting of this Council, it was unanimously decided that no further offer be made." it will be remembered that Latchford Without Parish Council was approached with a view to a recreation ground being provided at Stockton Heath. The chairman said he had no remarks to make about the matter but the matter had now fallen through.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE</head>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE</p>
        <p>Mr. Deaoin said he he was going down Ellesmere-road one night at eight o'clock and there were no lamps lit. Mr. McDermid said the matter Wll.B before the Parochial Committee. A lamp from the vicarage side of the road IN ordered to be removed to the other side of the road.</p>
        <p>THE HOARDINGS.</p>
        <p>It was resolved on the motion of Jr.A Plinston that it be a recommendation to the County Council that the hoardings at the corner of Fairfield-road be removed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>IA PROGRESSIVE INDUSTRY,</head>
        <p>IA PROGRESSIVE INDUSTRY,</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE RICHMOND GAS STOVE AND METER COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY YEAR.</p>
        <p>I The seventeenth annual ordinary general meeting of the shareholders of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., was held at the offices, Academy- . street, Warrington, at noon on Thursday. Mr. Thomas Dwerryhouse (chairman), presided, and there were also present :—Messrs. Horace M. Thornton (vice-chairman and managing director), John Coxon, Warrington, J. E. Atkins, M. B.,Birmingham, John A. Ransoms, managing director, and J. W. Glover, Chelsea (directors), Mr. Louis A. Weiser, (auditor), Mr. J. \V. Slack (secretary), The Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Smethurst), Messrs. : E. J. Hall, J. Tennant, J. Plant, D. Garnett, W. Duff I and John Booth. THE REPORT. In their report the directors stated :—The balance standing to the credit of profit and loss account (after I deducting debenture interest), including the amount I brought forward as per balance sheet, is £25,501 Gs. 5d., which the directors recommend be appropriated, as follows :—Depreciation on buildings, patterns, plant, showroom fixtures, &amp;c., £5,281 Os. 2d. ; dividend on preference capital at the rate of 6 per cent, per annum, for the year ended December 31st., 1907 £5,933 35.10 d.; dividend (free of income tax) at the rate of 10 per cent. per annum on the ordinary capital, for the year ended December 31st 1907, £8,036 12s. ; bonus (free of income tax) at the rate of 21 per cent, per annum, £2,009 3s ; special reserve account to meet depreciation on invest- ments, .21,244 7s. sd. ; balance to next account subject to directors' remuneration, £3,000. Considering the high prices of raw materials and other circumstances, the business of the Company continues to be satisfac- tory, and it is gratifying to your directors that they are able, after placing entire cost of advertising as in former years against revenue, to again recommend a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent., and a bonus of 21 per cent, in addition. The Company's works and various branches are in good conidtion and have been main- tained out of revenue. THE CdAIRMAN'S REVIEW.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in moving the adoption of the report and statement of account expressed his pleasure at the Presence of the Mayor who was thus showing his interest in Warrington industries. (Hear, hear.) Most people on receiving directors' reports at ones looked to see what the dividend was. (Laughter.) Well, their dividend so far was right, for they had earned it and had paid the same dividend for some years. They would notice that the profit made last year was less, but it mast be borne in mind that it was a curious year. The price of raw materials went up and they had to suffer for it. Almost everything they dealt with cost more, but they could not get a correspondingly increased price for their goods. That would account in some measure for the decreased profit. In addition, they had built a new foundry at Drapoenhall, and the building and furnishing had properly gone to their assets. There was, however, a great deal of expense I attending the removal of machinery and stock from Stratford to the new works, and all the expenditure had to be charged against revenue. The advance in the price of coal had re-acted upon them because gas managers had had to economise and had no doubt got rid of their old stocks. While the gas managers were prob- ably saving, they (the manufacturers) were losing,as well as losing on what they themselves had purchased. He did not think the expense of the new foundry would come again, for they were now fixed and everything was in working order. Looking over the whole of the year, and having in view the extraordinary circum- stances, he thought they had done fairly well. (Hear, hear.) In 1993 the reserve fund was £17,000 ; now it was £40,000, and they had built and equipped the new foundry at large expense. Their liabilities were much the same as they were last year, the total being £290,000 as against £239,000. The additions to works, plant etc., during the year were stated in the balance sheet as £12,855 ss.and he considered that,com pared with other people in the same line, they had been very economical. Having defended the inclusion in the balance sheet of an item of £5,000 for goodwill, Mr. Dwerryhouse said the directors had devoted £1244 to a special reserve to meet depreciation on invest- ments. That was a good thing, although he hoped they would not need it. They bought most of the invest- ments at a time when they were low but they went a great deal lower. They were now pulling up again. so he expected the reserve would not be required for that purpose. They owed sundry creditors £17,000 and sundry people owed them £54,000 so that they were quite safe there. Altogether he considered it was a very good balance sheet, although they had not made as much profit, but the prospects seemed very fair and he was in hopes that they would have a much better report to present to them that time next year. (Applause.) Questions having been invited, Mr. DAVID GA.RNE TT said £54,000 seemed a large amount to have out as debts and he hoped it was all in good hands. The CHAIRMAN : It is all in good hands. I think if you examined balance sheets in the same trade you would find the same thing. I was looking at one this morning where the amount was larger than ours. THE PROGRESS OF THE GAS INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. M. THORNTON, in seconding the adoption of the report, pointed out that not only was the reduc- tion kn profit due to the high prices of materials and the fact that they had no 6 been able to get a corres- ponding increase for their goods, but they had in 1907 a most abnormal year from the point of view of their trade. Tne summer of 19)7 was one which they all hoped they wonid not get again —'laughter and " Hear, hear ")—and they in the business particularly honed they would not experience another season like it. Not only was the summer cold, but the three months ending December, when their other season was on, were abnormally mild—in fact the temperatures had shown them such a season bad not been known for years,—so that they had been handi- capped in two ways. That, Coupled with the materials being higher without a corresponding increase in the price of the article, made all the difference in the profit ns compared with previous years. He did not think they need have any feeling that the gas industry as a whole was in a decaying or dying condition. As a matter of fact, he was convinced that greater prosperity was likely in the industry than ever was known before. Ta. gas corporations and companies a more progressive spirit was . being engendered every year. There was a determination on the part of the management of the gas profession to see that the old idea that used to be in the minds of people that the industry was old- fashioned no longer existed, and he (Mr. Thornton) could point them to a large number of most important companies where the selling part of their business, the commercial side, was being taken in hand with a great deal of intelligence, and with a determination to see that that side of the gas industry was equally as good as the manufacturing side. As that side of the industry increased,so as a result would their business. Therefore it only remained for them as gas stove and meter manu- facturers to see that their article was second to none. and that they were in the position to sell it the same as any other manufacturers. He had- no hesitation in saying that their business from the manufacturing point of view was equal to any other gas stove manufacturer and certainly would be now that they had their new works. In regard to their selling power, he did not think they were boasting when they said they had as fine a staff of representa- tives as any gas stove and meter manufacturer in the Kingdom. Competition was keen, but they could face it, as long as it was fair. The trade stocks, for which his colleague, Mr. Ransome was largely responsible, showed a great deal of credit to them as a company. That figure stood at many thousond pounds more five years ago when their trade was a great deal less than it was to-day. He regarded the outstanding debts as being in every way perfectly safe and sound book debts. Both the Board and the shareholders owed a great deal of thanks to the staff,not only to the inside staff,but to all those who were in their business, for their extreme loyalty and splendid work during what had, been undoubtedly a most trying year. (Applause). The resolution was unanimously carried. THE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>The CFIAIRMAN moved : " rhat a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent, per annum (less five per cent. already paid) and a bonus of 2,4 per cent, per annum for the year ended December-31st, 1907, both free of income tax, be now declared and paid." Mr. JOHN CoxoN seconded and said that as a director he was satisfied the dividend had been thoroughly earned. They would possibly have presented a better balance sheet had raw materials been lower in price and if they bad had more favourable weather, but he did not think the shareholders would blame the directors for those causes, and it would be equally unfair for the directors to blame the management and other employees of the company. They looked forward with great confidence I to the prosperity of the concern and he (lid not see why itwelve months hence they should not present as good a balance sheet as they had done that day. The resolution was carried. DIRECTORS' REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM Drier proposed : :` That the sum of £7OO be placed at the disposal of the directors for their services during the year 1907." The directors, he said, had worked admirably during the past year and when they considered the great amount spent on the Grappeuhall works the present position of the company spoke very well for them. He was sure there was every prospect of good yeirs in the future for the company. lhe Bill which was before Parliament with reference to the eight hours' movement would no doubt increase the price of Coal if it passed, hut he was glad to see their old friend Mr. George Livesey appeared before Mr. Gladstone and tried to explain to him the evils of the Bill. He (Mr. Duff) bad no doubt from what Mr. Gladstone said, that everything would be taken into consideration before the Bill came before the House again. Mr. JOHN BOOTH seconded, and congratulated the directors on the successful working of the past year. The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>For Influenza Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/11 and 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>Dr. ATKINS returned thanks, and said the directors were keenly alive to the interests of the shareholders, for whom they were always anxious to earn and pay as large a dividend as possible. The directors never had a firmer grip of the concern than at the present time and, in medical parlance, he would say that under the professorship of their worthy Chairman, they were always dissecting the anatomy and vital parts of the company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF AUDITOR. THE MAYOR AND THE RATES.</head>
        <p>THE APPOINTMENT OF AUDITOR. THE MAYOR AND THE RATES.</p>
        <p>The ikavott proposed :—" That Mr. L. A. Voisey be re-appointed auditor of the company for the year 1908 at a remuneration of He spoke in compli- mentary terms of the ability and tact which IVfr. Voisey had placed at the service of the company, and said the shareholders could rest assured that as long as he occu- pied that position, the accounts would be thoroughly audited. The shareholders had reason to be highly gratified at the success of the concern. Corporations . . . .</p>
        <p>in particular were suffering from the rise in the price of coal, and he would be delighted if he could hold out to the inhabitants such a glowing prospect of a reduc- tion of rates as could be held out of another satisfactory dividend to the shareholders of that company next year. tie did not want to forestall anybody, but he was afraid he could not hold out a prospect of a reduction in rates, and it was all through the advance in the price of coal and other materials they had to use. The balance sheet of that company was gratifying all round, and considering they had so many customers the amount of outstanding debts was not unduly large. The CHAIRMAN said the directors were quite s^.tis- fled with the work of Mr.......Voisey—in fact his work</p>
        <p>could not be better done. He had. introduced many; improvements in book-keeping which made it far easier for the staff. He might say that the bad debts for the year amounted to only £B6 15s. 3d. Mr. GARNETT It is very satisfactory. Mr. E. J. HALL in seconding said that to further eulogise Mr. Voisey's services would be like gilding refined gold. The directors were to be congratulated on many features of the balance sheet, in fact he con- sidered it was a very mode‘t one. In a great many balance sheets the item" freehold and leasehold works and other buildings, patterns, plants and fixtures " was made to include the total amount which had been spent for these purposes since the incorporation of the company. The directors of Messrs. Richmond however adopted a different method, and they only stated the value of the works and plant as they stood at the present time, although they had been providing for depreciation every year. Another notable feature was that the paid- up capital (.C1.81,471) was practically balanced by the amount set down for works, plant, fixtures, and invest- ments. He did not know whether, in view of the fore- shadowed legislation to which they were told to look forward from their Socialist friends, it would be wise for the directors to go on writing off depreciation and providing for reserve fund. In view of all those promises or threats—whether they were benefited or attacked by their fulfilment—it might be wise some day for the directors to say : "We won't depreciate any longer and we won't carry any more to the reserve, but all we make we will divide as soon as we get it." (Laughter). The CHAIRMAN : I hope that will be a long time off. (Hear, bear.) The resolution was carried. Mr. VOISEY in returning thanks said that during the audit the accounts had been most minutely examined and he heartily complimented the staff on the very excellent way in which their work had been done. VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>A vote of thank i was accorded to the Chairman and directors on the proposition of the INIA.Yon, seconded by Mr GARNETT. A similar compliment was paid to,the officials on the motion of Mr. BOOTH seconded by Mr. PLANT. Mr. RANSONIE responded and said he was sure all the officials were d )ing their best. He trusted the com- pany would continue to meet with that success which had been so evidently shown in the balance sheets for the last three or four years. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>INFIRMARY EXTENSION. PROGRAMME OF NEXT THURSDAY'S OPENING CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>INFIRMARY EXTENSION. PROGRAMME OF NEXT THURSDAY'S OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>Thursday neat will be a red letter day in the history of the Warrington Infirmary, for it will witness the opening of the new buildings by the Right Hon. Viscount Cross, G. 0.8. who, it is interesting to note, opened the Infirmary on January 24th, 1877. The occasion is naturally being looked forward to with considerable pleasure as the consummation of a great undertaking which has been so much before the , public mind during the last two years, and every effort will be put forth to make the proceedings worthy of such a memorable occasion. The pro- gram is as follows : 1.15 o'CLocK.—Reception at the Town Hall of Lord Cross, by the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. , Smethurst), and the President and representatives of the Infirmary. A procession will be formed in front of the Town Hall, an the following order : 1. Volunteer Band. 2. Board of Management. 3. Conveyances as allotted. The procession will go through the Central Gates and alone. Sankey-street and Legh street to the main door of the Infirmary in Kendrick-street. Lora Cross and party will proceed through the ' Infirmary to the verandah on the ground floor of the New Pavilion.</p>
        <p>1.30 o'Ctocx. —l. Band and People—Hymn, " Thou to Whom the sick and dying." (During the singing of the hymn a collection will be taken.) 2. Prayer by the Rector. 3. Sir Gilbert Greenall will present to Lord Cross a key, and will request him to onen the door. 4. Lord Cross to speak. 5. Ceremony of opening. 6 Mr. W. Peter Rylands will propose, and the Rev. Harry V. Pigot will second, a vote of thanks to Lord Cross for his services on the occasion. 7. L)rd Cross will reply, and, followed by the com- pany present, will proceed the New Ward Volunteer Band will play the "National Anthem" and immediately afterwards Lord Cross and Party will proceed to the Lecture Room, Volunteer Quarters, where, by the kind permission of Colonel J. A. Edelsten, YD., and the Officers of the Ist V.B. South Lancashire Regiment, a luncheon will be provided at nuarter.past tam, for ladies and gentlemen. Tickets ss. each, exclusive of wine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CONGREGATIONAL UNION. Women's Guild of Christian Service. Address by Mrs. Yonge.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CONGREGATIONAL UNION. Women's Guild of Christian Service. Address by Mrs. Yonge.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this guild was held on Wednesday last, in connection with the annual meeting of the above Union, in Chorley Old-road Congregl- tional Church,Bolton. The chair was occupied by Mrs. Yonge, of Wycliffe. Warrington, who is the President of the Women's Guild for this year. The report was read by Miss Olivia Jones, of Waterloo, Liverpool, who stated that there are now 24 branches of the Guild, and encouraging accounts were given of the growth and usefulness of the various branches.</p>
        <p>The PRE'3IDENI' (Mrs. Yonge) then delivered an address in the course of which she said that the Guild is a comparatively new organization, but " Christian service" is nothing new. Indeed, looking back on her own life, even from girlhood, she said it was what she had ever desired and sought, notwithstanding many hindrances and obstacles. But many of these had been overcome, and she had been able to take some share in various forms of work for Christ and the Church. Christian service should not be considered the duty merely of a few leisured, or cultured, or gifted women, hut of every woman who realizes that she has been redeemed by Christ. Many, however, are hindered by a mistaken idea of what Christian service means. It is often urged that " people are not all alike ; what is possible for one is not so for another." One woman says: " I cannot speak at meetings, or teach in a Sunday school, or lead in prayer at a women's meeting." These, however, are iii tilculties which disapoear before earnest attempt. qrs. Yonge said she had passed through all those experi- ences of inability and reluctance, and had overcome them. She found that sometimes when she felt least qualified she had been made most useful. She quoted instances which illustrated her meaning. The thing is to make ourselves willing to 13.3 of service : and if we do that, the power will come. The value of a guild is that it links us together in a fellowship the very soul of which is service, mutual help, mutual conference, mutual endeavour. All the churches would be the better for such an organiza- tion. What a help the women of the churches could become to the ministers, providing them with a sort of Volunteer Army, noon which they could call for help. The nucleus for a Guild should be easily found from the existing workecs in a Church or school. The Great Master asks that the women who love him should be willing to do all that they can for His sake. The President's address was followed be an interest- ing address from Sister Selwyn, of tho Salford Central Mission. The meeting was one of the best attended which has yet been held, and its interest was greatly increased by the asking and answering of various ques- tions after the addresses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>NASAL CATARRH &amp; ASTHMA.</head>
        <p>NASAL CATARRH &amp; ASTHMA.</p>
        <p>THEIR COMPLETE HOME CURE. POST FREE TO SUFFERERS WHO APPLY AT ONCE. Just published, the second edition of a 48 page book fully describing Nasal Catarrh, Asthma and Chronic Bronchitis, and how these dangerous diseases may be completely cured at home. The most practical work ever issued, and should be in the hands of sufferers who desire to speedily cure themselves. This offer stands open for 30 days from this date- A post card giving your name and address will bring you the book free by return post. Address Dept. B. 341, Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd., Ayton Buildings, King St., West, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Support for the Licensing Bill.</p>
        <p>There was a large and enthusiastic gathering at the annual meeting of the General Council of the Warring- ton Liberal Association, which was held at the Liberal Club on Monday evening. Mr. Charles E. Parker (president) presided, and amongst those present were : —Alderman Tinnion, Councillors Locker (hon. secre- tary), Wilkinson and Hawthorn, Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Messrs. W. Furness (hon. treasurer), J. Howes, jour., (agent), E. England, N. Cook, W. Bibby, T. Pemberton, J. Critchley, P. Peacock, T. Critchley, W. Clegg, S. Wright, and H. W. Guest. Mr. C. Jackson Holmes wrote intimating that as he had undertaken duties in connection with the Liberal Association of the Knutsferd division, in which he resided, it would preclude his working for the Warring- ton Liberal Association, and he therefore asked them to accept his resignation. Mr. P. O'Brien, in the course of a letter regretting his inability to attend, wrote :—" There is no cause for discouragement in recent events, only the necessity of putting a little more stiffening into our principles and doing our best to help on and encourage a Government who are doing their best to legislate in the interests of the masses."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Gordon Roberis also sent a letter of apology for non-attendance. PRESIDENT RE-ELECTED. THE TESTING TIME OF' LIBERALISM.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. T. RUSHTON moved the re-election of Mr. C. E. Parker as president of the Association, and said he did not think they could find an abler man in Warring- ton to represent the Liberals. (Applause). Mr. MYLES' seconded and said Mr. Parker was undoubtedly the right man in the right place. Councillor LOCKER in supporting said it would be impossible to find another gentleman even equal to Mr. Parker to occupy the position.</p>
        <p>„ The resolution was carried with acclammation.</p>
        <p>Mr. PARKER said he accepted the election with very great pleasure. He was, in a way, rowing stroke oar in the election two years ago. At that time the wind and tide were with them—ft was practically, although they did not know it at the time, a walk over. He would like to be in the boat with them when the con- ditions were not so favourable because he liked a thorough good fight. There was no doubt that, so far as Liberalism was concerned, they were coming to a testing time. (Hear, hear.) The flood tide which carried them in at the last election would not be with them. There was the natural swing of the pendulum which the indifferent voter managed to move,and the fact that for the public good the Government was reducing the power of different monopolies and interests had raised a great deal of opposition—opposition which was of an interested character. It was always a diffi- culty when they had an impulse for reform springing from a desire for the public good and when that impulse was set against those who were working for their own hand. It required that they as Liberals in the testing time that was coming should hold together and hold high the great principles which were for the benefit of the whole of the people and not of mere trusts and monopolies. It would be a time which would try the Liberal Association in Warrington. Great forces of power, influence and wealth would be arrayed against the interests of the people and it would show whether there were in this town a sufficient number who cared more for the public good than for private interest. He trusted that he would be in that or some other position in the ranks so that he would have the joy and pleasure of being in the fight when it might be that the stress of wind and storm was against them. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>OTHER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>OTHER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>!", • ~,„;,t'.-L-_,t;:..7J41</p>
        <p>Mr. JAMES CRITCHLEY moved the re-ejection of the vice-presidents :—Messrs. A. H. Orosfleld, 'MP., Frank A. Roberts' Ziba Armitage E. J. Guest, Alderman W. Pierpoint, Henry Roberts, D. Tinnion, and W. Madden. Mr. CROSKILL seconded. The proposition was agreed to and it was also decided to add Mr. William Bibby's name to the list. Mr. BIBBY proposed the re-election of Mr. W. Furness as hon. treasurer and said he had carried out the duties most ably. He had been a regular attender at the meet- ings and had done everything a man could be expected to do in that office. MT PETER PEACOCK seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. FURNESS said he had held the office for ten years and had determined to resign. Alderman TINNION moved the election of Mr. Thomas Pemberton as treasurer. He referred to his services as treasurer of the Liberal Club and said that as treasurer and book-keeper he had not his superior in Narrington. Mr. S. WRIGHT seconded and the motion was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. PENIII3ERTON intimated his willingness to under- take the duties.</p>
        <p>A cordial vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Furness for his long and valuable services. On the motion of Mr. J. WILLETT seconded by Kr. VlASSEY,Councillor Locker was re-elected hon.secretary. Councillor LOCKER said his first year of office had been a very pleasant one and had witnessed some little progress on the part of the Association. There were responsibilities attaching to the position and he felt he was only just getting into harness. Messrs. Joseph Wilkinson, James Jarman and Frank Plumpton were elected co-optative members of the General Council. The following ladies were elected to the Council to represent the Women's Liberal Association Parker, Mrs. Osborne, Mrs. Walker, Mrs. Clegg, Mrs. F. Mao6inn, Mrs. T. Locker. The meeting also elected Mrs. Parker, Mrs. Osborne and Mrs. Walker members of the Executive Committee. SUPPORT FOR THE LICENSING BILL.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mrs. Osborne stating that the Women's Liberal Association had passed a resolution expressing their approval of the Licensing Bill and urging the advisability of a public meeting being held, at which they would give their support. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK said he had come from a very interesting meeting, called to form a temperance association in Warrington, and he wanted to ask that the Liberal party would present a bold and solid front on the question of the Licensing Bill. Mr. Balfour might have done a little right but there was no question that he did a great wrong by his Act with reference to the licensing question. They were to-day faced with this question : They were either to be controlled by or control- the liquor traffic. He could not understand why businessmen should allow one trade to he protected and to be a monopoly whilst all the trades in which they earned their living were free trades. Why should the brewer not be subject to the same government inquiry into what he made his goods from as the man who sold butter? It should he laid 1 down what was pure beer and what was not. He did not want to take away a man's glass of beer. He thought the man who would turn the traffic out hag and baggage was a little unwise, but at the same time he held that the strong ought to protect the weak, and in this matter he pleaded that the Liberal party as a body and not only as individuals should show a solid and bold front. They must do a great right, even if they did a little wrong in accomplishing it. Tnere was much talk as to the effect the Bill would have upon brewing shares and brewers generally. The cost which the trade entailed on the country seemed to escape the attention of those gentlemen. Supposing that in Warrington they bad half the public-houses that they had to-day, they would have a decreased crime bill, a decreased police force, and decreased asylum accommodation, and the cost of these things would be gradually curtailed. Therefore to suppose that by touching the liquor traffic they were going to upset trade, make things bad, and bring about a state of bankruptcy, seemed to him to be talking rather wide of the mark. The cost of the trade was one side of the questions of temperance which he thought they did not sufficiently bear in mind. If they struck at once, continued Mr. Cook, their strength would amount to nine times what it be three months hence. The fourteen years' time limit in the new Bill was very ample justice. Of course, it was true to say that the licences had never been in the hands of the nation. It was never thought to make it legal and to say that they were in the hands of the nation. For years licences had been granted and if there were no complaints against the houses they were renewed. It was never thought that the licences belonged to the people. The matter was brought to a head by Mr. Bal roar and he was able to show this to be a vested interest and got his bill carried. It was no use talking about getting the licences back to the people ; because practically the people had never had the licences. Therefore the brewers were frankly logical in saying the licences had never been the property of the public. " But we bave to get them," continued Mr. Cook," and make them the property of the public so that we shall, as the people, reap the benefit that comes from them. We are in a very severe struggle. We are in the throes of one of those gladiatorial exhibitions when, if we do not throw, we shall be thrown." (Applause.) The CHAIRMAN said that for four hundred years at least the right of licence was with the people. The case of Sharpe v. Wakefield settled that beyond dis- pute. The great mistake was when Mr. Balfour in the Act of 1904 sacrificed and handed over the privilege of granting the licence for one year and made it into perpetuity. The Liberal Association, continued Mr. Parker, should approach the question as citizens rather than as teetotallers or abstinence men, because the question was a citizen's question. It was a question of the people's rights which were infringed by the existence of licences. It threw certain burdens upon the population. The extra number of licences meant increased police supervision and all the costs inci- dental to the regulation of the traffic. Further, it affected the valise of the property surrounding any licensed house. Although he should deprecate the question being brought op from a total abstinence point of view, they wanted to feel that whether they drank or not, they as the people should control the trade and limit that monopoly in so far as it was injurious to the public well being. The ques- tion with : What should they do? His opinion was</p>
        <p>that they should have a demonstration of all the force in the town who believed that the Bill was a fair and just one. The Labour party were almost to a man in favour of the measure. In the Church of England the Bishops of Birmingham, London and Hereford and several others had spoken out strongly on the question. Therefore he thought it would be well for them to have a united demonstration, apart from what was purely Liberalism, which would gather together all the forces that made for sobriety and the well-being of the people in the town. They wanted to correct the grossly. exaggerated and one-sided statements which were going about the town. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. C. HARVEY Cool( said that prior to the Sharpe v. Wakefield case, there was never a decision given that the licences belonged to the people. Up to that time it was thought that the licence was a kind of vested interest. He moved : "That this meeting of the Warrington Liberal Association desires to express its entire approval of the Licensing Bill introduced by Mr. Asquith and pledges itself to give it its enthusiastio support." (Applause). Alderman TINNION in seconding said they as Liberals sent the Government into power among other things to deal with the licensing question and to his mind, Mr. Asquith had brought in a bill that every fair- minded man would admit was a reasonable basis on which to settle this vexed question. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was heartily carried and it was decided to send a copy to the Borough Member. Mr. ENGLAND moved that the question of future action be referred to the Executive Committee, witla a view to holding a demonstration as suggested by the I President.</p>
        <p>Councillor HAWTHORN seconded and this was also agreed to.</p>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL. Engineers' Secession. The monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Independent Labour Party rooms, Friars-gate, on Tues day evening, the president (Mr. W. H. Richardson) in the chair. MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P., AND OLD-AGE PENSIONS. The following letter was received from Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P. :—" Many thanks for your favonr embodying the resolution passed by you with regard to Old-Age Pensione which has my careful consideration." MUNICIPAL COAL. A letter was read from the Flint Glassworkers stating that the following resolution was passed at their quarterly meeting held on February 15th :—" That this meeting support the Warrington Trades and Labour Council in their resolution to bring pressure to bear upon the Town Council to adopt the principle of a municipal coal supply, believing that it will be a benefit to the ratepayers and residents of the town." THE BAKERS. The CHAIRMAN, Mr Plumb and Mr.'Haley waited on the Bakers last Saturday evening with a view to inducing them to re-consider their decision teleave the Council. i Mr. PLUMB said the bakers did not come to a definite I decision at the meeting and the chief reasons for the i secession were stated to be that the members were against paying a shilling per hour to the representatives , on the local bodies, as stated in the rules. The other reason was the amount of the contribution. The deputation did all they could to persuade the bakers to re-consider their decision and they promised that a full report would be sent in to the secretary of the Trades Council but it had not yet arrived. Mr. HALEY endorsed all that Mr. Plumb had said. The bakers thought the shilling per hour to representa- tives on local authorities was too high. He (Mr. . Haley) pointed out to them that work of that descrip- tion was not ordinary work. They all remembered that - when that matter was discussed the Council were not absolutely unanimous in their decision. It was not a , large matter for the bakers to withdraw for. HOUSING REFORM. 1 A letter was read from the National Housing Reform Council asking the Council for their sympathy and support in their efforts to abolish slums and inviting I them to send delegates to a meeting to be held in the Y..M.C.A. Hall, Manchester, this Saturday afternoon. ' 1 Mr. LEACH said the question was a most serious one and be moved that the Council send either one or two ' delegates to that meeting. 1 Mr. OWEN seconded. I Mr. SEED asked what benefit the Housing Reform 1 Council would be to them as trade unionists? They were there to support trade unionism. Mr. OWEN thought it would be of great benefit to them as trade unionists. (Hear, hear.) The CHAIRMAN said that they as trade unionists wanted decent dwellings and they must do all in their power for the benefit of the workingmen. They would I get great benefit from the movement in his opinion. , Mr..‘lootteiELD said that next to Old-Age Pensions the ' i housing question was the man point for workingmen. It was time some movement was begun to abolish the , pigstyes in which some people had to live. (Hear, hear.) It could not do them any harm to help forward such a humane movement to abolish those properties which ought not to have been built. Mr. PLINSTON said that movement was in no way a Socialist movement. (Hear, hear.) He thought that those people who lived in the slums were to be pitied. I They made it part of their duty to see that workrooms I were sanitary and they must see that sanitary houses I were built. Mr. DARBYsIIIRE supported the resolution. They were twitted as a Trades Council with being of little or no use. He thought to support housing reform was 1 the best thing they could do. Mr. PLUMB also supported. If they were not there to discuss those matters he did not know what they were there for. He felt somewhat surprised at the way people referred to the Socialist party. There WB3 ' I no man in that Council who could say he had protested ,! r against any resolution which had been sent by members , of any political party, if it was for the benefit of trade 4 unionists. Mr. HALEY said they wanted to have proper houses to live in. Some of the houses built thirty years ago were considerably better houses than some of those built six ' years ago. It was not a Tory or a Liberal question. It I was a national question. (Hear, hear.) Speaking on the way in which some of the houses were built, he said he knew of a man who went into a house drunk, fell through the ii.'all and into the next house. (Laughter.) Mr. SEF.D disclaimed any intention of casting retlec. tions on Socialists. The resolution was carried unanimously, Messrs. Richardson and Plumb being appointed delegates. SHOULD LOCAL BODIES USE THE TOWN HALL? Mr. MOORFIELD then raised the qu;stion of public bodies meeting at the Town Hall, and maintained that the privilege of assembling there ought to be granted to the Trades Council and similar bodies. He moved that the Trades Council approach the town authorities and ask them to grant them the use of those premises for their monthly meetings. Mr. BosTocx seconded. Mr. DARBYSHIRE supported. The SECRETARY said that for the life of him be could not understand trades unionists standing on their dignity. They would not benefit one iota by meeting at the Town Hall. He opposed the resolution although as secretary he was not allowed to vote. Mr. LEACH said if they were to p,et the use of a room for nothing he was going to support it very strongly. Councillor STOTT said it did not speak very well for the religious bodies of Warrington who claimed that they were interested in the moral and spiritual welfare of others. If they were so interested they would provide rooms for such an organization as that Council for nothing, but as it was they charged them for the privilege. Mr. Moorfield's resolution was a step in the right direction. They were a representative body. Mr. SEED said they were ratepayers and they had &amp; claim for the right to use the municiparbuildings. The resolution wes carried with one dissentient. HULL CONFERENCE. Councillor STOTT presented a very interesting report on the Labour Party Conference at Hall. This was adopted and the best thanks of the meeting were accorded to him on the motion of Mr. Ptumn,seconded by Mr. OWEN. ENGINEERS' SECESSION. A letter was read from the Amalgamated Society of Engineers which stated that they would withdraw from the Council at the end of the quarter. Councillor Stott is the delegate from the engineers. The SECRETARY said some of the members thought that Socialism was dominating the Council, whie statement- was absolutely without fact. Councillor STOTT said he had been a member of the' Council for ten years. He did not think that any mein. her had taken a keener interest in it than he had. (Hears hear). It was decided, as the letter stated, that the engineers should withdraw at the end of the quarter. There was no definite reason given for leaving the" Council. If they adhered to their decision, he woulst have to resign the position of president of the Federation - After dealing with one or two remarks which the engineers had made, Councillor Stott referred to the criticism of the Trades Council uhich had appeared I, the local Press. It was not fair to criticise ar individual in such a severe manner ; especially as tt writer or writers used nom-do-plume. He hoped the. Council would progress in spite of the criticisms ane' although he would not be able to attend the meeting he would take a keen interest in the work they di( (Applause.) It was moved, seconded and carried that the he thanks of the meeting be accorded to Councillor Stotl for his past services. A deputation, consisting of Messrs. Owen,Richa, and the secretary was appointed to wait noon engineers and ask them to re-consider their decision.</p>
        <p>Deaths. SA MBROOK.—On Mardi 6tb,at 56,Mount Pleasant-1 Sheffield, George Sambrook, Organiser, Natic Amalgamated Union of Enginemen„ aged 60 yen Interred at City-road Cemetery, Sheffield, March 111</p>
