        <p>Fact versus Fiction. The fiction is that another cocoa "as good as Van Houten's." The fact is there is not another cocoa to equal Van Houten's. In every market in the world it is the standard by which all other cocoas are judged. Van Houten's, Best &amp; Goes Farthest.</p>
        <p>DRESS—FASHION—HOME. THE LINGERIE BLOrSE. In this bitter colds it is amazing, writes our Paris correspondent, to see with what persistency the mondaine clings to her lingerie blouse, which is made every day more and more fragile. Tulle, of the illusion quality, is not too fairy-like, and is daintily tucked and ruched, inset with Irish and Valenciennes, ending in a ruche, high about the ears. It is cravatted with a velvet bow of some decided colour, which encircles the throat and knots low in front, amidst, or below, th• t jabots. By the way, a very decided modish re- vival is the high collar. Becoming as was the picturesque little bodice, which seems, on your , side of the Channel, to have been nicknamed the "pneumonia blouse," the craze for bare necks assumed most absurd proportions, and the high stiff collar always gives a more distinguished, full-dressed look to a feminine figure. Still, the influence of the turn-over collarette is felt, for we are making most of our collars of transparent materials, and, when it is possible, of real lace. A SMART LITTLE COAT. The little separate coat of light-coloured cloth has come back to fashion once more, and pro- mises to be one of the smartest and most popular garments of the corning spring. The charming example pictured in our sketch is very typical of the latest ideas on the subject, and is carried out in fine cloth of a pale and particularly modish shade of cinnamon. This little coat is cut to fit most accurately at the back and sides, but with a distinctly semi-fitting effect in front. From ~.....---------__- •:::••-*1) • .. ... •,-.,,f - _ „ • . •-:-. _ \ • . , '. F. , , i ifi I Titilh • it A i //// IN 0 , l'. ! (11 t ' L i I</p>
