ssued a manifesto to his become law. It is not a Bill that will settle the diocese, in which he declares that " as a specimen of educational problem, and no Bill will, unless justice is Class legislation, of unscrupulous rapacity, and of given to the Church which has been the pioneers of all religious intolerance in the teentieth century," Mr. education in the past, and the bitter opponents of the McKenna's Bill will" deserve a place in historical Church to-day owe in a great measure their education archives by the side of racks thumbscrews, boots, and to the Church that they are trying to cripple and other engines of torture." it appears that all along hinder in her good work. . The Bishop knew what would happen. "I warned 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds You," he says, "in the election of 1900 to expect Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Adve robbery and confiscation of your schools." It is quite , Boy's Theft. comforting to know that to our other sins is not added n that of breach of faith. At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, James Clarke, aged nine years, who lives with his parents at ...._ 11, Beatrice-street, was charged with stealing the sum of 10e. from the person of Samuel James Bell, aged seven year 3, in Knutsford-road, on Saturday morning. —The Chief Constable said a small boy named Samuel James Bell, was sent by his mother to purchase some goods. When in Knutsford-road, defendant snatched the purse containing half a sovereign from the lad, and ran away. Defendant was seen on Satur- , day evening by Detective Metcalfe, and he admitted . having taken the money. The sum of 9s. 3d. was found in his pockets. He said he had spent 9d. in sweets. He thought the boy did not realize the seriousness of the offence, and it was probably done in a childish freak. —The Mayor gave the boy a severe warning, as well as some admirable advice, and he was then discharged. , TRY GILLETTE SAFETY RAZORS for comfort in shaving.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge- 1 street:Warrington.—Advt. , New Curate of St. Barnabas' Church. The Rev. J. W. Glendenning, the newly-appointed I curate of St. Barnabas' Church, commenced his duties on Sunday. He occupied the pulpit at the evening service and preached a powerful and well-reasoned sermon from the words: " For the Jews require a sign ; and the Greeks seek after wisdom ; but we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumbling block and unto the Greeks foolishness; but unto them which are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God." He referred to some of the forces which were arrayed against Christianity to day and said there was less to fear from the opposition of a blatant agnosticism than from the indifference of nominal Christendom. Mr. Glendenning is an M.A. of Corpus Christi College, Cambridge, and took second class iii the theological tripos, gaining distinction in Hebrew. PUBLIC BENEFIT—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The Late Miss Piernoint. The remains of the late Miss Pierpoint (sister of Mr. Robert Pierpoiat, of St. Austin's, Warrington), were laid to rest on Saturday afternodn in St. Elphin's churchyard. The principal mourners were :—Mr. Robert Pierpoint (brother), Mrs. Pierpoint (sister-in- law), Mr. Charles Pierpoint Brabazon, of Bath (nephew), Dr. Burrowes, Mrs. Burrowes (niece), her devoted Miss Schussler, and Miss Ann Evans for over 30 years housekeeper at St. Austin's. Tne officiating clergymen were the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton and T. A. J. Ridpath. As the mourners entered the Church Mr. Darby gave an impressive rendering of Chopin's Funeral &amp;latch, and as they left the building the Dead March in "Saul" was played. By request no wreaths were sent, but a cross of ivy gathered from Mr. Pierpoint's own garden and made up by Mrs. Pierpoint was placed on the coffin. At the usual service on the following morning at the Parish Church, the organist, at the conclusion of the service, played the Dead March in "Saul " the congregation standing as a mark of respect. For Chronic Chest Complaints, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Adyt. Buckley-street P.S.A. Society. The tenth anniversary was celebrated on Monday evening when the annual distribution of prizes took place in the church. Each member and friend on entering was heartily welcomed by Mr. W. H. Woolley, vice-president. It proved the largest gathering since the formation of the society, there being about 350 members and friends present, and a portion had to be accommodated in the schoolroom. Mr. J. Withinshaw, of Penketh, occupied the chair,and gave a very interesting address. During the evening, the Co-operative Glee Society, under the conductor- ship of Mr. A. H. Trow, gave flue renderings of " Comrades in arms," and " Remember me." Miss M. Plinston contributed a pianoforte solo in capital style, and she also acted as accompanist throughout the evening. At the close of the meeeing in the church, Mr. Withinshaw distributed 150 prizes to the members, and complimented the Society on the fine selection of prizes that were given. An adjournment was made to the schoolroom, where refreshments were provided. A specially augmented orchestral band, under the leadership of Mr. James Scott, rendered some fine selections of music. Miss A. Woodland (soprano) gave a solo in excellent style. 'LINSEED COMPOUND 'of 40 years' proven ecffica for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt. Finest DANISH BUTTER, is. 2d. lb. at ASHLEY'S.—Adv Death of Mr. George Wright. We regret ta anuounce the death of Mr. George Wright, the popular landlord of the Royal Court Hotel, Rylands-street, who passed away in the early hours of Saturday after a very brief illness. Mr. Wright, who was well known in Warrington and Cheshire, was born at Sandiway in 1845, and was the second son of the late Mr. William Wright, of Tooler Stone Farm, Sandiway, who was bailiff to Major Littledale, Sandiway Bank, for 50 years, Mr. George Wright being a volunteer in the force commanded by the Major (original corps). He was educated at 'Weaverham Grammar School under Mr. Griffithe. Upon leaving school he went to Mr. Smith, of Hodge- lane Brewery, for whom he started travelling at the early age of 17, taking as his district the whole of .Cheshire. Mr. Smith was one of the gentlemen who inaugurated the Delamere Forest Races, then a small meeting which has since developed into the famous Tarporley Hunt. Mr. Wright assisted him at the first meeting, and from that time until three years ago never missed a meeting. Upon Mr. Smith's retirement Mr. Wright joined the late Mr. Edward S. Woolf at the South Cheshire Brewery, Crewe. He came to Warrington in 1875, joining his brother-in-law, Mr. Maxfield, of the Borough Brewery. He afterwards took over the Royal Court Hotel, then 'called the Public Hall Hotel, and completed his 28th year of tenancy on March Ist, 1908.—The funeral took place at Weaverhatn Churchyard on Monday , afternoon. The c9reege left the late residence of the deceased at 12.30, and time following members of the , Licensed Victuallers' Association followed in carriages I to the borough boundary :—Alderman John Evans (president), Mr. Joseph Shillcock (vice-president), Mr. Robert Shacklady (huu. treasurer), Councillor Rogers ' and Messrs. Thomas Partridge, John Lee, A. E. I Denham, Peers Britnlow, Robert Walker,N.Hankinson, ' P. E. Lake, J. E. Shaw, George Finch, Thomas Weir, I George Sitncock, Thomas Cooper, J. McClinton, T. Walsh, J. West, W. Turnbull, and E. Lockett (secre- tary). Councillor W. B. Roberts represented Messrs. ' Greenall, Wbitlev and Co., Ltd. and Mr. George Turley represented Messrs. G. and J. Greenall, Ltd. Mr. Ashton Hayes was also preseqt.</p>
