        <p>I THE NORTHERN RUGBY .UNION. 1 1 THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE. G. T. P. G. T. P. Oldham* Hunslet* Barrow* York* .. Wakefield T.* 2 2 10 Batley Wigan Hull E. Rovers* 2 0 4 Leigh Rochdale H. Warrington* Huddersfield* Runcorn* 0 2 6 Swinton 1 1 5 Broughton 11.*... 4 4 20 Ebbw Vale Halifax 1 3 11 Salford* Warrington Overwholm The Hull team paid their annual visit to Warrington on Saturday and were beaten in overwhelming fashion by the Cup-holders, before • crowd of 5,000 spectators. Both teams were at full strength with the exception that the respective full-backs, Taylor and Tille, were nursing injuries. Beautiful sunny weather prevailed and on the firm turf at Wilderapool a very fast game was witnessed. Teams:— Warrington—E. Jordan, back ; J. Fish, G. Dickenson, I. Taylor and E. Watkin, three-quarter backs ; J. Lloyd and E. Brooks, half-backs ; F. Shugars, P. G. Jenkins, G. Thomas, W. Belton, A. Naylor, and G. Heath, forwards. Hull—Rogers, back ; Parry, Cook, Darcy and Castles, three-quarter backs ; Anderson and Wallace, half backs; Ilerridge, Holder, Fulton, Owen, Major and Carroll, forwards. Referee, Mr. G. F. Dickenson, Halifax. Touch judges, Messrs. B. Jones, Widnes, and B. Onion, Wigan. ahe home team started against the wind but very soon demonstrated their superiority. A fine bit of work by Fish resulted in Dickenson scoring the opening try before the game was five minutes old. Hull next attacked, and Wallace and Anderson opened out the game smartly only to see the movement break down at Cook. Another hot raid by the home forwards let in Heath, who scored after a run from near half-way. Lack of finish spoiled many favourable openings for the visitors, and after Anderson and Parry had been pulled up near the lino, Brooks led the way to the Hall quarters, and Fish following up a kick, raced in behind the posts, also adding a goal. Hull rallied in effective fashion, and Wallace snd Anderson again opening out the game, Parry scored, Wallace missing the goal. At half-time Warrington led by 1 goal and 3 tries (11 points), to Hull 1 try (3 points). With the wind in their favour the " Wirepullers " made strong play, and Naylor scored over the posts. Hull continued to be hard pressed, and Jenkins crossed, the scorer also adding the extra points. Watkin next scored at the corner,and in response to a fine movement by the Hull forwards was practically responsible for Wallace placing a penalty goal. Warrington continued .on the aggressive, and tries were added by Heath, Dickenson and Watkin, Thomas placing two goals and Brooks one goal. Hull had a turn, when Major put on the finishing touches to capital exchanges. in the last five minutes Fish and Taylor both left the field injured, but Warrington's superior play behind resulted in a try even with only eleven men. Result :—Warrington 5 peals and nine tries (47 points), Hull 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points). LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. LEIGH SHAMROCKS V. WARRINGTON "A."—This return match was played at Leigh on Saturday, and after an exciting game in which the exposition was of a high order, Warrington was two tries (six points) to a goal and a try (five points). In the first half Shawcross scored a try for the home side, which Stirrup improved upon, whilst near the close P. Boardman crossed for Warrington, but the goal-kick was a failure. In the second half the Warrington backs showed superior form and Tiuckler gaining an unconverted try, the game ended as above stated. ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. _ __— WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. , Goals. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. Buttonwood A.F.O l6 15 1 0 59 13 30 Oughtrington Park l3 11 1 1 54 13 23 Fairfield Palatine Lyrnm Ryland' Res. Latchford Warrington Central *Burtonwood Social l2 2 6 4 17 38 6 Warrington St. Paul's l5 312 0 19 50 6 Monks Hall *Two points deducted. SECOND Divistos. Orford A.F.O Warrington St. John's l5 8 4 3 37 23 19 St. Elphin's Oughtrington Park Res l2 5 4 3 35 18 13 Walkers Warrington St. Mary's l5 5 8 1 16 41 11 Warrington Central Res l2 3 6 3 26 28 9 Arpley A.F.O l3 310 0 26 36 9 Fairfield Reserves 9 0 5 4 10 18 4 Matches for March 14th. Oughtrington Park T. Rylands. Lyrum v. Burtonwood Social. Palatine v. Monks' Hall. Burtonwood A.F.C. v. Fairfield. Fairfield Res. v. Walkers. Results for March 7th. Rylands' Res. 5, Werrington St. Paul's 0. Burtonwood A.F.C. 2, Oughtrington Park 1. Oughtrington Park Res. 9, Werrington St. Mary's 0. Walkers 3, Warrington St. John's 2. Orford A.F.C. 1, Fairfield Res. 1. WORKS COMPETITION. Preliminary Round. T. Locker and Co., v. Orford Tannery. IL A. Naylor v. Longford Wire. Castle Rubber v. Sankey Wire Mills. Forshaw's v. Whitecross. Bifurated Rivet Co. v. Co-operative Society. Richmond's (Grappenhall) v. Corporation. Mostyn Tube v. Cable Co, Pearson and Knowles (No. 3) v." Guardian " Office. The following have byes :—Stockton Heath Spade. Garnett's. Robertson's, Grappenhall. Rylands Ns.. 2. Rylands No. I. Penketh Tannery. Greenalls'. Walkers. Armitage and Rigby. Firth Co. Crosfields. Rich- mond's (Academy-street). Monks Hell. Monks Hall (Bedsteads). General Post Office. Rylands Wet Shop. Pletcher Russell and Co. Robinson's. Union Tannery. Greening's. L. &amp; NAV. (Goods.) Pearson a Knowles (No. 3.) C.L.C. Whyman'e. WARRINGTON ALBIONS WIN LIVERPOOL JUNIOR CUP. There was a good crowd at Widnes on Saturday when Warrington Albions and Liverpool North-Western Rubber Works met in the final round for the Liverpool Junior Cup. Liverpool ought to have opened the score but Lowery missed a chanoe. Albion were soon one up through a shot by Gregson and had much the better of the play to the interval when the score was I-0. In the second half Albion were soon on top of their opponents and Ball added a second goal after good forward work. A little slackness after this gave Liverpool a chance and the scores were quickly equalised by Lowery and Stewart. Luckily the Warrington men were ecoal to another great effort and Gregson rushed a third goal through before tne close. Final : Albion 3, Liver- pool 2, PRESENTATION OF THE CUP. The cup was banded to Nir. Ernest Ball, captain of the Warrington Albion, by Sir. Silas H. Gill, the vice- president of the Liverpool Football Association. Mr. Gill remarked that the losers would probably agree that the winners well deserved the honours. (Cheers.) Eleven gold-centre medals accompanied the cup, and the losers received handsome silver medals for their success in "running up."</p>
        <p>DARESBURY LICENSING SESSIONS. Wine Licence Refused. The adjourned Licensing Sessions for Daresbury Division were held at Daresbury on Tuesday, Mr. H. Nield presiding. THE RING 0' BELLS, STRETTON. William L. Greaves applied for a renewal of the licence of the Ring O'Bells. This was granted, and the Chairman said applicant must be careful in the future in the conduct of the house and not be summoned again. ALTERATIONS AT THE CAT AND LION. Mr. R. A. White appiied on behalf of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., for sanction to make alters tions at the Cat and Lion, Stretton. He said there would be no increase in the drinking area at all. In the house proper, the only alteration proposed was to have a new lavatory. The plans bad been before the police, who had no objection to them.—The application was granted. WINN LICINCE REFUSED. Herbert Turton, chemist, Old London-road, applied for a licence to sell medicated wines. He now submitted the list of wines he proposed to sell, and said he did not wish to call any evidence to show that the licence I was required. Mr. A. Browne, on behalf the Warrington Licensed Victuallers' Association, again opposed the application on the grounds that the licence was not required and that it would increase the number of I licences in the division. If the new Licensing Bill became law, this could count as a licence and be taken into consideration if the areas in the Bill were carried through. Medicated wines were only wanted to restore I persons to their normal c.mdition of health, and were not therefore required in ten minutes or half an hour. In twenty minutes' time they could get in Warrington. —The licence was refused. A SPECIAL OFFER. To Users of Fluid Beef and Meat Extracts. We invite the readers of the " ‘Varrington Examiner" to test for themselves the advantages of the new pre- paration for making Beef Tea. Gravies, Soups, etc., called Fluid Fluid Ju-Vis is Beef strengthened and vastly improved in flavour by the addition o